3000:
** λατρ-εύω**,
Elean **λατρείω** (which see), **work for hire** or **pay**, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Solon Lyricus 13.48}}: **to be in servitude, serve**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.1.36; παρά τινι 2nd c.AD: Apollodorus Damascenus Mechanicus 2.6.3.}}
**2** λ. τινί **to be subject** or **enslaved to**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 35, etc.}}: with _accusative_ _person_, **serve**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 1115 (Lyric poetry), _falsa lectio_ in [prev. author] “El.” 131: _metaphorically_, λ. πέτρᾳ, of Prometheus, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 968; μόχθοις λατρεύων τοῖς ὑπερτάτοις βροτῶν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 105}}; λ. νόμοις **obey**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Agesilaus” 7.2}}; λ. καιρῷ, = Latin _temporibus inservire_, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD(?): Pseudo-Phocylidea 121}}; τῷ κάλλει λ. **to be devoted to**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 10.57; λ. ἡδονῇ 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Nigr.” 15.}}
**3** **serve** the gods **with prayers and sacrifices**, λ. Φοίβῳ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 152 (Lyric poetry): with _accusative_ _cognate_, πόνον λ. τινί}} **render due** service, {{Refs|[prev. work] 129 (Lyric poetry)}}; πόνον.. τόνδ᾽ ἐλάτρευσα θεᾷ {{Refs|“IG” 2.1378.}}

3001:
** λάχᾰνον** [λᾰ], τό,
mostly in _plural_, **garden-herbs**, opposed to wild plants, **vegetables**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 313, 4th c.BC: Epicrates Comicus 11.15, +others, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 372c, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 1.3.1, etc.; but also λ. ἄγρια 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 456, “Pl.” 298: _sing._ is rarer, οὐδὲ λ. οὐδὲν.. ὁρῶ}} not **a** single **herb**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 191; ὥστε μηδὲ λ. γενέσθαι ἐν τῷ κήπῳ 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 50.61}}; ἐν τῷ λ. τούτῳ, i.e. the lettuce, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Eubulus Comicus 14, compare 54, 4th c.BC: Epicrates Comicus 11.25.}}
**2** in _plural_ also, **the vegetable-market**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 557, 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 46.8, 4th-3rd c.BC: Diphilus Comicus 32.22.}}

3004:
** λέγω**,
**B** **pick up**, etc.: tenses for _signification_ 1 and 11, _future_ λέξω {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.224}}: _aorist_ ἔλεξα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 292}}:—_middle_, _future_ in _passive_ sense λέξομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 322}}: _aorist_ ἐλεξάμην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.27 (_transitive_)}}; _Epic dialect_ ἐλέγμην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.335; λέκτο 4.451}}:—_passive_, _aorist_ ἐλέχθην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.188: also post- 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus in these senses, but only in _composition_, especially with ἀπο-, ἐκ-, κατα-, συν}}-; post-{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}_perfect_ εἴλοχα (κατ-, συν-), _passive_ εἴλεγμαι, in these senses rarely λέλεγμαι (see. the compounds); also _future_ λεγήσομαι (συλ-): _aorist 2_ ἐλέγην (κατ-, συν-):—**gather, pick up**, ὀστέα.. λέγωμεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.239, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24. 72, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 8.53}}; αἱμασιάς τε λέγων **picking out stones for building** walls, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.359 (ubi see Scholia, compare λογάς 2), compare 24.224}}:—_middle_, **gather for oneself**, ἐπὶ δὲ ξύλα πολλὰ λέγεσθε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.507; ὀστέα λευκὰ λέγοντο 24.793; φάρμακα λέξασθαι 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 3.807.}}
**B.2** _middle_, **choose for oneself, pick out**, λέξαιτο.. ἄνδρας ἀρίστους {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.108; κούρους 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.27}}:—_passive_, **to be chosen**, εἰ.. λεγοίμεθα πάντες ἄριστοι {{Refs|13.276.}}
**B.II** **count, tell**, ἐν δ᾽ ἡμέας λέγε κήτεσιν he **counted** us among the seals, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.452}}; and in _aorist_ _middle_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.125}}; ἐγὼ πέμπτος μετὰ τοῖσιν ἐλέγμην I **reckoned myself**.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.335}}; λέκτο δ᾽ ἀριθμόν he **told him over** the number, {{Refs|4.451}}:—_passive_, μετὰ τοῖσιν ἐλέχθην I **was counted** among these, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.188.}}
**B.II.b** so, but not frequently, after {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, λ. ποντιᾶν ψάφων ἀριθμόν 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 13.46, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 570; καθ᾽ ἓν ἕκαστον λ. 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 2.45}}; also καὶ σὲ δ᾽ ἐν τούτοις λέγω **count** you among.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 973}}; λ. τινὰ οὐδαμοῦ **count** him as naught, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 183}}; κέρδος λ., εἰ.. **count** it gain, that.., {{Refs|[prev. work] 462}}:—_middle_, λέξατο πάντας [ναύτας] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.189}}:—_passive_, λέγεσθαι ἐν τοῖς ἱππικωτάτοις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 11.20; ἐνὶ πρώτῃσι λέγεσθαι 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Delum” 16}}: _future_ _middle_ in _passive_ sense, ἐν τοῖς οὐκέτ᾽ οὖσι λέξομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus [prev. cited]}}
**B.II.2** **recount, tell over**, οὔ τι διαπρήξαιμι λέγων ἐμὰ κήδεα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.197; σὺ δέ μοι λέγε θέσκελα ἔργα 11.374; τὰ ἕκαστα λέγων 12.165; ὅσα τ᾽ αὐτὸς.. ἐμόγησε, πάντ᾽ ἔλεγ᾽ 23.308: so in Trag., λ. τύχας, πάθη, μόχθους, etc., 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 633, “Pers.” 292, “Ag.” 555, etc.}}; also Ἀγαμέμνονι.. λέγ᾽ ὀνείδεα **repeated** reproaches against him, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.222; so perhaps ψεύδεα πολλὰ λ. 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 27 (but see below 111):}} —_middle_, τί σε χρὴ ταῦτα λέγεσθα; why need'st thou **tell the tale** thereof? {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.275}}; and so, μηκέτι ταῦτα λεγώμεθα νηπύτιοι ὥς{{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. work] 292, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.240, 13.296; μηκέτι νῦν δήθ᾽ αὖθι λεγώμεθα 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.435.}}
**B.III** **say, speak**, first in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 27 (see. above 11.2)}}: _future_ λέξω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 38.1, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 859, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.14, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.48, 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 6.33, etc.}}: _aorist_ ἔλεξα {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Anacreon Lyricus 45, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 217e, 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 1.15 (rare in 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus}} and the Orators, common in some dialects, as Boeotian, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“IG” 7.504.2 (from Tanagra), Thessalian, [prev. work]9(2).461.21, Ionic, variant in 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Aër.” 12):}}_perfect_ λέλεχα {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 16.249, λέλεγα and λέλογας 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus (εἴρηκα in correct writers)}}:—_passive_, _future_ λεχθήσομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.86, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 67c, etc.}}: also _future_ _middle_ in _passive_ sense, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1186, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 906 (Lyric poetry), etc.; and λελέξομαι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.53 (variant{λέξεται}), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 457b:}}]_aorist_ ἐλέχθην (never ἐλέγην in this sense) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1442, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.32, etc.}}: _perfect_ λέλεγμαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 8.20, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.21, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 389, etc. (εἴλεγμαι}} in this sense only in _compound_ δι-): rare in compounds (only ἀντιλέγω, ἐπιλέγω, καταλέγω, προλέγω), the _present_ in most compounds being supplied by ἀγορεύω, the _future_ by ἐρῶ, the _aorist_ by εἶπον, the _perfect_ by εἴρηκα:
**B.III.1** **say, speak**, never in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, first in 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus [same place], frequently from 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus}} and Trag. downwards; of all kinds of **oral** communications, ἐκέλευε λέγειν εἴ τι θέλοι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.58}}; so λέγοις ἄν **speak, say on**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 268e, etc.; λ. μῦθον 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 698 (troch.); ψευδῆ λ. [prev. author] “Ag.” 625; ἀληθῆ λ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 12b}} (so in _passive_, λόγος λέλεκται πᾶς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 389)}}; of oracles, **say, declare**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.136}}; ὥσπερ τοὔνομα λέγει **indicates**, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 312c: with Preps., λ. ἀμφί τινος 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 1017, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 580; περί τινος 6th c.BC: Xenophanes Poeta Philosophus 34.2, 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 165, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 151 (_anapaest meter_), 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.48}}; ὑπέρ τινος in his defence, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 555, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.7.16}}; κατά τινος against him, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 1240a, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.5.2}}; λ. ἐπί τισι εὐχὰς ἀγαθάς **express** good wishes for them, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 625 (_anapaest meter_)}}; λ. τά τινος take his part, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 8.64}}; λ. πρός τι in reference or in answer to.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 753, etc.; εἴς τι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.5.1.}}
**B.III.2** with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, **say** that.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 2.59, etc.: with _negative_ οὐ, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 348c, etc., but μή [prev. work] 346e, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 4.5 (_passive_)}}, and usually in later Gr., {{LXX+NT+5th c.BC+|LXX.Gen.38.22; λ. μὴ εἶναι ἀνάστασιν NT.Matt.22.23: frequently also followed by ὡς, ὅτι (generally so in the _active_ voice) when the subject of the relative clause may become the object of the principal Verb, γυναῖκα λέγουσιν, ὡς κάθηται.. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.3.5, etc.: rarely with}}_participle_, λ. Οἰδίπουν ὀλωλότα **speak of** him as dead, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1580; λέγουσιν ἡμᾶς ὡς ὀλωλότας 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 672; λέξασ᾽ ἀδελφῷ σ᾽ ἐνθάδ᾽ ὄντα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 888}}:—_passive_, λέξεται ἔχων {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “IT” 1047, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 170 (conjecture).}}
**B.III.3** λέγειν τινά τι **say** something **of** another, especially κακὰ λ. τινά **speak** ill **of** him, **revile** him, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.61; ἀγαθὰ λ. τινάς 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 435}}; τὰ ἔσχατα, τὰ ἀπόρρητα λ. ἀλλήλους, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.2.9, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.123}}; also εὖ or κακῶς λ. τινά, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 445 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 524, compare 1028; εὖ λ. τὸν εὖ λέγοντα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.3.8.}}
**B.III.4** **call by name**, ἃς τρέμομεν λ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 128 (Lyric poetry)}}: with _double_ _accusative_, **call** so and so, λέγοιμ᾽ ἂν ἄνδρα τόνδε τῶν σταθμῶν κύνα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 896, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 939 codices, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.32, etc.}}
**B.III.5** λ. τινὰ ποιεῖν τι **tell, command** one to do, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 553, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 101, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.1.22, etc.}}: so with τινι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 840, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.150 (no object expressed in 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 925, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 856)}}; λέγε τὸν ἐρωτῶντα ἵνα.. εἴπῃ σοι.. {{NT+4th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Astrampsychus Onirocriticus “Oracula” p.1 H.; ὡς ὁ νόμος λέγει 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 22.20; ὁ λέγων μὴ μοιχεύειν NT.Rom.2.22.}}
**B.III.6** λ. τι **say something**, i.e. **speak to the point** or **purpose**, βούλῃ λέγειν τι, καὶ λέγων μηδὲν κλύει; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 757}}; λέγω τ; **am I right**? the answer being λέγεις, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “OT” 1475; κινδυνεύεις τι λέγειν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 404a; ἴσως ἄν τι λέγοις 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.1.12, compare “Cyr.” 1.4.20}}; opposed to οὐδὲν λέγει **has** no **meaning**, no **authority**, οὐδὲν λ. τὸ σωφρόνως τραφῆναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 334, compare “V.” 75}}; οὐδὲν λέγεις **nonsense**! {{Refs|[prev. author] “Th.” 625}}; but οὐδὲν λέγειν, also, **say what is not, lie**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Av.” 66, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 30b}}; also εὖ γε λέγεις, εὖ λέγεις, εὖ ἂν λέγοις, **good news!, that is well**!,{{Refs|[prev. work] 24e, “Grg.” 447b, “Prt.” 310b}}; καλῶς, ὀρθῶς λ., **you are right**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.3.4, 3.6.8}}; κοὔπω λέγω **and what is more**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 7.44}}; τί λέγει; τὸν ἔποπα παῖ καλεῖ; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 57, compare “Ec.” 298 (Lyric poetry).}}
**B.III.7** _pleonastic_, ἔφη λέγων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.156, 5.36; ἔλεγε φάς [prev. author] 1.122; ἔφασκε λέγων 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 472; ἦ δ᾽ ὃς λέγων [prev. author] “V.” 795; ὡς ἔφη λέγων 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 757; καὶ λέγων εἶπεν οὕτω πως 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 8.74, etc.}}
**B.III.8** at the beginning of letters or documents, Ἄμασις Πολυκράτεϊ ὧδε λέγει.., Μαρδόνιος τάδε λέγει.., etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.40, 8.140}}. ά, etc.; τὰ γράμματα ἔλεγε τάδε {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.124, etc.}}; γράμμασι λέγον τάδε, of an inscription, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.54}}: in roman edicts, Μάρκος Μέττιος Ῥοῦφος.. λέγει {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“POxy.” 237 viii 28 (1st c.AD).}}
**B.III.9** **wish to say, mean**, οὔτοι γυναῖκας ἀλλὰ Γοργόνας λέγω {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 48}}; τί τοῦτο λέγει, πρὸ Πύλοι; what **does** πρὸ Πύλοιο **mean?** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1059, compare 1021, 1375, “Ec.” 989, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 60e: frequently in Platonic dialogue, πῶς λέγεις}}; how **do you mean**? in what **sense do you say this? Ap.**{{Refs|24e, +others}}; ἢ πῶς λέγομε; or what **do we mean to say? Grg.**{{Refs|480b}}; πῶς δὴ οὖν αὐτὸ λέγει; {{Refs|“Phdr.” 265c}}; ποῖόν τί ποτε ἄρα λέγοντές φασι.. what they can possibly **mean** by saying.., {{Refs|“Tht.” 181c, +others: with double _accusative_, τοιοῦτόν τι σὲ λέγειν τὸ κρεῖττον “Grg.” 489d, +others: frequently (especially in Trag.) to explain more fully, εἴσω κομίζου καὶ σύ, Κασάνδραν λέγω}} you, **I mean** Cassandra, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1035; ὁ μάντις, υἱὸν Οἰκλέους λ. [prev. author] “Th.” 609, compare 658 (variant), “Pr.” 946; ποταμός, Ἀχελῷον λέγω 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 9, compare 1220, “Ph.” 1261, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 987}}; ἐμὲ λέγων **meaning** me, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.215; τὸ δ᾽ ὑμεῖς ὅταν λέγω, τὴν πόλιν λ. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.88: sometimes, however, the word after λέγω}} is put in _apposition_ with the word to be explained, Ἀντικλείας.., τῆς σῆς λέγω τοι μητρός {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 175, compare “Th.” 658 codex M; περὶ τῶνδε.., λέγω δὲ Φωκέων 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.152; παρ᾽ ὧν.., τούτων τῶν τὴν Ἀσίαν οἰκούντων λέγω [prev. author] 8.24, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 202b: _absolutely_, μηδενὸς ὄντος ἐν [τῇ χώρᾳ] λέγω 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 1.27.}}
**B.III.9.b** περὶ ἃς (i.e. ἀπολαύσεις) λέγομεν τὸν σώφρονα in regard to which we **use the term** 'temperate', {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1148a5, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 494b.}}
**B.III.10** ὡς λέγουσι as **they say**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 23, etc.; ὡς λ. μοι [prev. author] “OC” 1161:}} —_passive_, λέγεται **it is said**, c._accusative_ et _infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.2.30, +others}}; but also πατρὸς λέγεται γενέσθαι.. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Cyr.” 1.2.1}}; θανεῖν ἐλέχθη he **was said** to have been killed, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 292; so λεγόμενον ἐρέω 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 5.108: τὸ λεγόμενον}} _absolutely_, **as the saying goes**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.68, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 447a, “Smp.” 217e, etc.; τὸ λ. δὴ τοῦτο [prev. author] “Grg.” 514e: ὁ λεγόμενος γραῶν ὕθλος}} the **so-called**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Tht.” 176b; οἱ λ. αὐτόνομοι εἶναι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.3.8}}; οἱ λ. ὅτι.. **of whom it is said** that.., {{Refs|[prev. author] “Cyr.” 8.6.16.}}
**B.III.11** of orators, **speak** (emphatically), λέγειν δεινός {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 545, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.5.9, etc.; λέγειν ἠσκηκότες 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 963, compare 5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 95 (see. λαλέω); λ. τε καὶ πράσσειν δυνατώτατος 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.139; οἱ ἐν τῷ πλήθει λέγειν δυνάμενοι 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 3.8, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.286}}; **plead** one's cause in a court of law, {{Refs|[prev. author] 23.78}}; δίκας λέγειν ὑπέρ τινος **speak** as an advocate for.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 1.111.}}
**B.III.12** **boast of, tell of**, τὴν ἑαυτοῦ ῥώμην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.3.10}}; in Poets, **sing of**, θέλω λ. Ἀτρείδας {{Refs|“Anacreontea” 23.1.}}
**B.III.13** **recite** what is written, λαβὲ τὸ βιβλίον καὶ λέγε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 143c; and frequently in Oratt., as λέγε τὸν νόμον 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.8}} and 10, etc.; of lectures, ἀκούσατέ μου σχόλια λέγοντος {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 3.21.6, compare 15.8}} (the sense of Latin _lego_, **read**, occurs only in the compounds ἀναλέγομαι, ἐπιλέγομαι).
**B.III.14** **say** or **send word** by another, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.9.25, 7.4.5.}}
**B.III.15** **maintain** as a thesis, οἱ τὰς ἰδέας λέγοντες {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physica” 193b36, “Metaphorical” 1036b14.}}
**B.III.16** **nominate**, Latin _dicere [dictatorem_], {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus “fragment” 36.26 (_passive_)}}. (Cf. Latin _lègo, legio, legulus_ ('olivegatherer').)

3005:
** λεῖμμα**, ατος, τό,
(λείπω) **remnant, residue**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Herc.” 1251.6 (_plural_), 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Nicias” 17, τοῦ παιδὸς τὰ λείμματα}} **what was left** of him, his **remains**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.119}}; so, of persons, {{LXX+NT|LXX.2Ki.19.4, NT.Rom.11.5.}}
**2** in Music, **interval** of {{Refs|256 243}}**left** over when two τόνοι of {{Refs 4th c.BC+|9 8 are measured off from the διὰ τεσσάρων (4 3), 2nd c.AD: Ptolemaeus Mathematicus “Harmonica - Opera Mathematica” 1.10, Gaudentius Musicus 13, 15, Adrast. cited in Theon.Sm. p.68 H., +others, 5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “in Platonis Timaeum commentarii” 2.168, 179 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator}}; misunderstood as the number 13 (256—243) by {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.1017f, cf. Anonymus cited in Theon.Sm.p.69 H.}}
**2.b** in Rhythmic, the shortest pause, λ. ἐν ῥυθμῷ χρόνος κενὸς ἐλάχιστος {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD(?): Aristides Quintilianus Musicus 1.18.}}
**3** in Medicine, **intermission** in fever, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|7th c.AD: Stephanus Medicus “in Gal.” 1.268 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator (_sing._and _plural_).}}
**4** **deficiency**, μὴ γενέσθαι μήτε δανεισμὸν μήτε λ. περὶ ταύτας τᾶς εἰσφορᾶς {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“IG” 5(1).1432.9 (Messene, 1st c.BC-1st c.AD).}}

3006:
** λεῖος**, α, ον,
**smooth** to the touch, [αἴγειρος] {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.484; λ. ὥσπερ ἔγχελυς 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 218, compare 5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 338}}; χῆμαι, χηραμβίς, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PCair.Zen.” 82.12 (3rd c.BC)}}, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus see at {χήμ; τὰ τραχέα καὶ τὰ λ}. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.10.1; frequently in 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus, “Cra.” 414b, +others, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Categoriae” 10a17, etc.}}]; also, of cloths, **smooth, plain, not embroidered**, ὑφαντά τε καὶ λ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.97; λ. ὕφασμα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 310e}}; λεῖα ἐκπεποιημένα worked **smooth**, of marble, {{Refs|“IG” 12.372.134; also λεία ἐργασία [prev. work]372.165}} **unsculptured**, Ἀθήνης ἕδος {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Fragmenta” 105.4}}; of plate, **unembossed**, φιάλαι {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“IG” 11(2).161 B 27 (Delos, 3rd c.BC), “Inscription Délos” 442 B 78 (2nd c.BC).}}
**2** in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}, chiefly of **level** places or countries, λεῖος δ᾽ ἱππόδρομος ἀμφίς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.330}}; ἐν λείῳ πεδίῳ{{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. work] 359; λ. ὁδός 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.103, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 288 (cited in 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus, etc., ὀλίγη codices)}}; λ. ἄροσις {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.134}}; λεῖα δ᾽ ἐποίησεν made a **smooth place**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.30; πεδίον λ. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.29; χωρίον λειότατον [prev. author] 7.9. ; ἡ -οτάτη τῶν ὁδῶν [prev. author] 9.69}}; λ. θάλασσα a **smooth** sea, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 2.117; λ. χώρα καὶ ἄξυλος 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Atheniensium” 2.12}}; λ. βάσεις **flat** feet, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 6.856.}}
**2.b** with _genitive_, χῶρος.. λεῖος πετράων **smooth** (i.e. **free) from** rocks, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.443, 7.282.}}
**3** **smoothskinned, without hair**, of animals, {{LXX+4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 582b35, LXX.Gen.27.11; - ότατον τῶν ζῴων ἐστὶν ἄνθρωπος 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 583a6}}; especially of youths, **smooth-chinned, beardless** (compare λείαξ), {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 5.90, compare “Anthologia Graeca” 12.13 (2nd c.AD: Strato Epigrammaticus)}}; also, of fish, **smooth**, ἱππίδια {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 44}}; opposed to λεπιδωτοί, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 505a26}}; [γαλεός] the **smooth** shark, **Mustelus laevis**,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 565b2, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Oppianus Anazarbensis Epicus “Halieutica - Poetae Bucolici et Didactici” 1.380; τὸ λ. 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 3.14, 6.3.16}}; λείη ὑπόστασις a **smooth** or **uniform** sediment, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. author] “Coac.” 462; [γάλα] λ. καὶ ὁμαλὸν καὶ συνεχὲς ἑαυτῷ 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.91.}}
**4** _metaphorically_, **smooth, soft**, πνεῦμα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1003}}; of the sound of the voice, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 307a, “Ti.” 67b, “Phlb.” 51d; διάλεκτος 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Po.Herc.” 994.36}}; of the taste, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-4th c.BC: Timaeus Locrus Philosophus 100ef; also λ. μῦθοι 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 647}}; [τὸ] ἥμερόν τε καὶ λ. [τοῦ ἤθους] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 406a}}; λ. πάθημα, opposed to τραχύ, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ti.” 63e; λ. κινήματα τῆς σαρκός 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Fragmenta” 411}}; λ. κίνησις, Cyrenaic phrase for ἡδονή, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 2.86, compare 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Par.” 10, 3rd c.AD: Alexander Aphrodisiensis Philosophus “in Aristotelis Topicorum libros octo commentaria - pars” 94.32; λ. ἡσυχίη “Anthologia Graeca” 7.278 (1st c.BC: Archias Byzantine)}}; ὡς -οτέρου ἐλέους ὑπάρξοντος (sed to be read τελειοτέρου) {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 20.9.11}}; τὸ λ., ={λειότης, τῆς ἑρμηνείας} {{Refs 4th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Lysia” 24; τὸ λ. καὶ ὁμαλὲς τῆς συνθέσεως 4th c.BC: Demetrius Phalereus Rhetor “Demetrius on Style” 48.}} _adverb_ λείως **smoothly, gently**, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 144b, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.384a; καί με κωτίλλοντα λ. τραχὺν ἐκφανεῖν νόον 6th c.BC: Solon Lyricus cited in 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 12.3.}}
**II** **rubbed** or **ground down**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 1.3, +others, “PHolm.” 19.39; compare λειόω 11: λεῖον, τό}}, **fine sand,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|Inscription Délos 500 A 9 (3rd c.BC)}}. (Prob. λειϝος, cf. Latin _lēvis._)

3007:
** λείπω**,
_imperfect_ ἔλειπον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.288, etc.}}: _future_ λείψω {{Refs|18.11:}}_aorist 1_ ἔλειψα, _participle_ λείψας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 965 (= 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 32), elsewhere only late, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 12.15.12 (παρ-), 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 6.3.10 (παρ-), 1st c.AD(?): Pseudo-Phocylidea 77 (ἀπ-), etc.}}; uncompounded, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Ptolemaeus Epigrammaticus “Alm.” 10.4, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Par.” 42, 3rd c.AD: Pseudo-Callisthenes Historicus 1.44 (codex C)}}; also in later Poets, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|4th c.AD(?): Manetho Astrologus 1.153, 3rd c.AD: Oppianus Apamensis Epicus “Cynegetica” 2.33, and in Inscrr., “epigram Gr.” 522.16 (from Thessalonica), 314.27 (from Smyrna)}}, etc.: but correct writers normally use _aorist 2_ ἔλῐπον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.35, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 984 (Lyric poetry)}}, etc.: _perfect_ λέλοιπα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.134:}}_pluperfect_ ἐλελοίπειν (_Attic dialect_ -η) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.1.21}}:—_middle_, in properly sense chiefly in compounds: _aorist 2_ ἐλιπόμην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.186, 2.40, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 169, etc. (in _passive_ sense, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.693, +others)}}:—_passive_, _future_ _middle_ in _passive_ sense λείψομαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 200, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.8.ά, 48}}; also λειφθήσομαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1071, λελείψομαι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.742, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.105, etc.}}: _aorist_ ἐλείφθην, λείφθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 2.43}}; _Epic dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ἔλειφθεν {{Refs|“hymnus ad Mercurium” 195}}: _perfect_ λέλειμμαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.256, 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 228, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 61a, etc.}}: _pluperfect_ ἐλελείμμην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.700}}; _Epic dialect_ λέλειπτο {{Refs|10.256}}: _Epic dialect_ _aorist_ also ἔλειπτο {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 1.45, etc.:}}
**1** **leave, quit**, Ἑλλάδα, δώματα, etc., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.447, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.116, etc.: with a _negative_, [σκόπελον] οὔ ποτε κύματα λείπει 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.396; νιν.. χιὼν οὐδαμὰ λ. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 830 (Lyric poetry)}}; χερσὶν ὕπο Τρώων λείψειν φάος ἠελίοιο, i.e. die, be killed, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.11; λ. τὸν βίον ὑπό τινος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 872e}}; λ. βίον, βίοτον, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1444, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 226 (Lyric poetry), etc.; αὐτόχειρι σφαγῇ λ. βίον [prev. author] “Or.” 948.}}
**1.b** conversely, τὸν δ᾽ ἔλιπε ψυχή {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.696, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.426; τὸν.. λίπε θυμός 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.470; ἔπειτά με καὶ λίποι αἰών 5.685, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.224; λίπε δ᾽ ὀστέα θυμός 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.743}}; ψυχὴ δὲ λέλοιπεν (i.e. ὀστέα) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.134; νῦν δ᾽ ἤδη πάντα λέλοιπεν (i.e. ἐμέ) [prev. work] 213}}; in these two last passages some take it _intransitive_, **is gone**, see below {{Refs|11.}}
**2** **leave behind, leave at home**, παιδὶ τὸν ἐν μεγάροισιν ἔλειπες {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.403, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.480}}; especially of dying men, **leave** (as a legacy), Ἀτρεὺς δὲ θνῄσκων ἔλιπεν πολύαρνι Θυέστῃ [τὸσκῆπτρον{{Refs 5th c.BC+|] 2.106; πατέρι γόον καὶ κήδεα λυγρά 5.157, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 973; παῖδα ὀρφανὸν λ. [prev. work] 653}}; λ. ἄρρενας, θυγατέρας, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 923e, 924e; λ. εὔκλειαν ἐν δόμοισι 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 348 (Lyric poetry)}}:—also in _middle_, **leave behind one** (as a memorial to posterity), μνημόσυνον λιπέσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.186, 6.109, +others; λιπέσθαι τιμωρούς 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 169; διαδόχους ἐμαυτῷ 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Aemilius Paulus” 36, etc.}}
**2.b** **leave standing, leave remaining, spare**, οἰκίαν οὐδεμίαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.4.1; μηδένα [prev. author] “HG” 2.3.41, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 567b, etc.}}
**3** **leave, forsake**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.13, etc.; λ. τινὰ χαμαί 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 6.45; εὕδοντα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 273}}; τὴν αὑτοῦ φύσιν λ.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 903; λ. τὴν τάξιν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 29a, etc.}}; λ. ἐράνους **fail in paying**.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 27.25, compare 25.22}}; λ. δασμόν, φοράν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.1.1, 34}}; λ. μαρτυρίαν, ὅρκον, **fail in**.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 49.19, 59.60, λ. δίκην}} **allow** it **to go by default**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“SIG” 134b24 (Milet., 4th c.BC)}}; λοιβὰς.. οὐ λίπε **neglected** them not, {{Refs|“IG” 3.1337.8.}}
**3.b** conversely, λίπον ἰοὶ ἄνακτα they **failed** him, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.119.}}
**4** in Mathematics texts, **lose** or **drop** something, i.e. have something subtracted from it, τὸ KP λιπὸν τὸ BO the area KP **minus** the area BO, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd-2nd c.BC: Apollonius Pergaeus Geometra 3.12, compare 1st c.BC: Ptolemaeus Epigrammaticus “Alm.” 10.4, +others, 3rd c.AD: Diophantus Mathematicus 2.21.}}
**II** _intransitive_, **to be gone, depart,** {{Refs|epigram Gr. 149.2 (from Rhenea)}}; see above 1.1b.
**II.2** **to be wanting** or **missing**, οὔ τί πω ἔλιπεν ἐκ τοῦδ᾽ οἴκου.. αἰκία {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 514 (Lyric poetry); οὔποτ᾽ ἔρις λείψει κατὰ πόλεις 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 1157 (Lyric poetry); τὸ κακοτυχὲς οὐ λέλοιπεν ἐκ τέκνων [prev. author] “HF” 133 (Lyric poetry)}}; λείπουσιν αἱ ἐκ τῆς κεφαλῆς [τρίχες] {{NT+4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 518a24; ἔτι ἕν σοι λείπει NT.Luke.18.22}}; τί λείπει τῶν ἐπιτηδείων αὐταῖ; {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 10.18.8}}; τί γάρ σοι λείπε; {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 2.22.5, compare 2nd c.AD: Diogenes Oenoandensis Epicureus 64}}; [εἰς τὴν προκειμένην πραγματείαν] τὸ ὑφ᾽ οὗ γίνεται.. μὴ ῥηθὲν οὐ λείπει **is** not **needed**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Marcellinus Medicus “de Pulsibus - Festschrift zur 49. Versammlung deutscher Philologen und Schulmänner” 69: with}}_infinitive_, λείπει μὲν οὐδ᾽ ἃ πρόσθεν ἤδεμεν τὸ μὴ οὐ βαρύστον᾽ εἶναι **nihil absunt quin**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1232: so with _genitive_, βραχὺ λείπει τοῦ μὴ συνάπτειν 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.14.6, etc.}}; πρότασις τῆς προειρημένης λείπουσα ὑποθέσει a proposition **containing less** in the hypothesis than that aforesaid, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd c.AD(?): Pappus Mathematicus 648.1: frequently with numerals, κεφάλαιον γίγνεται μικροῦ λείποντος πέντε καὶ δέκα τάλαντα 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 19.43; οὐ πολὺ λεῖπον τῶν ἐνενήκοντα ἐτῶν 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 12.16.13; τριήρεις πέντε λείπουσαι τῶν ἑκατὸν εἴκοσι 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 13.14: generally, παντὸς μὲν οὖν λείπει 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 728a; ὁ λιπών [prev. work] 759e}}; τὸ λεῖπον **what is lacking**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.38.9, etc.}}; esp. Grammars, **to be incomplete**, of a phrase, λειπούσης τῆς φράσεως {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Adverbiis;” 159.28, +others}}; **to be wanting, omitted**, λείποντος τοῦ καί {{Refs|[prev. author] “Conj.” 225.24: also with _dative_, λείπει ἡ κεῖνος φωνὴ τῷ ε ¯ [prev. author] “Adv.” 147.17.}}
**II.2.b** of the moon, **to be invisible** (compare λειψιφαής), {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 2.3.5.}}
**II.2.c** λείποντα εἴδη, in Algebra, ={λείψεις}, **negative** terms, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Diophantus Mathematicus 1 “Def.” 10.}}
**B** _passive_, **to be left, left behind**, ἄλοχος Φυλάκῃ ἐλέλειπτο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.700; οἱ δ᾽ οἶοι λείπονται 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.250, etc.}}; also ὀπίσσω, μετόπισθε, κατόπισθε λ., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.160, 22.334, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.116}}; παῖδες.. μετόπισθε λελειμμένοι **left behind** in Troy, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.687; μόνα.. νὼ λελειμμένα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 58, etc.}}; τὸ λειπόμενον βίου (variant{βιοτᾶς}) {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Ariphron Lyricus 1 (= “IG” 3.171).}}
**B.b** in Mathematics texts, **to be subtracted**: τὸ ἀπὸ τῆς ΔΦ λειφθὲν ὑπὸ τοῦ ἀπὸ τῆς ΔΑ ποιεῖ.., the square on ΔΦ **subtracted** from the square on ΔΑ gives.., {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Ptolemaeus Epigrammaticus “Alm.” 10.7.}}
**B.2** **remain, remain over and above**, τριτάτη δ᾽ ἔτι μοῖρα λέλειπται {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.253; ἐμοὶ δὲ λελείψεται ἄλγεα λυγρά 24.742; ὀλίγων σφι ἡμερέων λείπεται σιτία 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.45; ὃ πᾶσι λ. βροτοῖς.. ἐλπίς 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 681; αὐτόνομοι ἐλείφθημεν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.11; ἕως ἄν τι λείπηται [prev. author] 8.81: _impersonal_, λείπεται}} **it remains**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 157e}}: c._accusative_ et _infinitive_, πεπληρῶσθαί με {{Refs|[prev. author] “Phdr.” 235c.}}
**B.3** **remain alive**, πολλοὶ δὲ λίποντο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.495, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 480, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.1.2.}}
**B.II** with _genitive_,
**B.II.1** **to be left without, to be forsaken of**, κτεάνων λειφθεὶς καὶ φίλων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 2.11; σοῦ λελειμμένη 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 548}}; but στρατὸν λελειμμένον δορός **which has been left** by the spear, i.e. not slain, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 517.}}
**B.II.2** **to be left behind in a race**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.407, 409, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.125}}; λελειμμένος οἰῶν **lingering behind** the sheep, {{Refs|9.448}}; λείπετ᾽.. Μενελάου δουρὸς ἐρωήν he **was left** a spear's throw **behind** Menelaus, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.529}}; ἐς δίσκουρα λέλειπτο he **had been left behind** as much as a quoit's throw,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 523; κίρκοι πελειῶν οὐ μακρὰν λελειμμένοι 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 857, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 1244}}; τοῦ κήρυκος μὴ λείπεσθαι not **to lag behind** the herald, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.131}}; but ἀπό τινος **to be left behind by** one, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.437, 445}}; λ. βασιλέος or ἀπὸ βασιλέος **by** the king, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.113, 9.66}}; λείπεσθαι τοῦ καιροῦ **to be behind** time, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.3.29; τῆς ναυμαχίης [prev. author] 7.168; τῆς ἐξόδου [prev. author] 9.19}}; but, λείπου μηδὲ σύ, παρθέν᾽, ἀπ᾽ οἴκων **fail** not [to come] from the house, i.e. follow us, uncertain in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1275 (_anapaest meter_)}}: _absolutely_, **to be left behind, be absent**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.229, 8.44.}}
**B.II.3** **come short of, be inferior to**, τινος, like{ἐλαττοῦσθαι, ἡττᾶσθαι, ὑστερεῖσθαί τινος}, because the Verb has a _comparative_ sense, {{Refs|[prev. author] 7.48, etc.}}; οὐκ ἔσθ᾽ ὁποίας λείπεται τόδ᾽ ἡδονῆς **falls short of**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 138.3}}; λείπεσθαί τινος ἔς τι or ἔν τινι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.99, 7.8. ά (see. below 4); περί τι 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 6.52.8; πρός τι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 266; καμήλους ταχυτῆτι οὐ λειπομένας ἵππων 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.86; ξύνεσιν οὐδενὸς λ. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.72; πλήθει λ. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.4.24; πλήθει ἡμῶν λειφθέντες [prev. author] “An.” 7.7.31}}; οὐδ᾽ ἔτι θηρὸς ἐλείπετο δερκομένοισιν, i.e. resembled.., Epic.in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Arch.Papyrus” 7p.4: also with _genitive_ _of things_, λειφθῆναι μάχης 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 732; οὐδὲν σοῦ ξίφους λελείψομαι [prev. author] “Or.” 1041: in Mathematics texts, τὸ ἐγγραφὲν τοῦ περιγραφέντος ἐλάσσονι λείπεται}} the inscribed figure **falls short of** the circumscribed by less than.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Archimedes Geometra “περὶ κωνοειδέων καὶ σφαιροειδέων” 21: also with _dative_ _of things_, λειφθῆναι μάχῃ 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 344}}: with _participle_, οὐδὲν ἐμοῦ λείπει γιγνώσκων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 18.5}}; λέλειψαι τῶν ἐμῶν βουλευμάτων **you come short of, understand not** my plans, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1085; λέλειμμαι τῶν ἐν Ἕλλησιν νόμων [prev. author] “Hel.” 1246}}: _absolutely_, **to be defeated**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.62.6; ὑπό τινος “Anthologia Graeca” 11.224 (Antip.)}}; λείπεσθαι ἐν [τῇ ἀγορανομίᾳ], Latin _repulsam ferre_, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Marius” 5, etc.: _absolutely_, in}}_participle_, ἄνδρας λελειμμένους **inferior** men, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 37; also, λειπόμενοι}}**the poor,** {{Refs|Inscriptiones Graecae 14.1839.7.}}
**B.II.4** **to be wanting** or **lacking in** a thing, **fail of** or **in**, with _genitive_, ὀδυρμάτων ἐλείπετ᾽ οὐδέν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 937; γνώμας λειπομένα σοφᾶς [prev. author] “El.” 474 (Lyric poetry); λελειμμένη τέκνων 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 680}}; λελ. λόγου **failing to heed** my word, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 543}}; μῆνας ἓξ.. λειπόμενος (i.e. τῶν εἴκοσι ἐτῶν) {{Refs|“epigram Gr.” 519 (from Thessalonica)}}; also, λ. ἐν τῷ μὴ δύνασθαι μηδ᾽ ὁρᾶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 495; see above 3.}}
**B.II.5** **to be in need of**, τῆς σῆς βοηθείας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 289.28. (I.- 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus}} **leiq[uglide]**-, cf. Latin _li-n-quo_, Sanskrit _ric_-, _present_ _3rd.pers._ _singular_ **ri-ṇa-k-ti** 'leaves', etc.)

3008:
** λειτουργ-έω**,
earlier _Attic dialect_ **λητουργέω** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“IG” 22.1147.6 (4th c.BC)}}, etc. (λειτ- 22.665.11 (3rd c.BC)): _perfect_ λελειτούργηκα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 18.7, 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 6.60, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 15.145:}}
**I** at Athens, **serve public offices at one's own cost**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.132, +others, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 27.64: with _accusative_ _cognate_, λ. τὰ προσταττόμενα 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 6.61; δύο λειτουργίας 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 50.9, compare 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 3.47}}; λ. ὑπέρ τινος **serve** these **offices** for another, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 3.80, 6.64}}; τὰ λελειτουργημένα **public services performed**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.169.}}
**II** generally, **perform public duties, serve the state**, τῇ πόλει {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.7.6; ἐκ τῆς ἰδίας οὐσίας ὑμῖν λ. 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 8.13; τὸ ταῖς οὐσίαις λειτουργοῦν, ὃ καλοῦμεν εὐπόρους 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1291a34; τοῖς σώμασιν καὶ τοῖς χρήμασιν λ. [prev. author] “Ath.” 29.5; λ. τοῖς σώμασιν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.165; τὸ περὶ τὰς ἀρχὰς λ. 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1291a35}}; λ. τῇ πόλει ταύτην τὴν λειτουργίαν{{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 37, compare 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 6.33.6; λ. πρὸς τεκνοποιΐαν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1335b28; ἄρχειν καὶ λ. “POxy.” 1119.16 (3rd c.AD).}}
**III** generally, **serve** a master, with _dative_, οἱ ἑνὶ λειτουργοῦντες τὰ τοιαῦτα δοῦλοι [εἰσι] {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1278a12, compare “PSI” 4.361.15 (3rd c.BC), 1st c.BC: Nicolaus Damascenus Historicus 4 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus}}; λ. τρισὶν ἀνδράσιν, of a prostitute, {{Refs|“Anthologia Graeca” 5.48 (from Gallus).}}
**III.2** **perform religious service, minister**, ἐπὶ τῶν ἱερῶν {{NT+1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 2.22; τῷ Κυρίῳ NT.Act.13.2, etc. (Written λιτ- in “Rev.Et.Anc.” 32.5 (Athens, 1st c.BC), etc., compare λειτούργιον, λειτουργός.)}}

3009:
** λειτουργ-ία**, ἡ,
earlier _Attic dialect_ **λητ-** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“IG” 22.1140.14 (4th c.BC):—at Athens, and elsewhere (e.g. Siphnos, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 19.36; Mitylene, 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.77)}}, **public service** performed by private citizens at their own expense, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 4.42, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 21.19, etc.}}; λ. ἐγκύκλιοι ordinary, i.e. annual, **liturgies**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 20.21; λειτουργίαι μετοίκων, opposed to πολιτικαἰ, [prev. work]18.}}
**II** **any public service** or **work,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|PHib. 1.78.4 (3rd c.BC), etc.}}; ὁ ἐπὶ τῶν λειτουργιῶν τεταγμένος, in an army, **the officer who superintended the workmen, carpenters**, etc., {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.93.4; οἱ ἐπί τινα λ. ἀπεσταλμένοι [prev. author] 10.16.5}}: generally, military **duty**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“UPZ” 15.25 (_plural_, 2nd c.BC).}}
**II.2** generally, **any service** or **function**, ἡ πρώτη φανερὰ τοῖς ζῴοις λ. διὰ τοῦ στόματος οὖσα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Partibus Animalium” 650a9, compare 674b9, 20, “IA” 711b30; φιλικὴν ταύτην λ. 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Salt.” 6.}}
**II.3** **service, ministration, help**, {{NT|NT.2Cor.9.12, NT.Phil.2.30.}}
**III** **public service** of the gods, αἱ πρὸς τοὺς θεοὺς λ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1330a13; αἱ τῶν θεῶν θεραπεῖαι καὶ λ. 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 1.21, compare “UPZ” 17.17 (2nd c.BC), “PTeb.” 302.30 (1st c.AD), etc.}}; **the service** or **ministry** of priests, {{LXX+NT|LXX.Num.8.25, NT.Luke.1.23.}}

3011:
** λειτουργ-ός**, ὁ,
(λήϊτος, ἔργον) **one who performed a** λειτουργία (which see), {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“POxy.” 82.3 (3rd c.AD), etc.}}; λ. τῶν ἐν παισὶ λειτουργιῶν {{Refs|“CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 2881.13, compare 2882, 2886 (from Branchidae).}}
**II** **public servant**, ἡ στάσις τῶν λ. [τοῦ Σαλομῶνος] {{LXX|LXX.1Ki.10.5}}; of **workmen, carpenters**, etc., οἰκοδόμοι καὶ λ. {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PPetr.” 3p.139 (3rd c.BC),compare 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.93.5}}; at Magnesia, an official of the γερουσία, {{Refs|“Inscription Magn.” 116.17}}; = Latin _lictor_, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Romulus” 26: _metaphorically_, λ. τῆς χρείας μου}} **ministering** to my need, {{NT|NT.Phil.2.25.}}
**II.2** **private servant**, {{LXX|LXX.2Ki.13.18.}}
**III** in religioussense, **minister**, [θεοῦ]{{LXX+NT+1st c.BC+|LXX.Psa.102(103).21, NT.Rom.13.6, +others; τῶν θεῶν 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 2.22, compare 73; τῶν ἁγίων λ. NT.Heb.8.2}}; θεοῖς λιτουργοί (sic) {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“Rev.Et.Anc.” 32.5 (Athens, 1st c.BC)}}; **attendant** at sacrifices, **acolyte,** {{Refs|Inscriptiones Graecae 3.1005, +others}}
**IV** Astrology texts, λειτουργοί, οἱ, astral gods **subordinate** to the δεκανοί, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “de Mysteriis” 9.2, 4th c.AD: Firmicus Maternus Astrologus 2.4.4, 5th c.AD: Martianus Capella Grammaticus Latinus 2.200.}}

3013:
** λεπ-ίς**, ίδος, ἡ,
(λέπω) **epithelial debris**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἀφορισμοί” 4.81}}; **layer** of the skull, {{Refs|“PMed.” in “Arch.Papyrus” 4.270}}; ᾠοῦ λ. egg-**shell**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Scholia 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 198}}; **cup** of a filbert, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“Anthologia Graeca” 6.22 (from Zonas), 102 (1st c.AD: Philippus Epigrammaticus)}}; **coat** of an onion, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|Scholia 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Hist.Conscr.” 26.}}
**2** collectively, **scales** of fish, λεπίδος σιδηρέης ὄψιν ἰχθυοειδέος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.61; ὃ ἐν ὄρνιθι πτερόν, τοῦτο ἐν ἰχθύϊ ἐστὶ λ. 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 486b21; opposed to φολίς, [prev. work] 490b23, 517b5}}; also of serpents, variant in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “Theriaca” 154, compare 5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 82.}}
**3** of other things, λ. χαλκοῦ **flakes** that fly from copper in hammering, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 5.78, 79: _absolutely_, λεπίς 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “γυναικεῖα” 1.63.}}
**4** **plate** of metal, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd-2nd c.BC: Philo Mechanicus “(Belopoeica) Excerpte aus Philons Mechanik - Ath. Berl. Akad.” 69.50, 2nd-1st c.BC(?): Hero Mechanicus “Automatopoetica” 12.2, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 20.91, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Phocion” 18; collectively, λ. σιδηρᾶ “BGU” 544.8 (2nd c.AD)}}; of gold and silver, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 10.27.10; λ. ἀργυρᾶ “PMag.Par.” 1.258.}}
**5** λ. πρίονος **blade** of a saw, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Heliodorus Medicus cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 47.14.5.}}
**6** λεπίδες (i.e. χιόνος) snow-**flakes**, conjecture in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 4.14.13, “CP” 5.12.11.}}

3015:
** λεπρ-ός**, ά, όν,
**scaly, scabby, rough**, of places, conjecture Coraës in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Aër.” 13, 24, etc.; so βουνὸς λ. “Schwyzer” 289.169 (Priene, 2nd c.BC)}}; ἀκταὶ λ. {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Lycophron Tragicus 642}}; Λ. ἀκτή as _proper name_, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Hipponax Iambographus 47.}}
**II** **leprous**, {{LXX+4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 2.6.4, LXX.Lev.13.44}}: as _substantive_, **leper,** {{NT|NT.Mark.1.40, etc.}}; λ. ὄνυχες, probably **psoriasis unguium**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ὑγρῶν χρήσιος” 4, 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 2.114}}; τὸ λ., = λέπρα, ἡ, {{LXX|LXX.4Ki.5.11}}; ἱμάντας ἐκ λεπρῶν (i.e. δερμάτων, for the toughest leather, _accusative_ to Scholia, was supposed to be made of **mangy** skins) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 724 (but Scholia prefers ἐκ Λεπρῶν}}, _proper name_ of a “Tannery” outside the walls); λ. βαυβών {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 6.36.}}

3016:
** λεπτός**, ή, όν,
(λέπω) rare in literal sense, **peeled, husked**, ῥίμφα τε λέπτ᾽ ἐγένοντο, of barley being **threshed out**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.497.}}
**2** **fine, small**, κονίη {{Refs 5th c.BC+|23.506; κόνις 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 256; τέφρα 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 177; λεπτοῖς ἁλσί 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 187.5: frequently in 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus, διατρήσεις λ. “Loc.Hom.” 10, +others}}; of soil, **light**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 1.8.1.}}
**3** **thin, fine, delicate**, frequently in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}, mostly of garments and the {{Refs 8th c.BC+|like, ὀθόναι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.595}}; πέπλοι, φᾶρος, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.97, 10.544; ἀράχνια 8.280; μήρινθος 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.854; - ότατος χαλκός 20.275; ἔβενος, ἐλέφας, σίδηρος “BCH” 35.286 (Delos, 2nd c.BC)}}; ῥινὸς βοός {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.276 (_superlative_)}}; δέρμα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 517b27 (_superlative_)}}; τρίχες {{Refs|[prev. author] “GA” 783a4 (_comparative_)}}; σάρξ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 1189; χαλκὸς καὶ δόνακες 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 12.25, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 949, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.49, etc.}}; λεπτὰ τὰ πρῴραθεν ἔχειν, of ships, to have the bows **thin and weak**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 7.36.}}
**4** of the human figure, mostly in bad sense, **thin, lean**, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 39}}; opposed to παχύς, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 8 (_comparative_)}}; ἐγὼ δὲ λεπτὴ κἀσθενής {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 539; σοφιστῶν λεπτῶν, ἀσίτων 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 122.4; λ. καὶ αὐχμῶν 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 26.5, compare 1st c.AD: Cebes Philosophus 10; λ. χείρ 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 497; στῆθος 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1018 (_anapaest meter_); τράχηλος 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 5.30; λεπτὸς [ἐκ] τοῖν σκελοῖν 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Nav.” 2; λ. ὑπὸ μεριμνῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Amatores” 134b}}; of animals, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.4.11}}; also, **slender, taper** (opposed to παχύς), δάκτυλος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 523d}}; ἀπολήγειν εἰς λεπτόν, of the fingers of a statue, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Im.” 6.}}
**5** of space, **strait, narrow**, εἰσίθμη {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.264; ἀταρπός 7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 81}}; ἐπὶ λεπτὸν τετάχθαι in **a thin line**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.4.46, compare 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.115.6; οὔτε εὐρεῖαν οὔτε λεπτὴν.. ὁδόν 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.964c (cited in 3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “de Abstinentia - Porphyrii Opuscula” 1.6).}}
**6** generally, **small, weak, impotent**, λεπτὴ μῆτις {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.226, 23.590; ἐλπίς 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1244, compare ὀχέω 11.3; ἀσφάλεια 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator “Epistulae” 2.20}}; λ. ἴχνη **faint** traces, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 5.5}}; λ. οὖας, of a child's ear, **tiny**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 37.14}}; τὰ λ. τῶν προβάτων **small** cattle, i.e. **sheep and goats**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.137}}; λ. πλοῖα **small** craft, {{Refs|[prev. author] 7.36}}; ἄκραι λ. **small** headlands, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 8.107; λ. κλιμάκια 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 69; τὸ -ότατον τοῦ χαλκοῦ νομίσματος 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cicero” 29; λ. χαλκός “OGI” 485.12 (5th c.BC: Magnes Comicus Mae.): without χαλκός, “Inscription Perg.” 374 D 7; ἀργύριον Ῥόδιον λ. “CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 2693e5 (from Mylasa), compare “TAM” 2(1).15 (from Telmessus)}}; see below 111.2. _adverb_ -τῶς, ζῆν **poorly, meanly**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Μονόστιχοι - Fragmenta Comicorum Graecorum” 682}}: _neuter_ _plural_ as _adverb_, λεπτὰ λεύσσω κόραις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 224.}}
**7** **light, slight**, λεπταῖς ὑπαὶ κώνωπος.. ῥιπαῖσι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 892}}; λ. πνοαί **light** breezes, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 813}}; λεπταῖς ἐπὶ ῥοπῇσιν on **slight** turns of fortune, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 555.}}
**8** of size or quantity, λ. πυρίδια **small**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 1206; λ. κύλικες 5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 143.5 (but _falsa lectio_): _neuter_ _plural_ as}} _adverb_, λ. τῖλαι 'pluck into small pieces', {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 3.21.}}
**9** of liquids, **thin**, γάλα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ διαίτης” 2.46; λεπτὰ ἀνεμέειν [prev. author] “Coac.” 310}}; λ. οἶνος **light** wine, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Merc.Cond.” 18}}; also of food, λ. δίαιται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἀφορισμοί” 1.4; λ. ὀψάρια “OGI” 484.16 (Pergam.).}} _adverb_ -τῶς, διαιτᾶσθαι, διαιτᾶν{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 19.191, 7th c.AD: Paulus Aegineta Medicus 3.43.}}
**10** ={λεπτομερής}, **consisting of fine parts**, ὅσῳ -ότερον ἀὴρ ὕδατος {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physica” 215b4, compare “Cael.” 303b26, +others}}
**II** _metaphorically_, **subtle, refined**, νοῦς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 529; -ότεροι μῦθοι [prev. work] 1082 (_anapaest meter_)}}; - ότατοι λῆροι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 359; πυκνῇ.. λεπτὰ μηχανᾷ φρενί [prev. author] “Ach.” 445; λ. λογιστά [prev. author] “Av.” 318; λ. καὶ ἀκριβής 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 3.4.2; ἐς τὰς τέχνας παχέες, οὐ λεπτοί 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Aër.” 24; λόγοι λ... τρέφουσ᾽ ἐκείνους 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 220.8}}; compare λεπτολόγος. _adverb_ - τῶς, μεριμνᾶν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“Lyrica Adespota” 135; λ. καὶ πυκνῶς ἐξετάζειν 4th c.BC: Amphis Comicus 33.5}}: _comparative_ -οτέρως {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Anaxandrides Comicus 36: also κατὰ λεπτόν}}**in detail,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|PPetr. 2p.118 (3rd c.BC), 1st c.BC: Cicero, M. Tullius Orator et Philosophus “Epistulae ad Atticum” 2.18.2, 2nd c.AD: Phrynichus Atticista “Praeparatio Sophistica” p.83 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus, 9th c.AD: Photius Legal icographus, etc.}} see at {νιφετός}; compare κατάλεπτον, καταλεπτολογέω: τὰ κατὰ λεπτόν, title of poems by Aratus, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|3rd c.AD(?): Achilles Tatius Astronomus “Introductio in Aratum” p.79 M., 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 10.5.3}}; also of minor poems of Virgil; τῶν κατὰ λεπτὸν πόρων ἀραίωσις, perhaps **small** pores, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 15.201.}}
**II.2** rarely of the voice, **fine, delicate**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 545a7, 3rd c.BC: Lycophron Tragicus 687; ἁρμονία 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 773.23 (Lyric poetry)}}: _neuter_ as _adverb_, λεπτὸν ἀμφιτιττυβίζειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 235 (Lyric poetry)}}; of sound, λ. ὑποτρύζουσα {{Refs 6th c.AD+|“Anthologia Graeca” 11.352.5 (6th c.AD: Agathias Historicus et Epigrammaticus)}}; compare λεπταλέος.
**II.3** of smell, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 66e (_comparative_).}}
**II.4** of persons, οἱ λ. **the poor**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 24.7.3}}; λεπτὴν πλέκειν, proverbial of poor people, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus; λεπτὰ ξαίνεις 10th c.AD: Suidas Legal icographus}}
**III** _substantive_ λεπτόν (i.e. ἔντερον), τό, the **small intestine**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Κωακαὶ προγνώσιες” 311, 449.}}
**III.2** (i.e. νόμισμα) **a very small coin,** {{NT|NT.Luke.21.2}}, Phot. see at {ὀβολός}; compare#1.6.
**III.3** (i.e. κεράμιον) **jar,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|POxy. 920.4 (2nd-3rd c.AD), “PStrassb.” 40.48 (6th c.AD)}}; compare λεπτίον, λεπτοκεραμεύς.
**III.4** Astron. (i.e. ἑξηκοστόν), division of a degree, πρῶτα λεπτά, = **minutes**, δεύτερα λ., = **seconds**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Geminus Astronomicus 18.11, 18}}; λεπτά alone, = **minutes,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|PLond. 1.98r.47 (1st-2nd c.AD), “POxy.” 1476 (3rd c.AD).}}

3021:
** λευκαίνω**,
late _aorist 1_ _infinitive_ λευκᾶναι (see. below): _perfect_ _passive_ λελεύκασμαι {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Diphilus Siphnius Rerum Naturalium Scriptor cited in 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 2.54b, 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus “fragment” 102}}:—**make white**, λεύκαινον ὕδωρ ξεστῇς ἐλάτῃσιν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.172; ἅλα ῥοθίοισι λ. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 17; ἐς γένυν ἕρπει λευκαίνων ὁ χρόνος 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 14.70}}; **plaster with whitening**, λευκᾶναι τὰ λευκώματα {{Refs|“Ephes.” 3p.148}}:—_passive_, **to be** or **become white**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione Animalium” 730a6, 4th c.BC: Sopater Comicus 8, 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 1.545}}, Diph.Siph.[same place]; **to be clarified**, of oil, Orib. [prev. cited]
**2** **cause to appear white**, of the effect of dawn on lamplight, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 157 (_anapaest meter_).}}
**II** _passive_, **have a sensation of whiteness**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 7.191, +others}}
**III** _intransitive_, **grow white**, {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hyps.fragment” 34(60) i13, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 890a9, LXX.Lev.13.19}}; ἀφροῖο **with** foam, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “Alexipharmaca” 170.}}

3022:
** λευκός**, ή, όν,
**light, bright, clear** (opposed to μέλας in all senses), αἴγλη {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.45}}; λευκὸν (variant{λαμπρόν}).. ἠέλιος ὥς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.185; λ. φάος 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 708 (Lyric poetry),compare below 11.3; αἰθήρ 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 1228 (_anapaest meter_)}}; of metallic surfaces, λέβης {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.268}}; λ. γαλήνη a **glassy** calm, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.94}}; of water, **clear, limpid**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.282, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.70, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 23 (_anapaest meter_); λ. νᾶμα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 573; - ότατος ποταμῶν 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Jovem” 19.}}
**2** _metaphorically_, **clear, distinct**, of the voice, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Topica” 106a25, 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 6.41}}: in literary sense, **clear**, λ. στίχος {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“Anthologia Graeca” 11.347 (1st c.AD: Philippus Epigrammaticus): proverbial, λευκὸς Ἑρμῆς}}, when a rogue was detected, {{Refs|15th c.AD: Macarius Paroemiographus 5.53.}} _adverb_ -κῶς, πάντα φαίνειν, of Hermes, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Cornutus Philosophus “de Natura Deorum” 16}}: _comparative_ -ότερον, διαλεχθῆναι {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Heliodorus Scriptor Eroticus 7.20.}}
**II** of colour, **white**, frequently in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}, varying from the pure **white** of snow ([ἵπποι] -ότεροι χιόνος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.437)}} to the **grey** of dust (λευκοὶ ἐγένοντο κονισάλῳ {{Refs|5.503); γάλα λ. 4.434; κρῖ 5.196; ἄλφιτα 18.560; ἡνία λεύκ᾽ ἐλέφαντι 5.583; ὀδόντες 10.263; ὀστέα 16.347; ἱστία 1.480; φᾶρος 18.353, etc.}}; λ. ἅρμα, ={λεύκιππον}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 172}}; of the white horses used by tyrants, λ. ζεῦγος {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.158}}, compare λεύκιππο; λ. λίθος marble, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“OGI” 219.36 (Sigeum, 3rd c.BC)}}, etc., compare λευκόλιθο; λευκῷ[ν]λίθῳ λ. στάθμη a **white** line on a **white** stone, proverbial of explanations which do not explain, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 330}}; ἡ λ. ῥίζα **white** root (= ἡ τοῦ δρακοντίου, _accusative_ to {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 19.118), 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ νούσων” 2.48, “Nat.Mul.” 32}}; frequently of **white** or **grey** hair, λ. κάρη {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Tyrtaeus Elegiacus 10.23; θρίξ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1092; λ. γῆρας [prev. author] “Aj.” 625 (Lyric poetry); λευκὰ γήρᾳ σώματα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 909, etc.}}
**II.b** of the human skin, **white, fair**, sometimes as a sign of youth and beauty, χρώς, πήχεε, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.573, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 23.240}}; λ. παρειά, παρηΐς, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1239, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 923; σάρξ, δέρη, [prev. work] 1189 (variant), “IA” 875 (troch.)}}; frequently with the notion of **bare**, κῶλον, πούς, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ba.” 665, 863 (Lyric poetry), “Ion” 221 (Lyric poetry)}}; compare λευκόπους.
**II.c** of persons, **white-skinned**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 474e}}: hence, **weakly, womanish**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 191, “Ec.” 428, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.4.19; λευκῶν ἀνδρῶν οὐδὲν ὄφελος 15th c.AD: Macarius Paroemiographus 5.55}}; compare λευκόπρωκτος, λευκόχρως.
**II.d** λευκαὶ φρένες in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.109 is explained by 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus μαινόμεναι}}, **frantic, passionate** (compare λευκῶν πραπίδων· κακῶν φρενῶν, {{Refs|[prev. author]).}}
**II.2** λ. χρυσός, **pale** gold, i.e. gold **alloyed with silver** (probably the same as ἤλεκτρον), opposed to ἄπεφθος χρυσός, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.50.}}
**II.3** _metaphorically_, **bright, fortunate, happy**, λευκὸν ἦμαρ νυκτὸς ἐκ μελαγχίμου {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 301, compare “Ag.” 668}}; a **joyful** day or **holiday**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Aetia” 1.1.2}}; λ. ἡμέρα a **happy** day, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 6, cf. Scholia Callias Epigrammaticus “Iamb.” in “PSI” 9.1094.39; variously explained in 3rd c.BC: Phylarchus Historicus 83 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pericles” 27}}; ἡ λ. ψῆφος the vote of acquittal, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Harm.” 3, compare 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}
**III** λεύκη, ἡ, leuko/n, τό, as _substantive_, (see entry). (Cogn. with Latin _lux_, etc.)

3023:
** λέων**, οντος, ὁ,
_Epic dialect_ _dative_ _plural_ λείουσι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.782, etc., λεόντεσσι “IG” 12(2).285 (Mytil., probably)}}:—**lion**, ὠμοφάγοι λ. Il.[same place]; αἴθων 18.161; χαροποί {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.611; ὀρεσίτροφος 6.130, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.126}}; compare λίς: _metaphorically_, of Artemis, σὲ λέοντα γυναιξὶ Ζεὺς θῆκεν Zeus made thee a **lion** toward women (because she was supposed to cause their sudden death), {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.483}}; used of savage persons, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 938 (Lyric poetry)}}; but also, of brave men, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ag.” 1259, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1401 (Lyric poetry), 1555}}; of a baby, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 514 (but by way of contrast, of a coward, λέοντ᾽ ἄναλκιν, of Aegisthus, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1224); οἴκοι μὲν λέοντες, ἐν μάχῃ δ᾽ ἀλώπεκες 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 1189 (Lyric poetry); ἀντὶ λέοντος πίθηκον γίγνεσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 590b; ξυρεῖν ἐπιχειρεῖν λέοντα, of a dangerous undertaking, 'to bell the cat', [prev. work] 341c.}}
**2** “Leo”, the sign in the Zodiac, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Eudoxus Astronomus cited in 2nd c.BC: Hipparchus Astronomicus 1.2.8, 3rd c.BC: Euclides Geometra “Phaenomena” p.12 M., 4th-3rd c.BC: Aratus Epicus 148, “IG” 14.1307.}}
**3** ={λεοντῆ}, **lion's skin**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Hist.Conscr.” 10.}}
**4** as an ornament, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“BGU” 387ii5 (2nd c.AD).}}
**II** a kind of **crab**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Diphilus Siphnius Rerum Naturalium Scriptor cited in 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 3.106c, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 14.9.}}
**III** a kind of **serpent**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “Theriaca” 463, _falsa lectio_ in 2nd c.AD: Artemidorus Daldianus Onirocriticus 2.13.}}
**IV** ={λεοντίασις}, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “περὶ αἰτιῶν καὶ σημείων χρονίων παθῶν” 2.13.}}
**V** a kind of **dance**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 14.629f, 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 4.104.}}
**VI** title of grade of initiates in the mysteries of Mithras, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “de Abstinentia - Porphyrii Opuscula” 4.16.}}
**VII** a **sea-monster**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 9.49, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Oppianus Anazarbensis Epicus “Halieutica - Poetae Bucolici et Didactici” 1.367, 4th-5th c.AD(?): Nonnus Epicus “Dionysiaca” 1.273.}}
**VIII** ={ὀροβάγχη}, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 2.142, “Geoponica” 2.42 at Title Page}}

3024:
** λήθ-η**,
_Doric dialect_ **λάθα**, ἡ, **forgetting, forgetfulness**, personified in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 227; μηδέ σε λήθη αἱρείτω 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.33; [Περσεφόνη] βροτοῖς παρέχει λήθην, βλάπτουσα νόοιο 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 705; κακοῦ λ. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 878, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 282, “Or.” 213}}; λήθην ποιεύμενος τά μιν ἐόργεε **forgetting**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.127; λ. ποιεῖν τινος 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 259; Λήθην.. κωφήν, ἄναυδον [prev. author] “fragment” 670; χρόνος πάντα.. ἐς λ. ἄγει [prev. author] “fragment” 954; τῶν ἰδίων λ. λαβών 4th c.BC: Timocles Comicus 6.5, compare 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.254 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus; τῶν αὑτοῦ κακῶν ἐπάγεσθαι λ. 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 467; παρέχειν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 275a; ἐμποιεῖν [prev. author] “Phlb.” 63e; λήθην ἐμποιῆσαι τῶν πεπραγμένων 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 1.8; εἰς λήθην ἐμβαλεῖν τινα 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.205}}; λήθη λαμβάνει, ἔχει τινά, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.49, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.283; λήθη τινὸς ἐγγίγνεταί τινι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.2.21}}; εἰς λ. ἀφιγμένα **forgotten**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Ir.” p.19 W.}}
**II** after {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}, of **a place of oblivion** in the lower world, Λήθης δόμοι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 184.6; τὸ Λήθης πεδίον 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 186; τὸ τῆς Λ. π. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 621a, 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 8.52; Λ. ὕδωρ 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DMort.” 13.6, 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 9.39.8, 5th-4th c.BC: Aesopus Fabularum Scriptor 168}}; also, ὁ τῆς Λήθης ποταμός, of the river Λιμαίας in Lusitania, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 3.3.4, 5, compare 2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Ἰβηρική” 73 (71). (Λήθη as _proper name_ of a river is not found.)}}

3025:
** ληνός**,
_Doric dialect_ **λᾱνός** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 7.25, “IG” 14.150.5 (from Syracuse)}}: ἡ:— **anything shaped like a tub** or **trough**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Mochl.” 38}}; especially
**1** **winevat** in which the grapes are pressed, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PCair.Zen.” 300.15 (3rd c.BC), 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 7.25, 25.28, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 3.63.}}
**2** **trough**, for watering cattle, **watering-place** for them, {{LXX|“hymnus ad Mercurium” 104, LXX.Gen.30.38, 41.}}
**3** ={κάρδοπος}, **kneading-trough**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 116.}}
**4** **socket into which the mast fitted**, ={ἱστοπέδη}, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd-1st c.BC: Asclepiades Myrleanus Historicus cited in 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 11.474f, 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 1.91.}}
**5** **coffin**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 5, “CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 1979, +others (from Thessalonica), “IG” [prev. cited]}}
**6** part of the brain, **the meeting-point of the sinuses of the dura mater**, still called **torcular Herophili**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Herophilus Medicus cited in 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 2.712, compare “UP” 9.6.}}
**7** **hollow of a chariot**, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus (_plural_).}}
**8** in _plural_, **the lower parts of the nose**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 2.80.}}

3027:
** λῃστ-ής**, οῦ, ὁ,
_Ionic dialect_ **ληϊστής**, _Doric dialect_ **λᾳστής**, (ληΐς, ληΐζομαι) **robber, pirate**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 766, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.4.23, etc.}}; opposed to κλέπτης, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 351c}}; especially by sea, **buccaneer**, later πειρατής, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.138, etc.; λῃστοῦ βίον ζῆν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 507e}}; ληϊστὴς κατεστήκεε Καρχηδονίων he began a course of **piracies** upon them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.17, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.5, 8, 6.4; οἱ λ. αὑτοὺς ποριστὰς καλοῦσιν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1405a25}}; of irregular troops, {{Refs|“IG” 12(2).526 (from Eresos).}}
**II** _metaphorically_, λ. ἐναργὴς τῆς ἐμῆς τυραννίδος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 535; Κύπριδος 3rd c.BC: Lycophron Tragicus 1143}}; λῃστὰ λογισμοῦ, of love, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“APl.” 4.198 (1st c.AD(?): Maecius Epigrammaticus).}}

3028:
** λῆψις**, εως, ἡ
(later **λῆμψις** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“POxy.” 1088.45 (1st c.AD), etc.)}}, **taking hold, seizing, catching**, ῥύγχος.. πρὸς τὰς λ. τῶν ζῳδαρίων {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Partibus Animalium” 662b9; αἱ καμπαὶ τῶν δακτύλων καλῶς ἔχουσι πρὸς τὰς λ. καὶ πιέσεις [prev. work] 687b10}}; ἀπορώτερος ἡ λ. the **seizure** of them will be more difficult, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.110}}; ἡ λ. τῆς πόλεως the **seizure** of it, {{Refs|[prev. author] 4.114, compare 7.25.}}
**2** **accepting, receiving**, ἥδιστον ὅτῳ πάρεστι λ. ὧν ἐρᾷ καθ᾽ ἡμέραν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 356; ἡ τοῦ μισθοῦ λ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 346d; opposed to ἀπόδοσις, [prev. work] 332b}}; opposed to ἀποβολαί (loss), {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1362a35 (_plural_)}}: in _plural_, **receipts**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 343d, Alcaeus1.123a, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1122a13, +others}}
**2.b** **taking** of medicine, προλούσας πρὸ τῆς λ. {{Refs|“POxy.” [prev. cited]}}
**II** **attack** of fever or sickness, **seizure**, ἀπὸ τῆς πρώτης λ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 1.6, compare “Morb.” 1.18, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 866a26.}}
**III** in Logic, **assumption** {{Refs|compare (λῆμμα 11), [prev. author] “APr.” 24a23, 24b11.}}
**III.2** τῇ ἡμετέρᾳ λ. from our **point of view**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Asclepiodotus Tacticus “Tact.” 7.8.}}
**IV** **choice of matter**, in a poem, etc., {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Longinus Rhetor 10.3; compare λῆμμα 111.}}
**V** **choice of pitch**, in Music, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC(?): Ocellus Lucanus Philosophus 4.8, 3rd c.AD(?): Aristides Quintilianus Musicus 1.11 bis.}}
**VI** Geometry texts, τὴν τοῦ κέντρου τοῦ ἐκκέντρου λ. the **determination** of the centre of the eccentric circle, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “Hypotyposis astronomicarum positionum” 5.56.}}

3029:
** λίαν**
[see. near the end], _Ionic dialect_ and _Epic dialect_ **λίην**, _adverb_ **very, exceedingly**, in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} with an _adverb_, λ. ἑκάς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.496; λ. ἀεικελίως 8.231}}: with an _adjective_, λ. μέγα εἶπες {{Refs|3.227, 16.243; νήπιος λ. τόσον 4.371, compare 13.238; λ. λυπρός 13.243; λ. ἐνθύμιος [prev. work] 421}}: alone with a Verb, **very much, overmuch**, κεχολώατο λ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|14.282; λ. ἄχθομαι ἕλκος 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.361}}; οὔ τι λ. ποθὴ ἔσσεται not **exceedingly**,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|14.368; μή τι λ. προκαλίζεο 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.20, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.486; also, in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, καὶ λίην}}, which always begins the sentence or verse, **surely, aye surely**, καὶ λ. κεῖνός γε ἐοικότι κεῖται ὀλέθρῳ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.46, compare 3.203, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.553, +others}}
**II** after {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, ἀσχάλα μὴ λίην 7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 66.7, compare 6th c.BC: Solon Lyricus 6; λίην δὲ δειλιάζεις 6th c.BC: Anacreon Lyricus cited in Ptolemaeus Ascalonita p.409 H.}}; λ. πιστεύειν to believe **implicitly**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.96; μὴ κάμνε λ. 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 1.90; μὴ λ. στένε 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1172, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 158 (Lyric poetry); ἀσπάζου αὐτὴν λ. “POxy.” 936.13 (3rd c.AD)}}; καὶ λ. σαφῶς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1231; λ. ἀσελγῶς 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 24.15; λ. πόρρω 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 310c; ἐντὸς λ. τῶν τειχῶν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.5}}: with other words of {{Refs 5th c.BC+|like sense, πολὺ λ. 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 9.48; λ. πάνυ 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 184.2 (uncertain), compare 12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis 972.46}}; κόμπος λ. εἰρημένος, opposed to πεπλασμένος (compare our **very** and **verily**), {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 1031:}} —in Trag.and Comedy texts frequently between _Article_ and Noun, ἡ λ. φιλότης βροτῶν his **too great** love.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 123 (_anapaest meter_); τὸ λ. ποτόν 5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 187; ἡ λ. τρυφή 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 587}}; τὰ λ. μειράκια Theopomp.{{Refs 4th c.BC+|Comedy texts 29; αἱ πρὸς τυράννους λίαν ὁμιλίαι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 6.21}}; τὸ λ. **excess, violence**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 866, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 415c. [8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} has ῐ nine times, ῑ thirty-two times; the latter is found both in arsi and in thesi. In later _Epic dialect_ and Trag. both quantities are found: ᾱ always.]

3030:
** λίβᾰνος** [ῐ], ὁ,
**frankincense-tree, Boswellia Carterii**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.75, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 9.4.2, 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 1.68, etc.; ἱερόδακρυς λ. 5th c.BC: Melanippides Lyricus 1.5.}}
**II** ={λιβανωτός}, **frankincense**, in which sense it is _feminine_ in {{Refs 7th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 122.3, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 144 (Lyric poetry); but _masculine_ in “PCair.Zen.” 69.13 (3rd c.BC), “Anthologia Graeca” 6.231 (1st c.AD: Philippus Epigrammaticus), 9.93 (Antip. Thess.), “Edict.Diocl.” (Ἀθηνᾶ 18.6, Tegea); indeterminate in 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in E. Diehl, 20c. 2, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 1064, 4th c.BC: Anaxandrides Comicus 41.37, “SIG” 247ii 19 (Delph., 4th c.BC).}}

3031:
** λῐβᾰνωτός**, ὁ,
also ἡ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Sam.fragment” 1}}:— **frankincense**, the gum of the tree λίβανος, used to burn at sacrifices, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Xenophanes Poeta Philosophus 1.7, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.183, 2.40, 86, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 426, “V.” 96, “Ra.” 871, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 4.4.14, etc.; λ. ὑπὲρ αὐτῶν ἐπιτιθέναι 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 1.18}}: called, when in small pieces, χόνδρος λιβανωτοῦ {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Sat.” 16; when pounded, μάννα λιβανωτοῦ “Geoponica” 6.6.1}}; compare λιβανομάννα: the best kind was λ. ἄρρην {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 1.68, 4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 2.4.}}
**2** ={λίβανος}{{Refs 4th c.BC+|1, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 9.1.6.}}
**II** **the frankincensemarket**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 304, Chamaeleon Grammaticus cited in 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 9.374b.}}
**III** ={λιβανωτρίς}, {{NT|NT.Rev.8.3, 5.}}

3034:
** λῐθ-άζω**,
(λίθος) **fling stones**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 881b1, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 10.29.5; ἐπὶ σκοπόν 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 15.1.43.}}
**2** **throw like stones**, χρυσὸν εἴς τινα {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Βασιλική” 4.}}
**II** **stone**, τινα {{NT+4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Anaxandrides Comicus 16, LXX.2Ki.16.6, NT.John.10.31}}:— _passive_, uncertain meaning in {{Refs|“IGRom.” 4.494.13 (Pergam.).}}

3037:
** λίθος** [ῐ], ου,
o((see. below 11), **stone**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, etc.}}; especially of the **stones** thrown by warriors, τρηχὺς λ., λ. ὀκριόεις, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.308, 8.327}}; also, **stonequoit**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.190; ἑλέσθαι.. ἐκ γαίας λίθον 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 199.4}}; of building- **stones**, λίθοι βασιλικοί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“PSI” 4.423.28, “PCair.Zen.” 499.20 (both 3rd c.BC): proverbial, ἐν παντὶ γάρ τοι σκορπίος φρουρεῖ λίθῳ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 37}}; λίθον ἕψειν 'to lose one's labour', {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 280}}; also of stupid persons, 'blockheads', λίθοι {{Refs 6th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Nu.” 1202, compare 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 568, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Hp.Ma.” 292d, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 9.656}}; λ. τις, ou) dou/lh {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 6.4}}; προσηγορεύθη διὰ τὸ μὴ φρονεῖν λ., of Niobe, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 101; ὥσπερ λίθον ζῆν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 494a {λιθοσπαδὴς}}}]; λίθῳ λαλεῖς proverbial of ἀναίσθητοι, {{Refs|15th c.AD: Macarius Paroemiographus 5.61.}}
**2** **stone** as a substance, opposed to wood, flesh, etc., ἐπεὶ οὔ σφι λ. χρὼς οὐδὲ σίδηρος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.510}}; λαοὺς δὲ λίθους ποίησε turned into **stone, petrified**,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|24.611, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 198c; so [νῆα] θεῖναι λ. 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.156}}; as an emblem of hard-heartedness, σοὶ δ᾽ αἰεὶ κραδίη στερεωτέρη ἐστὶ λίθοιο {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|23.103, compare 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 3.18.}}
**II** λίθος, ἡ, twice in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.287, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.494, just like _masculine_, also in 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 7.26, 2nd c.BC: Bion Bucolicus “fragment” 1.2}}: later mostly of **some special stone**, as the magnet is called Μαγνῆτις λ. by {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 567 (but ἡ λίθος simply in 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 11k, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physica” 267a2, compare variant “de An.” 405a20)}}; also Λυδία λ. by {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 800 (but in 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus “fragment” 10 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus Λυδία λ.}} = **touchstone**); Ἡρακλεία λ. by {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Ion” 533d, 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Fragmenta” 293}}; so of a **touchstone**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 486d}}; ἡ διαφανὴς λ. **a piece of crystal** used for a burning-glass, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 767, compare 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Alex.” 21}}; χυτὴ λ. was perhaps a kind of **glass**, and so an older name for ὕαλος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Epinicus Comicus 1.8 (the same thing as the ἀρτήματα λίθινα χυτά in 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.69; compare τὴν ὕαλον.. ὅσα τε λίθων χυτὰ εἴδη καλεῖται 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 61c)}}; λ. = **precious stone** is _feminine_ in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ γυναικείης φύσιος” 99, “IG” 22.1421.92, 1460.21, but _masculine_ in 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.44, etc.}}; in the sense of **marble** mostly _masculine_, λευκὸς λ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 4.87 (simply λίθος 1.164), 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 330 (λευκοὶ λ. is opposed to πέτρινοι λ. “Supp.epigram” 4.446.8 (from Didyma))}}; Πάριος λ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 4.81, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.57; Ταινάριος λ. 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 8.5.7}}; λ. Θάσιος, Αἰγύπτιος, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 1.18.6, etc.; κογχίτης [prev. author] 1.44.6; κογχυλιάτης 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.4.10; but Παρία λ. 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 6.38, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Am.” 13}}; compare λυχνίας, -ίτη; πώρινος λ. **tufa**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.62.}}
**II.2** collectively, πέφυκε λίθος.. ἄφθονος, ἐξ οὗ.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Vectigalibus” 1.4.}}
**III** **grave-stone** (_feminine_), {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Epigrammata” 8.1.}}
**IV** at Athens, λίθος, ὁ, was a name for various **blocks of stone used for rostra** or **platforms**, as,
**IV.1** the βῆμα (which see) of the Pnyx, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 683, “Pax” 680, “Ec.” 87.}}
**IV.2** another in the ἀγορά used by the κήρυκες, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Solon” 8}}; probably the same as ὁ πρατὴρ λ., on which the auctioneer stood when selling slaves, etc., {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 3.78, compare 126.}}
**IV.3** an **altar** in the ἀγορά, at which the Thesmothetae, arbitrators, and witnesses took their oaths, [{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Philochorus Historicus 65, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 54.26 (restored from 1st-2nd c.AD: Harpocratio Grammaticus see at {λίθος}), 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 7.1, 55.5, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Solon” 25}}]; compare λιθωμότης.
**IV.4** two **stones** on which litigants stood in the Areopagus, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 1.28.5.}}
**V** **piece on a draughtboard**, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 82, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 6.18, compare γραμμή 111.1: hence pronoun, πάντα λίθον κινεῖν 2nd c.AD: Zenobius Paroemiographus 5.63 (who explains it differently).}}
**VI** in Medicine texts, **stone in the bladder, calculus**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 519b19, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ νούσων” 4.55, +others}}
**VII** Δία λίθον ὀμνύναι, = Latin _Jovem lapidem jurare_, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.25.6.}}
**VIII** λίθοι χαλάζης hail- **stones**, {{LXX|LXX.Josh.10.11.}}
**IX** λ. ὁ οὐ λ. the philosophers' **stone**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Zosimus Epigrammaticus Alch. p.122 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus}}

3038:
** λῐθό-στρωτος**, ον,
**paved with stones**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1204; δρόμος “POxy.” 2138.15 (3rd c.AD), “PFlor.” 50.97 (3rd c.AD).}}
**2** especially of **tessellated work**, λ. ἔδαφος {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 17.1.28, 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 7.121}}; λ., τό, **mosaic** or **tessellated pavement,** {{NT+4th c.BC+|Inscriptiones Graecae 42(1).110.19 (Epid., 4th-3rd c.BC), LXX.Cant.3.10, NT.John.19.13, “CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 2643}} (Cyprus, -στρατ-), Roussel **Délos: Colonie Athénienne** p.422, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 4.7.37 (variant), etc.}}

3040:
** λῐμήν**, ένσς, ὁ,
**harbour**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.432 (here distinguished from ὅρμος, mooringplace), +others, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 25a, etc.}}; Κανθάρου λ. a dockyard in the Piraeus, with a pun on κάνθαρος just above, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 145 (ubi see Scholia): frequently in _plural_, λιμένες νηῶν ὀχοί 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.404; λιμένες δ᾽ ἔνι ναύλοχοι αὐτῇ 4.846; λιμένες τε πάνορμοι 13.195, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 936, etc.: with _genitive_ objecti, λιμένες θαλάσσης}} **havens of refuge from** the sea, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.418, compare 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 207.}}
**II** _metaphorically_, **haven, retreat, refuge**, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 460}}; ἑταιρείας λ. **a haven of** friendship, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 683; οὗτος.. λ. πέφανται τῶν ἐμῶν βουλευμάτων 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 769: with _genitive_ objecti, λ. κακῶν}} **from** ills, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 471; ὦ ναυτίλοισι χείματος λ. φανείς 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 891; ὕπνον.. τῶν καμάτων λ. 5th c.BC: Critias Philosophus, Tragicus, Elegiacus 6.20 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator; λ. τῆς πλάνης ἥδε ἡ γῆ μόνη λείπεται 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 1.58.}}
**II.2** **gathering-place, receptacle**, πλούτου λ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 250; μέγας 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1077; παντὸς οἰωνοῦ λ. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1000}}; Ἅιδου λ. **harbour** of death, {{Refs|[prev. work] 1284 (Lyric poetry)}}; ξείνων αἰδοῖοι λιμένες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 112.3}}; βοῆς τῆς σῆς ποῖος οὐκ ἔσται λ.; what place shall not **harbour** (i.e. receive) thy cry? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 420.}}
**III** ={ἀγορά} in Thessaly and Paphos, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“IG” 9(2).517.42 (from Larissa), 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus “Θρασύβουλος - Scripta Minora” 32, 1st-2nd c.AD: Dio Chrysostomus Sophista 11.23 (interpolated).}}
**IV** **the source of birth, womb**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 98.3, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1208 (Lyric poetry).}}

3041:
** λίμν-η**, ἡ,
**pool of standing water** left by the sea or a river, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.317}}: hence, **marshy lake, mere**, distinguished from ἕλος, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Critias” 114e, “Lg.” 824c; Βοιβηΐς λ. 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.711}}; Γυγαίη{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 865; Κηφισίς 5.709; λ. Γοργῶπις 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 302; Μαιῶτις [prev. author] “Pr.” 419 (Lyric poetry), compare 729, “Pers.” 871 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.86; ἡ Βόλβη λ. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.103}}; λ. τροχοειδής, at Delos, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.170, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 9.}}
**b** also, **artificial pool** or **basin**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.185, 191, +others, “SIG” 799 ii 3 (Cyzic., 1st c.AD).}}
**2** in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} and other Poets, **the sea**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.79, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.1; βένθεσι λίμνης 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.21, 32}}: so in Trag. in Lyric poetry, λίμνᾳ πορφυροειδεῖ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 529; ἐπ᾽ οἶδμα λίμνας 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 476, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 446; Πόσειδον, ὃς γλαυκᾶς μέδεις.. λίμνας 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 371}}; Μηλίδα πὰρ λ. by the Malian **bay**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Tr.” 636.}}
**II** Λίμναι, αἱ, (used without the article), **a quarter of Athens** (once probably **marshy**), near the Acropolis, in which stood the Lenaeum, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 216, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.15, 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 8.35, etc., compare λιμναῖος 11.}}
**II.2** **a quarter** or **suburb of Sparta**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 8.5.1.}}
**II.3** **a place in Messenia**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 8.4.9.}}

3042:
** λῑμός**, οῦ, ὁ
(_Doric dialect_ ἡ, _accusative_ to {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Phrynichus Atticista 164, used by the Megarian in 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 743, compare 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 2.17, 2nd c.BC: Bion Bucolicus “fragment” 14.4}}; Λ. ἔχων γυναικὸς μορφήν Callisth. cited in {{NT+2nd c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 10.452b; also “hymnus ad Cererem” 311, Callias Epigrammaticus “anonymous fragment” 43, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.84.9, “Anthologia Graeca” 9.89 (1st c.AD: Philippus Epigrammaticus), NT.Luke.15.14, NT.Act.11.28)}}:—**hunger, famine**, δίψα τε καὶ λ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.166; λιμῷ θανέειν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.342; λιμὸν ὁμοῦ καὶ λοιμόν 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 243, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.54; λ. αἰανής 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 1.49; λιμῷ συνεστεῶτας 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.170; σκότῳ λ. ξύνοικος 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1642; δείπνου προφήτην λιμόν 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 217.23; ἅπανθ᾽ ὁ λ. γλυκέα πλὴν αὑτοῦ ποιεῖ [prev. author] 293; ὁ δὲ λ. ἐστιν ἀθανασίας φάρμακον [prev. author] 86.6: proverbial, ἀπολεῖτε λιμῷ Μηλίῳ}}, referring to the siege of Melos, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 186: _metaphorically_, ἤδη γὰρ εἶδον.. λιμόν τ᾽ ἐν ἀνδρὸς πλουσίου φρονήματι, γνώμην δὲ μεγάλην ἐν πένητι σώματι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 371.}}
**II** **a hungry wretch**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Κόλαξ” 78, 3rd c.BC: Posidippus Comicus 26.12, 12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis 1828.6.}}

3043:
** λίνον** [ῐ], τό,
**anything made of flax** (see. near the end)
**1** **cord, fishingline**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.408}}; **thread** spun from a distaff, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1431 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Archippus Comicus 38, etc.: _plural_, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 537 (Lyric poetry)}}; **thread** for stringing jewels, ὅρμον χρυσείοισι λίνοισιν ἐερμένον {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“hymnus ad Apollinem” 103; ἄλλα παντοδαπὰ χρυσᾶ ἀνηρμένα ἐπὶ λίνου “IG” 11(2).208.22 (Delos, 3rd c.BC)}}; **thread** used as a **ligature**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 2.669.}}
**2** _metaphorically_, **thread of destiny** spun by the Fates, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.128, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.198, etc.: _plural_, τά γε μὰν λ. πάντα λελοίπει ἐκ Μοιρᾶν 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 1.139, compare 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Lavacrum Palladis” 104}}; ὑπὲρ τὸ λ., ={ὑπὲρ μόρον}, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “JConf.” 2.}}
**3** proverbial, with or without _negative_, λίνον λίνῳ συνάπτειν, i. e. join like with like, deal with matters of like kind, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 298c, 5th c.BC: Strattis Comicus 38, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physica” 207a17.}}
**4** **fishing-net**, ἀψῖσι λίνου ἁλόντε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.487, compare κλωστήρ 11}}; also, **hunting-net**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 8.58, 27.17}}; for catching birds, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 1.60, “Anthologia Graeca” 9.396 (Paul. Sil.), 343 (1st c.BC: Archias)}}; λ. δορκάδεια **hunting-nets** for gazelles, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PCair.Zen.” 524 (3rd c.BC).}}
**5** **linen, linen-cloth**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.661, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.73, 118}}: _plural_, **linen cloths, linen garments**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 121, 132 (both Lyric poetry)}}: _singular_, **linen garment,** {{NT|NT.Rev. 15.6}}; **sail-cloth**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 364, 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 1.565, etc.}}
**6** **flax** for spinning, λίνου μεστὸν ἄτρακτον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1347, compare “PRev.Laws” 39.7 (3rd c.BC), etc.}}; λ. Καρπάσιον **asbestos**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 1.26.7.}}
**II** **the plant flax, Linum usitatissimum**, λίνον ἐργάζεσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.105, etc.}}; λίνου σπέρμα **linseed**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.26: _plural_, ἡ ἐκ των λ. δημιουργία 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 280c.}}
**II.2** ={θυμελαία}, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 4.172.}}
**II.3** ={χρυσόγονον}, {{Refs|[prev. work]56.}}
**II.4** λ. πύρινον, an unknown plant, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 9.18.6.}}
**II.5** λ. ἀπὸ τῶν δενδρέων **cotton, Gossypium herbaceum**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|Nearchus Historicus cited in 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Indica” 1.16.1.}}
**III** see at {Λίνος} {{Refs|11. (Lith.}}**linaĩ** _plural_ 'flax', with ῐ as in Gr., but Latin _linum_, O{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus}} **lin**, etc. with ῑ: ῑ also in the doubtful words λινόσαρκος, λινοπτάομαι, ἀμφίλινος.)

3045:
** λῐπᾰρ-ός**, ά, όν,
(λίπα) properly **oily, shiny with oil**, once in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}, **anointed**, λιπαροὶ κεφαλὰς καὶ καλὰ πρόσωπα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.332; λ. χωρεῖν ἐκ βαλανείου 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 616; θεᾶσθαι λιπαρὸν παρὰ τῷ Διονύσῳ [prev. author] “Eq.” 536, compare “Nu.” 1002}}; σοὶ δὲ μελήσει.. λιπαρῷ (Bentl. for -ρῶς) χωρεῖν ἐπὶ δεῖπνον {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ec.” 652}}; of oil or oily objects, **shiny**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 148.4, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.1.31, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 60a, 84a, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 520a27.}}
**2** **fatty, greasy**, ἄρτος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 109}}; τὰ λ. **unctuous dishes**, {{Refs|[prev. work] 506.1}}; τὸ λ. **fatty substance**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Partibus Animalium” 651a24}}; λιπαραί (i.e. ἔμπλαστροι), αἱ, **oily** plasters, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 1.112, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 12.468: sometimes opposed to τὸ πῖον (which implies resinous substance), 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 387b6, compare “Col.” 791b23}}; of vegetables **boiled in grease**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “γυναικεῖα” 1.66.}} _adverb_ -ρῶς, ἕψειν boil **in grease**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Mul.” [same place]; so λ. ἠρτυμένον 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 9.677}}; ἀνατρίβειν massage **with a greasy** or **oily lubricant**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 9.}}
**II** of the healthy look of the human body or skin, **shining, sleek**, in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus in phrase λιπαροὶ πόδες}} **bright, smooth** feet, without a wrinkle on the skin, mostly of men's feet, in the line ποσσὶ δ᾽ ὑπὸ λιπαροῖσιν ἐδήσατο καλὰ πέδιλα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.44, +others; of Hera, 14.186}}; of Themis, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 901; - ώτεροι ἐγένοντο 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.23; λ. στῆθος 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1012; θηρία 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.4.11; χείλεα 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Am.” 13}}; **radiant**, θυγάτηρ Χρόνου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 7.1; ἄκοιτις [prev. author] 5.169.}}
**III** of condition or state of life, **rich, comfortable, easy**, γῆρας {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.136, 19.368, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 7.99; λ. εὐφροσύνη “Anthologia Graeca” 11.63 (1st c.AD(?): Macedonius Epigrammaticus)}}. _adverb_ - ρῶς, γηρασκέμεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.210; πλήσαντα λ. κύκλον ἐτῶν ἑκατόν “epigram Gr.” 451.}}
**IV** of things, **bright, brilliant**, λιπαρὴ καλύπτρη {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.406; λ. κρήδεμνα 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.334, etc.; χοροί 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 63}}; of city walls, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.388; λ. δόμος 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 15.29; νίκα [prev. author] 10.38; στέφανοι [prev. author] 1.47}}; λιπαρὰς τελέουσι θέμιστας **splendid** or **rich** tribute, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.156, 298}}; also, of the **oily smoothness** of a calm sea, λ. γαλάνα {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 22.19, compare 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Epigrammata” 6.5; also σέλας 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 23.8}}; and of smells, λ. ὀσμή **rich, savoury**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Anima” 421a30, compare “Sens.” 443b10.}}
**V** of soil, **fat, rich, fruitful**, as epithet of places, Χίος, ἣ νήσων λιπαρωτάτη εἰν ἁλὶ κεῖται {{Refs 6th c.BC+|“hymnus ad Apollinem” 38; λ. πόλις 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 947}}; λ. Ὀρχομενός, Θῆβαι, Νάξος, Μαραθών, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 14.2, “P.” 2.3, 4.88, “O.” 13.110}}; λιπαραὶ Ἀθῆναι, favourite epithet with the Athenians, probably with allusion to the Attic olive, first in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 2.20, “fragment” 76, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 639, 640}} (where he plays on the double sense of **brilliant** and **greasy), Nu.**{{Refs|300, “fragment” 110}}; λ. τὸ χρῆμα τῆς πόλεως, of Nephelococcygia, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Av.” 826}}; λ. χεύματα, of rivers, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 1028 (Lyric poetry); λ. ὅρμος 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Delum” 155; ἄντρον 5th-6th c.AD: Orphica “Hymni” 59.4 (from Hermann), etc.}}
**VI** _adverb_ λῐπαρῶς, see above {{Refs|1, 111.}}

3046:
** λίτρ-α**, ἡ,
**a silver coin** of Sicily, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 9, 5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 72, 4th-3rd c.BC: Diphilus Comicus 71, 3rd c.BC: Posidippus Comicus 8.—On its value see 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Fragmenta” 476, 510.}}
**II** as a weight, {{Refs|12 ounces}}**a pound**, [{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus] 141, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 21.43.19, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 14.116, 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 1.43, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Tiberius Gracchus” 2, 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 14.7.1: _metaphorically_, λίτρᾰν ἐτῶν ζήσας}} having lived **a pound** of years, i.e. {{Refs 6th c.AD+|72 (in late times a pound of gold was coined into 72 pieces), “Anthologia Graeca” 10.97 (6th c.AD: Palladas Epigrammaticus).}}
**III** measure of capacity, = {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|1 Italic κοτύλη, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 6.287}}. [ῑ as in Latin _libra_; written λείτρα in {{Refs|“CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 2040.7 (Bosp.).] (Both λίτρα}} and Latin _libra_ probably from early Italic ***līprā.**)

3047:
** λίψ**, ὁ,
**A**_genitive_ λιβός, **the SW. wind**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.25, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 364b2 (_plural_), “Mu.” 394b27, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 9.11: _plural_, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 10.10.3.}}
**A.2** **the South**, frequently in {{LXX|LXX, LXX.Gen.13.14, +others}}
**A.3** **the West**, opposed to ἀπηλιώτης, {{LXX+2nd c.BC+|“PTeb.” 14.19 (2nd c.BC), 2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 8.5, 4th c.AD: Paulus Alexandrinus Astrologus “A.” 2, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Hermes Trismegistus cited in 5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 1.21.9, 1.49.45; rarely in LXX, LXX.2Ch.32.30, 33.14, 2nd c.AD(?): Theodotion LXX.Dan.8.5}}; λιβός or λίβα εἰς ἀπηλιώτην from {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“West” to East, “BGU” 1037.15 (1st c.AD), “CPR” 28.21 (2nd c.AD).}}
**A.4** Astron., πρωινὸς λ., μεσημβρινὸς λ., ὀψινὸς λ., **position** of a star **on the W. horizon** at sunrise, midday, or sunset, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Ptolemaeus Epigrammaticus “Alm.” 8.4.}}

3049:
** λογ-ίζομαι**,
_Attic dialect_ _future_ -ιοῦμαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author = 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus] “Ra.” 1263, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.87, etc.}}: _aorist_ ἐλογισάμην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 555, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.31, etc.}}: _perfect_ λελόγισμαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 32.24,27, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 28.12}}:—_passive_, see below 111: (λόγος):—properly of numerical calculation, **count, reckon**, οὐκ ἐπισταμένους λογίζεσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.16; εὗρον λογιζόμενος [prev. author] 7.28, compare 194, etc.}}; in full, λ. ψήφοισι {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.36}}; λόγισαι φαύλως, μὴ ψήφοις ἀλλ᾽ ἀπὸ χειρός **calculate** roughly, not by rule, but off-hand, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 656:}} with _accusative_ _of things_, λ. τοὺς τόκους **calculate** the interest, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Nu.” 20; τρεῖς μνᾶς ἀναλώσας λογίσασθαι δώδεκα spend 3}} minae and **set down**{{Refs|12, [prev. author] “Pl.” 381.}}
**2** with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, **reckon** or **calculate that**.., λ. μύρια εἶναι [τὰ ἔτεα] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.145; τὰς βλάβας, ἃς ἐλογίζεθ᾽ αὑτῷ γεγενῆσθαι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.176: without _accusative_, Θηριππίδῃ μισθὸν ἀποδεδωκέναι λ. [prev. author] 27.20.}}
**3** λ. τινί τι **set down to** one's **account**, οὗτος.. τὸ ἥμισυ τούτοις.. λελόγισται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 32.24}}, compare 27; τἀνηλωμέν᾽.. οὐκ ἐλογιζόμην I **did** not **charge** them.., {{NT+4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.113: _metaphorically_, τὰ παραπτώματα λ. τινί NT.2Cor.5.19.}}
**3.b** **audit the accounts of** a person, with _dative_, τοῖς ὑπευθύνοις {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 54.2; ταῖς ἀρχαῖς [prev. work] 48.3.}}
**II** without reference to numbers, **take into account, calculate, consider**, ταῦτα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.53, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 816, etc.; λ. τὰ ξυμφέροντα 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.76}}; λ. τι πρός τινας with them, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 5.24}}; also λ. περί τινος **calculate, form calculations** about.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.22, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.3.11.}}
**II.2** with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, **reckon, consider that**.., τὸν ἕτερον [παῖδα] οὐκ εἶναί μοι λ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.38; τὸν Πᾶνα τῶν ὀκτὼ θεῶν λ. εἶναι [prev. author] 2.46}}; λ. ὅτι.. or ὡς.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.4.28, 6.4.6}}; ἐλογιζόμην πρὸς ἐμαυτὸν.., ὅτι.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.52, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 21d}}: with _accusative_ et _participle_, Σμέρδιν μηκέτι ὑμῖν ἐόντα λογίζεσθε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.65}}: also with _infinitive_ omitted, **reckon** or **account** so and so, τὸν καθ᾽ ἡμέραν βίον λογίζου σόν [εἶναι], τὰ δ᾽ ἄλλα τῆς τύχης {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 789}}; πολὺν [εἶναι] τὸν κάτω χρόνον{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 692; λογίζεταί τ᾽ ἐκεῖνα πάνθ᾽ ἁμαρτίας 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 745}}; μίαν ἄμφω τούτω τὼ ἡμέρα λ. **count** both days as one, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.2.11.}}
**II.3** with _infinitive_ also, **count** or **reckon upon** doing, **calculate** or **expect** that.., ἐπισιτιεῖσθαι ἐλογίζοντο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.176; ἐλογίζετο κατύπερθέ οἱ τὰ πρήγματα ἔσεσθαι [prev. author] 8.136; λογιζόμενοι ἥξειν ἅμα ἡλίῳ δύνοντι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.2.13; λελογισμένοι.. εἰσὶν.. διαζῆν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 922, compare “Or.” 555 (uncertain reading)}}; τί λογίζομ᾽.. προσδοκῶν χάριν παρὰ γυναικὸς κομιεῖσθα; {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 564.}}
**II.4** **count upon**, εἴ τις δύο ἢ καί τι πλείους ἡμέρας λ., μάταιός ἐστιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 944.}}
**II.5** **conclude by reasoning, infer that**.., with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 524b, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Agesilaus” 7.3}}; λ. ὅτι.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “HG” 6.1.5, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 62e, +others}}
**II.6** _absolutely_, τοὺς ἐπισταμένους λογίζεσθαι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Archytas Tarentinus Philosophus 3}}; ὁ σπουδαῖος λελόγισται ἤδη **has finished reasoning**, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 3.8.6, compare 4.4.12.}}
**III** _passive_, mostly _aorist_ ἐλογίσθην and (less frequently) _perfect_ λελόγισμαι, also in _present_, _participle_ λογιζόμενον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.95}}, frequently in later Gr., {{NT+3rd c.BC+|“PPetr.” 3p.340 (3rd c.BC), NT.Rom.4.5, etc.}}; χρήματα εἰς ἀργύριον λογισθέντα **counted** or **calculated** in silver, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.1.33; ὁπλῖται ἐλογίσθησαν οὐκ ἐλάττους δισμυρίων [prev. author] “HG” 6.1.19; οὗτος λογισμὸς λογισθείς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 34b; οὐδ᾽ ἐξ ἑνὸς λόγου λελογισμένου [prev. author] “Phdr.” 246c}}; τὸ λελογισμένον, ={λογισμός}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 386, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Nigr.Prooem.”}}

3050:
** λογῐκ-ός**, ή, όν,
(λόγος) **of** or **for speaking** or **speech**, μέρη λ. the organs **of speech**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Coriolanus” 38: λογική, ἡ}}, **speech**, opposed to μουσική, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Compositione Verborum” 11; λ. φαντασία}}**expressed in speech,** {{Refs|Stoic. 2.61.}}
**2** **of** or **in eloquence**, ἀγῶνες {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vitae Sophistarum” 1.22.1; ἀκροάσεις λ. καὶ ὀργανικαί “Supp.epigram” 2.184.6 (Tanagra, 2nd c.BC).}}
**3** **suited for prose**, ὁ ἡρῷος σεμνὸς καὶ οὐ λ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demetrius Phalereus Rhetor “Demetrius on Style” 42; τὸ λ., opposed to τὸ μεγαλοπρεπές, [prev. work] 41}}; of persons, **writing in prose**, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 5.85; ἐγκώμιον λ.}}**in prose,** {{Refs|Inscriptiones Graecae 9(2).531.43 (Thess.).}}
**II** **possessed of reason, intellectual**, μέρος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-4th c.BC: Timaeus Locrus Philosophus 99e, +others; τὸ λ. ζῷον 3rd c.BC: Chrysippus Stoicus 3.95}}; ἀρεταὶ λ., ={διανοητικαί}, opposed to ἠθικαί, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1108b9.}}
**II.2** **dialectical, argumentative**, οἱ λ. διάλογοι of Plato, such as the Theaetetus and Cratylus, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 3.58; in 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus}} usually like{διαλεκτικός, λ. συλλογισμός} {{Refs|“APo.” 93a15, compare “Top.” 162b27; διὰ λογικωτέρων καὶ ἀκριβεστέρων λόγων}}**more abstract, Metaphorical** {{Refs|1080a10}}; λ. δυσχέρειαι{{Refs|[prev. work] 1005b22; λ. ἀπόδειξις “GA” 747b28}}; but also, **logical**, λ. συλλογισμοί, opposed to ῥητορικοί, {{Refs|“Rh.” 1355a13}}. _adverb_ - κῶς **dialectically, Metaphorical** {{Refs|1029b13, “APo.” 84a7, 88a19; φυσικῶς καὶ λ. “GC” 316a11}}: _comparative_ -ώτερον {{Refs|“Cael.” 275b12.}}
**II.2.b** _substantive_, ἡ λογική (i.e. τέχνη) **logic**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Cicero, M. Tullius Orator et Philosophus “de Finibus” 1.7.22; also τὰ λογικά [prev. author] “Tusc.” 4.14.33}}; περὶ λογικῶν title of work, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 10b}}; τὸ λ., opposed to τὸ φυσικόν, τὸ ἠθικόν, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Zeno Citieus Stoicus 1.15, etc.}}
**II.3** of the 'dogmatic' school of physicians, ἡ λ. αἵρεσις {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus “περὶ αἱρέσεων τοῖς εἰσαγομένοις” 1.}}

3051:
** λόγ-ιον**, τό,
**oracle**, especially one preserved from antiquity, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.178, 203, 8.60.γ, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Theseus” 26, “Lys.” 22}}: more frequently in _plural_, **oracles**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.64, 8.62, 141, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 405, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 120, +others, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Fabius Maximus” 4, “Marc.” 3: distinguished from χρησμοί, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.8}} (the former being **prose**, the latter **verse**, _accusative_ to {{Refs 1st c.AD+|Scholia, but this distinction does not hold),compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pelopidas” 20, “Nic.” 13, 2.412c.}}
**2** τὰ λ. Κυρίου the **sayings** of the Lord, {{LXX+NT|LXX.Psa.11(12).6, cf. NT.Act.7.38, NT.Rom.3.2, NT.1Pet.4.11.}}
**II** τὸ λ. τῶν κρίσεων **the oracular breastplate** worn by the Jewish High-Priest, {{LXX+1st c.AD+|LXX.Exo.28.26(30), compare 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.154; τὰ λόγια Aristeas 158.}}

3052:
** λόγ-ιος**, α, ον,
(λόγος) **of** or **belonging to** λόγοι:
**I** **versed in tales** or **stories** (compare λόγος see), λόγιοι καὶ ἀοιδοί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 1.94, compare “N.” 6.45: hence of chroniclers (opposed to poets), Περσέων οἱ λόγιοι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.1}}; Αἰγυπτίων -ώτατοι {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.3, compare 4.46}}; so later, οἱ - ώτατοι τῶν ἀρχαίων συγγραφέων {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 6.45.1, compare 38.6.1, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 2.4, 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 5.17, etc.}}
**I.2** generally, **learned, erudite**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 30, etc.; λ. περὶ τὴν ὅλην φύσιν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1267b28}}; ὁ λ. Ἀκεστῖνος, of a **learned** physician, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Heliodorus Scriptor Eroticus 4.7}}; οἱ -ώτατοι Τυρρηνῶν, of the Tuscan haruspices, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Sulla” 7; Χαλδαίων οἱ λ. 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Anabasis” 7.16.5, compare 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 17.6.2, etc.}}; λογιώτατος as title, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|“OGI” 408.5 (Theb. Aeg.), “POxy.” 902.1 (5th c.AD), etc.}}; ὁ τῆς λ. μνήμης σχολαστικός {{Refs 6th c.AD+|“PMasp.” 118.30 (6th c.AD).}}
**II** **skilled in words, eloquent**, τὸ μεγαλοπρεπὲς ὅπερ νῦν καὶ λόγιον ὀνομάζουσιν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demetrius Phalereus Rhetor “Demetrius on Style” 38, etc.; 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus}} is said to have made {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus [τὸν] -ώτατον (of his disciples), 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 13.2.4; λ. ἐξ ἀφώνου γενόμενος 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pompeius” 51}}; epithet of Hermes, as the god of eloquence, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Apol.” 2, “Gall.” 2 (_superlative_), 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 4.132a; οἱ λ. θεοί [prev. author] “Ep.” 80}}; this sense is condemned by {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Phrynichus Atticista 176}}. _adverb_ -ίως **eloquently**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.405a}}; ὡς ἐνῆν -ώτατα **as nearly in words as possible**, of the elephant, {{Refs|[prev. work]968d.}}
**III** **oracular**, Ἀπόλλωνος δῶμα λόγιον {{Refs|“Berl.Sitzb.” 1911.632 (from Cyprus).}}

3053:
** λογ-ισμός**, ὁ,
**counting, calculation**, τῶν ἡμερῶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.122; τυγχάνειν τοῦ ἀληθοῦς λ. [prev. author] 3.20; ἐκ τοιοῦδε λ. ἔξεστί τῳ σκοπεῖν [prev. author] 5.68; ἐν λ. ἁμαρτάνειν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 340d; ἀριθμὸς καὶ λ. [prev. author] “Phdr.” 274c; ἐπὶ λογισμὸν ἐλθόντες [prev. author] “Euthphr.” 7b; καθέζεσθαι ἐπὶ τοὺς λ. 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.59}}: in _plural_, **numbers, arithmetic**, λογισμοὺς μανθάνειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.7.8; λογισμούς τε καὶ ἀστρονομίαν καὶ γεωμετρίαν.. διδάσκειν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 318e, compare “R.” 510c, +others}}
**2** **account, reckoning**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 32.19, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.113, etc.}}; λ. λαμβάνειν hold an **audit**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1322b9.}}
**II** without reference to number, **calculation, reasoning**, τοῦ ξυμφέροντος λογισμῷ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.40; καθιστάναι τινὰς ἐς λ. [prev. author] 6.34; λογισμῷ ἐλάχιστα χρώμενοι [prev. author] 2.11; ἐνδέχεταί τι λογισμόν [prev. author] 4.92}}; λ. αὐτοκράτορι (see. αὐτοκράτωρ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1.4) [prev. work] 108; οὐ λογισμῷ δόντες τοὺς κινδύνους 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 2.23; λογισμὸν περί τινος ἔχειν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 805a; ὅσον ἦν ἀνθρωπίνῳ λ. δυνατόν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.300, compare 193; τοῖς λ. τοῖς ἰδίοις πταίων ἀεί 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 380; μετὰ λογισμοῦ πάντα πράττουσίν τινος [prev. author] 617, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 90.10, etc.}}; personified, opposed to Θυμός, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Cleanthes Stoicus 1.129.}}
**II.2** **reason, argument**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.4.27, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 34a.}}
**III** **reasoning power**, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus] 257.1, 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 187, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.3.11, 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Sententiae” 16, +others: frequently in 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus, τὸ τῶν ἀνθρώπων γένος [ζῇ] καὶ τέχνῃ καὶ λογισμοῖς “Metaphorical” 980b28, compare “de An.” 415a8, +others}} —Only in Prose and Comedy texts

3056:
** λόγος**, ὁ,
verbal noun of λέγω (B), with senses corresponding to λέγω (B) II and III (on the various senses of the word see {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Theon Smyrnaeus Philosophus pp.72,73 H., “Anecdota Graeca - MSS. Bibl. Oxon.” 4.327): common in all periods in Prose and Verse, exc. Epic, in which it is found in signification derived from λέγω (B)}} 111, compareinfr. VI. 1 a:
**I** **computation, reckoning** (compare λέγω (B) II).
**I.1** **account** of money handled, σανίδες εἰς ἃς τὸν λ. ἀναγράφομεν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“IG” 12.374.191; ἐδίδοσαν τὸν λ. [prev. work]232.2; λ. δώσεις τῶν μετεχείρισας χρημάτων 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.142, compare 143; οὔτε χρήματα διαχειρίσας τῆς πόλεως δίδωμι λ. αὐτῶν οὔτε ἀρχὴν ἄρξας οὐδεμίαν εὐθύνας ὑπέχω νῦν αὐτῆς 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 24.26; λ. ἀπενεγκεῖν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 54.1; ἐν ταῖς εὐθύναις τοῦ τοιούτου λ. ὑπεχέτω 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 774b; τὸν τῶν χρημάτων λ. παρὰ τούτων λαμβάνειν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 8.47; ἀδικήματα εἰς ἀργυρίου λ. ἀνήκοντα 4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 1.60}}; συνᾶραι λόγον μετά τινος settle **accounts** with, {{NT|NT.Matt.18.23, etc.}}; δεύτεροι λ. a second **audit,** {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th-6th c.AD: Codex Justinianus 1.4.26.1}}; ὁ τραπεζιτικὸς λ. banking **account**, Theo Sm.p.73 H.: _metaphorically_, οὐκ ἂν πριαίμην οὐδενὸς λ. βροτόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 477.}}
**I.1.b** **public accounts**, i. e. branch of **treasury**, ἴδιος λ., in Egypt, {{Refs|“OGI” 188.2, 189.3, 669.38}}; also as title of **treasurer**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|[prev. work]408.4, 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 17.1.12; ὁ ἐπὶ τῶν λ. “IPE” 2.29 A (from Panticapaeum)}}; δημόσιος λ., = Latin _fiscus_, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“OGI” 669.21 (Egypt, 1st c.AD)}}, etc. (but later, = **aerarium,** {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th-6th c.AD: Codex Justinianus 1.5.15); also Καίσαρος λ. “OGI” 669.30; κυριακὸς λ. [prev. work]18.}}
**I.2** generally, **account, reckoning**, μὴ φῦναι τὸν ἅπαντα νικᾷ λ. excels the whole **account**, i.e. is best of **all**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1225 (Lyric poetry)}}; δόντας λ. τῶν ἐποίησαν **accounting** for, i.e. paying the penalty for their doings, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.100; λ. αἰτεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 285e; λ. δοῦναι καὶ δέξασθαι [prev. author] “Prt.” 336c, +others; λαμβάνειν λ. καὶ ἐλέγχειν [prev. author] “Men.” 75d; παρασχεῖν τῶν εἰρημένων λ. [prev. author] “R.” 344d; λ. ἀπαιτεῖν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 30.15, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1104a3}}; λ. ὑπέχειν, δοῦναι, {{NT+4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.95; λ. ἐγγράψαι [prev. author] 24.199, +others; λ. ἀποφέρειν τῇ πόλει 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.22, compare “Eu. Luc.” 16.2, NT.Heb.13.17; τὸ παράδοξον τῶν συμβεβηκότων ὑπὸ λόγον ἄγειν 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 15.34.2}}; λ. ἡ ἐπιστήμη, πολλὰ δὲ ὁ λ. the **account** is manifold, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 6.9.4}}; ἔχων λόγον τοῦ διὰ τί an **account** of the cause, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Analytica Posteriora” 74b27}}; ἐς λ. τινός on **account** of, ἐς χρημάτων λ. {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.46, compare 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.89.6, LXX.2Mac.1.14, “JRS” 18.152 (from Jerash)}}; λόγῳ with _genitive_, by **way** of, {{Refs|“Codex Justinianus [4th-6th c.AD]” 3.2.5. +others}}; κατὰ λόγον τοῦ μεγέθους if we take into **account** his size, {{NT+4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 517b27; πρὸς ὃν ἡμῖν ὁ λ. NT.Heb.4.13, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Dio Chrysostomus Sophista 31.123.}}
**I.3** **measure, tale** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|compare(below 11.1), θάλασσα.. μετρέεται ἐς τὸν αὐτὸν λ. ὁκοῖος πρόσθεν 1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 31; ψυχῆς ἐστι λ. ἑαυτὸν αὔξων [prev. author] 115}}; ἐς τούτου (i.e. γήραος) λ. οὐ πολλοί τινες ἀπικνέονται to the **point** of old age, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.99, compare 7.9}}.; ὁ ξύμπας λ. the full **tale**, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.56, cf. NT.Phil.4.15}}; κοινῷ λ. νομίσαντα common **measure**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 746e}}; **sum, total** of expenditure, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“IG” 42(1).103.151 (Epid., 4th c.BC)}}; ὁ τῆς οὐσίας λ., = Latin _patrimonii modus,_ {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th-6th c.AD: Codex Justinianus 1.5.12.20.}}
**I.4** **esteem, consideration, value** put on a person or thing (compare infr. VI. 2 d), οὗ πλείων λ. ἢ τῶν ἄλλων who is of more **worth** than all the rest, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 39}}; βροτῶν λ. οὐκ ἔσχεν οὐδέν᾽ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 233; οὐ σμικροῦ λ. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1163: frequently in 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus, Μαρδονίου λ. οὐδεὶς γίνεται 8.102; τῶν ἦν ἐλάχιστος ἀπολλυμένων λ. 4.135, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 94; περὶ ἐμοῦ οὐδεὶς λ. 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 87}}; λόγου οὐδενὸς γίνεσθαι πρός τινος to be of no **account, repute** with.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.120, compare 4.138}}; λόγου ποιήσασθαί τινα make one of **account**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.33}}; ἐλαχίστου, πλείστου λ. εἶναι, to be highly, lowly **esteemed**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.143, 3.146}}; but also λόγον τινὸς ποιεῖσθαι, like Latin _rationem habere alicujus_, make **account** of, set a **value** on, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 187, etc.: usually in _negative_ statements, οὐδένα λ. ποιήσασθαί τινος 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.4, compare 13, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 21.14.9, etc.; λ. ἔχειν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.62, 115; λ. ἴσχειν περί τινος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 87c; λ. ἔχειν περὶ τοὺς ποιητάς 4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 107; λ. ἔχειν τινός 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.199, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1102b32, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Philopoemen” 18}} (but also, have the **reputation** of.., see below{{Refs 5th c.BC+|VI. 2 e); ἐν οὐδενὶ λ. ποιήσασθαί τι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.50}}; ἐν οὐδενὶ λ. ἀπώλοντο without **regard**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 9.70; ἐν σμικρῷ λ. εἶναι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 550a}}; ὑμεῖς οὔτ᾽ ἐν λ. οὔτ᾽ ἐν ἀριθμῷ Oracle texts cited in {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|Scholia 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 14.48}}; ἐν ἀνδρῶν λ. [εἶναι] to be **reckoned, count** as a man, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.120}}; ἐν ἰδιώτεω λόγῳ καὶ ἀτίμου **reckoned** as.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.AD: Eusebius Myndius Philosophus “fragment” 59; σεμνὸς εἰς ἀρετῆς λ. καὶ δόξης 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.142.}}
**II** **relation, correspondence, proportion**,
**II.1** generally, ὑπερτερίης λ. **relation** (of gold to lead), {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 418 = 1164; πρὸς λόγον τοῦ σήματος 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 519}}; κατὰ λόγον προβαίνοντες τιμῶσι in inverse **ratio**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.134, compare 7.36; κατὰ λ. τῆς ἀποφορῆς [prev. author] 2.109}}; τἄλλα κατὰ λ. in **like fashion**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀρχαίης ἰητρικῇς” 16, “Prog.” 17: with _genitive_, κατὰ λ. τῶν πρόσθεν [prev. work] 24; κατὰ λ. τῶν ἡμερῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 619; κατὰ λ. τῆς δυνάμεως 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.6.11; ἐλάττω ἢ κατὰ λ. 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 508a2, compare “PA” 671a18; ἐκ ταύτης ἐγένετο ἐκείνη κατὰ λ. [prev. author] “Pol.” 1257a31}}; compare εὔλογος: sometimes with ὁ αὐτός added, κατὰ τὸν αὐτὸν λ. τῷ τείχεϊ **in fashion** like to.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.186}}; περὶ τῶν νόσων ὁ αὐτὸς λ. **analogously**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 158d, compare “Prm.” 136b, +others}}; εἰς τὸν αὐτὸν λ. **similarly**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “R.” 353d}}; κατὰ τὸν αὐτὸν λ. in the same **ratio,** {{Refs|Inscriptiones Graecae 12.76.8}}; by parity of **reasoning**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 393c, “R.” 610a, +others}}; ἀνὰ λόγον τινός, τινί, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ti.” 29c, Alcaeus2.145d}}; τοῦτον ἔχει τὸν λ. πρὸς.. ὃν ἡ παιδεία πρὸς τὴν ἀρετήν is **related** to.. as.., {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “in primum Euclidis librum commentarius” p.20 F., +others}}
**II.2** in Mathematics texts, **ratio, proportion** (ὁ κατ᾽ ἀνάλογον λ., λ. τῆς ἀναλογίας,{{Refs 4th c.BC+|Theo Sm. p.73 H.), 4th-5th c.BC: Pythagoras Philosophus 2; ἰσότης λόγων 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 113a31; λ. ἐστὶ δύο μεγεθῶν ἡ κατὰ πηλικότητα ποιὰ σχέσις 3rd c.BC: Euclides Geometra 5 “Def.” 3; τῶν ἁρμονιῶν τοὺς λ. 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 985b32, compare 1092b14}}; λόγοι ἀριθμῶν numerical **ratios**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoxenus Musicus “Harmonica” p.32 M.}}; τοὺς φθόγγους ἀναγκαῖον ἐν ἀριθμοῦ λ. λέγεσθαι πρὸς ἀλλήλους to be expressed in numerical **ratios**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Euclides Geometra}}**Sect.Can. Proëm.**: in Metre, **ratio** between arsis and thesis, by which the rhythm is defined, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoxenus Musicus “Harmonica” p.34 M.; ἐὰν ᾖ ἰσχυροτέρα τοῦ αἰσθητηρίου ἡ κίνησις, λύεται ὁ λ. 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Anima” 424a31}}; ἀνὰ λόγον **analogically**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Archytas Tarentinus Philosophus 2}}; ἀνὰ λ. μερισθεῖσα [ἡ ψυχή] **proportionally**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 37a; so κατὰ λ. 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 319.6}}; πρὸς λόγον in **proportion**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 6.30.3, 9.15.3 (but πρὸς λόγον ἐπὶ στενὸν συνάγεται}} narrows **uniformly**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.9, cf. Diocl.fragment 171); ἐπὶ λόγον “IG” 5(1).1428 (from Messene).}}
**II.3** Grammars, **analogy, rule**, τῷ λ. τῶν μετοχικῶν, τῆς συγκοπῆς, by the **rule** of the participles, of syncopated ope, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th-5th c.AD: Choeroboscus Grammaticus “Scholia in Theodosii Canones” 1.75 Gaisf., 1.377 H.; εἰπέ μοι τὸν λ. τοῦ Αἴας Αἴαντος, τουτέστι τὸν κανόνα “Anecdota Graeca - MSS. Bibl. Oxon.” 4.328.}}
**III** **explanation**,
**III.1** **plea, pretext, ground**, ἐκ τίνος λ.; [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 515; ἐξ οὐδενὸς λ. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 731; ἀπὸ παντὸς λ. [prev. author] “OC” 762; χὠ λ. καλὸς προσῆν [prev. author] “Ph.” 352; σὺν ἀφανεῖ λ. [prev. author] “OT” 657 (Lyric poetry, variant{λόγων}); ἐν ἀφανεῖ λ. 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.59; ἐπὶ τοιούτῳ λ. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.124}}]; κατὰ τίνα λ.; on what **ground**? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 366b}}; οὐδὲ πρὸς ἕνα λ. to no **purpose**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Prt.” 343d}}; ἐπὶ τίνι λ.; for what **reason**? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.2.19}}; τὸν λ. τοῦτον this **ground** of complaint, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.228}}; τίνι δικαίῳ λ.; what just **cause** is there? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 512c}}; τίνι λ.; on what **account?** {{NT|NT.Acts.10.29}}; κατὰ λόγον ἂν ἠνεσχόμην ὑμῶν **reason** would that.., {{Refs|[prev. work]18.14}}; λ. ἔχειν, with personal subject, εἶχον ἄν τινα λ. I (i.e. my conduct) would have admitted of an **explanation**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 31b}}; τὸν ὀρθὸν λ. the true **explanation**, {{Refs|[prev. work] 34b.}}
**III.1.b** **plea, case**, in Law or argument (cf. VIII. I), τὸν ἥττω λ. κρείττω ποιεῖν to make the weaker **case** prevail, {{LXX+5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 18b, +others, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1402a24, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1042 (_plural_); personified, [prev. work] 886, +others; ἀμύνεις τῷ τῆς ἡδονῆς λ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 38a; ἀνοίσεις τοὺς λ. αὐτῶν πρὸς τὸν θεόν LXX.Exo.18.19}}; ἐχειν λ. πρός τινα to have a **case, ground of action** against.., {{NT|NT.Act.19.38.}}
