**IV** inward **debate** of the soul {{Refs 5th c.BC+|compare (λ. ὃν αὐτὴ πρὸς αὑτὴν ἡ ψυχὴ διεξέρχεται 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 189e (διάλογος in “Sph.” 263e)}}; ὁ ἐν τῇ ψυχῇ, ὁ ἔσω λ. (opposed to ὁ ἔξω λ.), {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Analytica Posteriora” 76b25, 27}}; ὁ ἐνδιάθετος, opposed to ὁ προφορικὸς λ., {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“Stoicorum Veterum Fragmenta” 2.43, 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.154)}}
**IV.1** **thinking, reasoning**, τοῦ λ. ἐόντος ξυνοῦ, opposed to ἰδία φρόνησις, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 2}}; κρῖναι δὲ λόγῳ.. ἔλεγχον test by **reflection**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Parmenides Poeta Philosophus 1.36}}; **reflection, deliberation**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|(cf. VI. 3), ἐδίδου λόγον ἑωυτῷ περὶ τῆς ὄψιος 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.209, compare 34, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 583, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 45.7}}; μὴ εἰδέναι.. μήτε λόγῳ μήτε ἔργῳ neither by **reasoning** nor by experience, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Anaxagoras Philosophus 7; ἃ δὴ λόγῳ μὲν καὶ διανοίᾳ ληπτά, ὄψει δ᾽ οὔ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 529d, compare “Prm.” 135e; ὁ λ. ἢ ἡ αἴσθησις 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1149a35, +others}}; αὐτῷ μόνον τῷ λ. πιστεύειν (opposed to αἰσθήσεις), of Parmenides and his school, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|Aristocles Epigrammaticus cited in 4th c.AD: Eusebius Caesariensis Scriptor Ecclesiasticus “Praeparatio Evangelica” 14.17: hence λόγῳ or τῷ λ.}} in **idea**, in **thought**, τῷ λ. τέμνειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 525e}}; τῷ λ. δύο ἐστίν, ἀχώριστα πεφυκότα two in **idea**, though indistinguishable in fact, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1102a30, compare “GC” 320b14, +others}}; λόγῳ θεωρητά **mentally** conceived, opposed to sensibly perceived, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“Placita Philosophorum” 1.3.5, compare 2nd c.BC: Demetrius Lacon Philosophus “Herc.” 1055.20; τοὺς λ. θεωρητοὺς χρόνους 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 1p.19U.}}; διὰ λόγου θ. χ.{{Refs 1st c.BC+|[prev. work] p.10 U.; λόγῳ καταληπτός 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ ποιημάτων” 5.20, etc.}}; ὁ λ. οὕτω αἱρέει **analogy** proves, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.33}}; ὁ λ. or λ. αἱρέει **reasoning** convinces, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 3.45, 6.124, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Crito” 48c}} (but, our **argument** shows, {{Refs|“Lg.” 663d): also with _accusative_ _person_, χρᾶται ὅ τι μιν λ. αἱρέει}} as the **whim** took him, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.132}}; ἢν μὴ ἡμέας λ. αἱρῇ unless we **see fit**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 4.127, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 607b}}; later ὁ αἱρῶν λ. ordaining **reason**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Zeno Citieus Stoicus 1.50, 2nd c.AD: Marcus Antoninus Imperator 2.5, compare 4.24, 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 2.2.20, etc.: coupled or contrasted with other functions, καθ᾽ ὕπνον ἐπειδὴ λόγου καὶ φρονήσεως οὐ μετεῖχε}} since **reason** and understanding are in abeyance, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 71d}}; μετὰ λόγου τε καὶ ἐπιστήμης, opposed to αἰτία αὐτομάτη, of Nature's processes of production, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Sph.” 265c}}; τὸ μὲν δὴ νοήσει μετὰ λόγου περιληπτόν embraced by thought with **reflection**, opposed to μετ᾽ αἰσθήσεως ἀλόγου, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ti.” 28a; τὸ μὲν ἀεὶ μετ᾽ ἀληθοῦς λ., opposed to τὸ δὲ ἄλογον, [prev. work] 51e, compare 70d, +others}}; λ. ἔχων ἑπόμενον τῷ νοεῖν {{Refs|[prev. author] “Phlb.” 62a}}; ἐπιστήμη ἐνοῦσα καὶ ὀρθὸς λ. scientific knowledge and right **process of thought**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Phd.” 73a; πᾶς λ. καὶ πᾶσα ἐπιστήμη τῶν καθόλου 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1059b26; τὸ λόγον ἔχον [prev. author] “EN” 1102b15, 1138b9, +others: in _sing._ and _plural_, contrasted by 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus and 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus}} as **theory, abstract reasoning** with outward experience, sometimes with depreciatory emphasis on the former, εἰς τοὺς λ. καταφυγόντα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 99e}}; τὸν ἐν λόγοις σκοπούμενον τὰ ὄντα, opposed to τὸν ἐν ἔργοις (realities),{{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 100a; τῇ αἰσθήσει μᾶλλον τῶν λ. πιστευτέον 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione Animalium” 760b31}}; γνωριμώτερα κατὰ τὸν λ., opposed to κατὰ τὴν αἴσθησιν, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ph.” 189a4}}; ἐκ τῶν λ. δῆλον, opposed to ἐκ τῆς ἐπαγωγῆς, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Mete.” 378b20}}; ἡ τῶν λ. πίστις, opposed to ἐκ τῶν ἔργων φανερόν, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Pol.” 1326a29; ἡ πίστις οὐ μόνον ἐπὶ τῆς αἰσθήσεως ἀλλὰ καὶ ἐπὶ τοῦ λ. [prev. author] “Ph.” 262a19; μαρτυρεῖ τὰ γιγνόμενα τοῖς λ. [prev. author] “Pol.” 1334a6}}; ὁ μὲν λ. τοῦ καθόλου, ἡ δὲ αἴσθησις τοῦ κατὰ μέρος **explanation**, opposed to perception, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ph.” 189a7}}; ἔσονται τοῖς λ. αἱ πράξεις ἀκόλουθοι **theory**, opposed to practice, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Sententiae” 25}}; in Logic, of **discursive reasoning**, opposed to intuition, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1142a26, 1143b1}}; **reasoning** in general, {{Refs|[prev. work] 1149a26}}; πᾶς λ. καὶ πᾶσα ἀπόδειξις all **reasoning** and demonstration, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|[prev. author] “Metaphorical” 1063b10; λ. καὶ φρόνησιν 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Mus.” p.105 K.; ὁ λ. ἢ λογισμός [prev. passage]; τὸ ἰδεῖν οὐκέτι λ., ἀλλὰ μεῖζον λόγου καὶ πρὸ λόγου}}, of mystical vision, opposed to **reasoning**, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 6.9.10.}} —Phrases, κατὰ λ. τὸν εἰκότα by probable **reasoning**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 30b; οὔκουν τόν γ᾽ εἰκότα λ. ἂν ἔχοι [prev. author] “Lg.” 647d}}; παρὰ λόγον, opposed to κατὰ λ., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica ad Alexandrum” 1429a29, compare “EN” 1167b19}}; compare παράλογος (but παρὰ λ. **unexpectedly**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 940).}}
**IV.2** **reason** as a faculty, ὁ λ. ἀνθρώπους κυβερνᾷ [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus] 256; [θυμοειδὲς] τοῦ λ. κατήκοον 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 70a; [θυμὸς] ὑπὸ τοῦ λ. ἀνακληθείς [prev. author] “R.” 440d; σύμμαχον τῷ λ. τὸν θυμόν [prev. work] b; πειθαρχεῖ τῷ λ. τὸ τοῦ ἐγκρατοῦς 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1102b26}}; ἄλλο τι παρὰ τὸν λ. πεφυκός, ὃ μάχεται τῷ λ.{{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 17; ἐναντίωσις λόγου πρὸς ἐπιθυμίας 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 4.7.13(8); οὐ θυμός, οὐκ ἐπιθυμία, οὐδὲ λ. οὐδέ τις νόησις [prev. author] 6.9.11: frequently in Stoic. Philos. of human “Reason”, opposed to φαντασία, 4th-3rd c.BC: Zeno Citieus Stoicus 1.39}}; opposed to φύσις, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“Stoicorum Veterum Fragmenta” 2.206; οὐ σοφία οὐδὲ λ. ἐστὶν ἐν [τοῖς ζῴοις] [prev. passage]; τοῖς ἀλόγοις ζῴοις ὡς λ. ἔχων λ. μὴ ἔχουσι χρῶ 2nd c.AD: Marcus Antoninus Imperator 6.23; ὁ λ. κοινὸν πρὸς τοὺς θεούς 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 1.3.3; οἷον [εἰκὼν] λ. ὁ ἐν προφορᾷ λόγου τοῦ ἐν ψυχῇ, οὕτω καὶ αὐτὴ λ. νοῦ 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 5.1.3}}; τὸ τὸν λ. σχεῖν τὴν οἰκείαν ἀρετήν (i.e. εὐδαιμονίαν) {{Refs 4th c.BC+|5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “in Platonis Timaeum commentarii” 3.334 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator}}; also of the **reason** which pervades the universe, θεῖος λ. [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus] 257; τὸν θεῖον λ. καθ᾽ Ἡράκλειτον δι᾽ ἀναπνοῆς σπάσαντες νοεροὶ γινόμεθα 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 7.129 (compare below x).}}
**IV.2.b** **creative reason**, ἀδύνατον ἦν λόγον μὴ οὐκ ἐπὶ πάντα ἐλθεῖν {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 3.2.14; ἀρχὴ οὖν λ. καὶ πάντα λ. καὶ τὰ γινόμενα κατ᾽ αὐτόν [prev. author] 3.2.15; οἱ λ. πάντες ψυχαί [prev. author] 3.2.18.}}
**V** **continuous statement, narrative** (whether fact or fiction), **oration**, etc. (compare λέγω (B) {{Refs|11.2)}}
**V.1** **fable**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.141; Αἰσώπου λόγοι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 60d, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1393b8; ὁ τοῦ κυνὸς λ. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.7.13.}}
**V.2** **legend**, ἱρὸς λ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.62, compare 47, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 3.80 (_plural_); συνθέντες λ. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 297; λ. θεῖος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 85d}}; ἱεροὶ λ., of Orphic **rhapsodies**, {{Refs|10th c.AD: Suidas Legal icographus S.V. Ὀρφεύς.}}
**V.3** **tale, story**, ἄλλον ἔπειμι λ. {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Xenophanes Poeta Philosophus 7.1, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.97, etc.; συνθέτους λ. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 686}}; σπουδὴν λόγου urgent **tidings**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 663}}; ἄλλος λ. 'another **story**', {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 34e}}; ὁμολογούμενος ὁ λ. ἐστίν the **story** is consistent, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 3.27}}: _plural_, **histories**, ἐν τοῖσι Ἀσσυρίοισι λ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.184, compare 106, 2.99}}; so in _singular_, **a historical work**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.123, 6.19, 7.152}}: also in _singular_, one **section** of such a work (like later βίβλος), {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.38, 6.39, cf. VI. 3d}}; so in _plural_, ἐν τοῖσι Λιβυκοῖσι λ. {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.161, compare 1.75, 5.22, 7.93, 213}}; ἐν τῷ πρώτῳ τῶν λ. {{Refs|[prev. author] 5.36}}; ὁ πρῶτος λ., of St. Luke's gospel, {{NT+5th c.BC+|NT.Act.1.1: in 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus, opposed to μῦθος}}, as **history** to legend, {{Refs|“Ti.” 26e; ποιεῖν μύθους ἀλλ᾽ οὐ λόγους “Phd.” 61b, compare “Grg.” 523a (but μῦθον λέγειν, opposed to λόγῳ}} (**argument**) διεξελθεῖν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“Prt.” 320c, compare 324d); περὶ λόγων καὶ μύθων 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1336a30; ὁ λ... μῦθός ἐστι 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 4.34.}}
**V.4** **speech**, delivered in court, assembly, etc., χρήσομαι τῇ τοῦ λ. τάξει ταύτῃ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.57, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1358a38; δικανικοὶ λ. [prev. author] “EN” 1181a4; τρία γένη τῶν λ. τῶν ῥητορικῶν, συμβουλευτικόν, δικανικόν, ἐπιδεικτικόν [prev. author] “Rh.” 1358b7; τῷ γράψαντι τὸν λ. 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 17.8, compare λογογράφος 11}}; ἐπιτάφιος λ. funeral **oration**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Menexenus” 236b}}; especially of the **body of a speech**, opposed to ἐπίλογος, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1420b3; opposed to προοίμιον, [prev. work] 1415a12}}; **body of a law**, opposed to proem, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 723b}}; **spoken**, opposed to written **word**, τὸν τοῦ εἰδότος λ. ζῶντα καὶ ἔμψυχον οὗ ὁ γεγραμμένος εἴδωλόν τι {{Refs|[prev. author] “Phdr.” 276a}}; ὁ ἐκ τοῦ βιβλίου ῥηθεὶς [λ.] **speech** read from a roll, {{Refs|[prev. work] 243c}}; published **speech**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 40.54}}; rarely of the **speeches** in Tragedy (ῥήσεις), {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Poetica” 1450b6, 9.}}
**VI** **verbal expression** or **utterance** (compare λέγω (B) {{Refs|111), rarely a single}}**word**, see below b, never in Grammars _signification_ of vocable (ἔπος, λέξις, ὄνομα, ῥῆμα), usually of a **phrase**, cf. I{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus 3 (the only sense found in _Epic dialect_).}}
**VI.a** _plural_, without _Article_, **talk**, τὸν ἔτερπε λόγοις {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.393; αἱμύλιοι λ. 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.56, “hymnus ad Mercurium” 317, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 890, “Op.” 78, 789, 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 704, 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 3.1141}}; ψευδεῖς Λ., personified, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 229; ἀφροδίσιοι λ. 7th-6th c.BC(?): Semonides Iambographus 7.91; ἀγανοῖσι λ. 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.101}}; ὄψον δὲ λ. φθονεροῖσιν **tales**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “N.” 8.21}}; σμικροὶ λ. brief **words**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1268 (assuming variant), “El.” 415}}; δόκησις ἀγνὼς λόγων bred of **talk**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “OT” 681 (Lyric poetry): also in _sing._, λέγ᾽ εἴ σοι τῷ λ. τις ἡδονή}} speak if thou delightest in **talking**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “El.” 891.}}
**VI.b** _singular_, **expression, phrase**, πρὶν εἰπεῖν ἐσθλὸν ἢ κακὸν λ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ant.” 1245, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 514; μυρίας ὡς εἰπεῖν λόγῳ 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.37}}; μακρὸς λ. **rigmarole**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 189, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1091a8}}; λ. ἠρέμα λεχθεὶς διέθηκε τὸ πόρρω a whispered **message**, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 4.9.3}}; ἑνὶ λόγῳ to sum up, in brief **phrase**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 241e, “Phd.” 65d}}; **concisely**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1103b21}} (but also, ={ἁπλῶς, περὶ πάντων ἑνὶ λ}. {{Refs|[prev. author] “GC” 325a1): _plural_, λ. θελκτήριοι}} magic **words**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 478}}; rarely of single words, λ. εὐσύνθετος οἷον τὸ χρονοτριβεῖν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1406a36}}; οὐκ ἀπεκρίθη αὐτῇ λ. answered her not a **word,** {{NT|NT.Matt.15.23.}}
**VI.c** coupled or contrasted with words expressed or understood signifying act, fact, truth, etc., mostly in a depreciatory sense, λ. ἔργου σκιή {{Refs 6th c.BC+|3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 145; ὥσπερ μικρὸν παῖδα λόγοις μ᾽ ἀπατᾷς 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 254}}; λόγῳ, opposed to ἔργῳ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 82, etc.; νηπίοισι οὐ λ. ἀλλὰ ξυμφορὴ διδάσκαλος [prev. author] 76; ἔργῳ κοὐ λόγῳ τεκμαίρομαι 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 338, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 59, “OC” 782; λόγῳ μὲν λέγουσι.. ἔργῳ δὲ οὐκ ἀποδεικνῦσι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.8; οὐ λόγων, φασίν, ἡ ἀγορὴ δεῖται, χαλκῶν δέ 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 7.49; οὔτε λ. οὔτε ἔργῳ 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 9.14}}; λόγοις, opposed to ψήφῳ, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.33}}; opposed to νόῳ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.100; οὐ λόγῳ μαθών 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 5; ἐκ λόγων, κούφου πράγματος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 935a}}; λόγοισι εἰς τὸ πιθανὸν περιπεπεμμένα{{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 886e, compare 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Anach.” 19; ἵνα μὴ λ. οἴησθε εἶναι, ἀλλ᾽ εἰδῆτε τὴν ἀλήθειαν 4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 23, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 30.34}}; opposed to πρᾶγμα, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Topica” 146a4}}; opposed to βία, {{Refs|[prev. author] “EN” 1179b29, compare 1180a5}}; opposed to ὄντα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 100a}}; opposed to γνῶσις, {{NT|NT.2Cor.11.6}}; λόγῳ in **pretence**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.205, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 361b, 376d, “Ti.” 27a, +others}}; λόγου ἕνεκα merely as a matter of **words**, ἄλλως ἕνεκα λ. ἐλέγετο {{Refs|[prev. author] “Cri.” 46d}}; λόγου χάριν, opposed to ὡς ἀληθῶς, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1280b8}}; but also, **let us say, for instance**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|[prev. author] “EN” 1144a33, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 10.46.4, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ σημείων καὶ σημειώσεων” 29, 2nd c.AD: Marcus Antoninus Imperator 4.32}}; λόγου ἕνεκα **let us suppose**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 191c}}; ἕως λόγου, μέχρι λ., = Latin _verbo tenus_, {{NT+5th c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 10.24.7, 1st-2nd c.AD: Epictetus Philosophus “Enchiridion” 16: sometimes without depreciatory force, the antithesis or parallelism being verbal (compare 'word and deed'), λόγῳ τε καὶ σθένει 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 68; ἔν τε ἔργῳ καὶ λ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 382e, compare 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 13.101, NT.Luke.24.19, NT.Act.7.22, 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 2.16.2}}; ὅσα μὲν λόγῳ εἶπον, opposed to τὰ ἔργα τῶν πραχθέντων, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.22.}}
**VI.2** **common talk, report, tradition**, ὡς λ. ἐν θνητοῖσιν ἔην {{Refs 7th c.BC+|Batrachomyomachia 8; λ. ἐκ πατέρων 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 71; οὐκ ἔστ᾽ ἔτυμος λ. οὗτος 7th-6th c.BC: Stesichorus Lyricus 32; διξὸς λέγεται λ. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.32; λ. ὑπ᾽ Αἰγυπτίων λεγόμενος [prev. author] 2.47}}; νέον [λ.] **tidings**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1289 (Lyric poetry)}}; τὰ μὲν αὐτοὶ ὡρῶμεν, τὰ δὲ λόγοισι ἐπυνθανόμεθα by **hearsay**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.148: also in _plural_, ἐν γράμμασιν λόγοι κείμενοι}} **traditions**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 886b.}}
