**IX.3.b** **sentence, complete statement**, ἄνθρωπος μανθάνει λόγον εἶναί φῃς.. ἐλάχιστόν τε καὶ πρῶτον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 262c; λ. αὐτοτελής 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 3.6, 2nd c.BC: Dionysius Thrax Grammaticus 634.1}}; ῥηθῆναι λόγῳ to be expressed in a **sentence**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 202b}}; λ. ἔχειν to be capable of being so expressed,{{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 201e, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1404b26.}}
**IX.3.c** **language**, τὰ τοῦ λ. μέρη parts of **speech**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Chrysippus Stoicus 2.31, 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 9.350, etc.; τὰ μόρια τοῦ λ. 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Compositione Verborum” 6; μέρος λ. 2nd c.BC: Dionysius Thrax Grammaticus 633.26, 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 4.6, +others}} (but ἓν μέρος [τοῦ codex] λόγου one **word**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Synt.” 340.10, compare 334.22)}}; περὶ τῶν στοιχείων τοῦ λ., title of work by Chrysippus.
**X** the **Word** or **Wisdom** of God, personified as his agent in creation and world-government, ὁ παντοδύναμός σου λ. {{LXX+1st c.AD+|LXX.Wis.18.15; ὁ ἐκ νοὸς φωτεινὸς λ. υἱὸς θεοῦ “Corpus Hermeticum” 1.6, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.376c}}; λ. θεοῦ δι᾽ οὗ κατεσκευάσθη [ὁ κόσμος] {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.162; τῆς τοῦ θεοῦ σοφίας· ἡ δέ ἐστιν ὁ θεοῦ λ. [prev. work] 56; λ. θεῖος.. εἰκὼν θεοῦ [prev. work] 561, compare 501; τὸν τομέα τῶν συμπάντων [θεοῦ] λ. [prev. work] 492}}; τὸν ἄγγελον ὅς ἐστι λ.{{Refs|[prev. work] 122: in “NT”}} identified with the person of Christ, ἐν ἀρχῇ ἦν ὁ λ. {{NT|NT.John.1.1, compare 14, NT.1John.2.7, NT.Rev.19.13; ὁ λ. τῆς ζωῆς NT.1John.1.1.}}

3057:
** λόγχ-η**, ἡ,
**A** **spear-head**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.69; λ. δορός 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 856, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 1318 (both Lyric poetry)}}: also in _plural_ of a single spear, **the point with its barbs**, τὸ ξυστὸν τῇσι λόγχῃσι ἐὸν ὁμοίως χρύσεον the shaft alike with **the spear-head**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.52, etc.; λόγχαι δ᾽ ἐκαυλίζοντο καὶ ξυστὴ κάμαξ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 404, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 10.3 (where the shaft is ῥάβδος)}}; οἱ κνώδοντες τῆς λόγχης the barbs **of the spear-head**, {{Refs|[prev. work] 16.}}
**A.2** **lance-shaped birth-mark,** {{Refs|Trag.Adesp. 84.}}
**A.II** **lance, spear, javelin**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Batrachomyomachia 129; χαλκέας λόγχας ἀκμᾷ 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 10.60, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 512 (Lyric poetry), etc.: _metaphorically_, ὀμμάτων ἄπο λόγχας ἵησιν [prev. author] “fragment” 157}}; λόγχας ἐσθίων, proverbial of a bragging coward, a 'fire-eater', {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Timocles Comicus 12.5.}}
**A.III** **troop of spearmen**, ξὺν ἑπτὰ λόγχαις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1312, compare “Ant.” 119 (Lyric poetry); μυρίαν ἄγων λόγχην 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 442; λόγχης ἀριθμῷ πλείονος κρατούμεναι [prev. author] “fragment” 286.12; χωρεῖτε, λόγχη [prev. author] “Cretan dialect” 45.}}

3058:
** λοιδορ-έω**,
_future_ -ήσω {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 40.48}}: _aorist_ ἐλοιδόρησα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 873, etc.}}: _perfect_ λελοιδόρηκα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 241e:}} —_middle_ and _passive_ (see. below), _future_ -ήσομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1400, etc.}}: _aorist_ ἐλοιδορησάμην {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 6.59}}; _Attic dialect_ more frequently ἐλοισορήθην {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 9.54, 54.5 (see. below)}}: (λοίδορος):—**abuse, revile**, τινα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.145; θεούς 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 9.37, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1140, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.4.49, “BGU” 1007.6 (3rd c.BC)}}, etc.: _absolutely_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus}} [same place], etc.; sometimes simply, **rebuke**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.4.9; οἶνον εἰς ἐπίνοιαν λ. 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 90, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.175b (_passive_)}}: with _neuter_ _adjective_, ἐμαυτὸν πόλλ᾽ ἐλοιδόρησα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 1171; οὐδὲν οὐδένα λ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 174c; λ. ἔνια 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1128a31: with a predicate added, [τὴν Τύχην] λ. τυφλήν}} **reproach** fortune as blind, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.98a}}:—_middle_, **rail at one another**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.1.4, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 857, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 54.18:}} —_passive_, λοιδοροῦντας ἢ λοιδορουμένους **reviling** or **reviled**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 2.47, compare 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ παρρησίας” p.29 O.}}; λελοιδορημένος ὑπὸ.. **rebuked**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.4.29; οὐκ ἐν δίκη λοιδορηθείς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 275e, compare “Grg.” 457d.}}
**II** _middle_ with _aorist_ _passive_ in _active_ sense, with _dative_ _person_, **rail at**, τινι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1400, “Pl.” 456, “Ec.” 248, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 395d, etc.; also λ. ἐπί τινι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Agesilaus” 7.3}}; τινος {{LXX+2nd c.AD+|3rd c.AD(?): Achilles Tatius Astronomus 1.6; εἴς τινα 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Anach.” 22; πρός τινα LXX.Exo.17.2: with _accusative_ _cognate_, πάντα τὰ αἰσχρὰ λοιδορέονται, ὅτι}}.. they **use** all kind of foul **reproaches**, saying that.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.184; λοιδορίαν ἣν ἐλοιδορήθη Κρατίνῳ περὶ τούτων 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.132.}}—_active_ never has _dative_ except in later Gr., {{LXX+5th c.BC+|LXX.Exo.17.2, LXX.2Mac.12.14, 1st-2nd c.AD: Epictetus Philosophus “Enchiridion” 34, 2nd c.AD(?): Antoninus Liberalis Mythographus 22.5; in 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.67}} (ἠναντιώθην καὶ ἀντεῖπον —καὶ ἐλοιδόρησα—ἐκείνῳ ὧν ἦν ἄξιος) the _dative_ (if correct) depends on the other verbs; as does the _accusative_ in οὓς ὕβριζες καὶ ἐλοιδοροῦ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “Dem.fragment” (a).}} —Only the _active_ is found in Trag.

3060:
** λοίδορ-ος**, ον,
**railing, abusive**, ἔρις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 534; πομπεῖαι 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Per.fragment” 4; ῥήματα “IG” 14.1857; φωναί 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Ir.” p.74}} W. _adverb_ -ρως {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 14.2.28.}}
**2** as _substantive_ λ., ὁ, **railer**, [{{NT+4th c.BC+|NT.1Cor.5.11, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.177d: τὸ λ., ={λοιδορία}, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physiognomonica” 808b37, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.810d; λοίδορα “Anthologia Graeca” 5.175 (1st c.BC: Meleager Epigrammaticus).}}]

3061:
** λοιμός**, ὁ,
**plague**, once in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus (8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.61), compare 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 243, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.171; λοιμοῦ σκηπτός 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 715 (troch.)}}; of the plague at Athens, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.47, 54, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 201d: _plural_, [prev. work] 188b, +others}}; coupled with λιμός, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus and Hdt.[same places], 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.54, Oracle texts cited in 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.135.}}
**2** of persons, **plague, pest**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 25.80.}}
**II** as _adjective_, **pestilent**, {{LXX|LXX.1Ki.1.16}}; ἀνὴρ λ. καὶ πονηρός{{NT+2nd c.AD+|[prev. work] 30.22, cf. NT.Act.24.5; λ. οἶνος 2nd-3rd c.AD: Symmachus LXX.Pro.20.1.}}

3062:
** λοιπός**, ή, όν,
(λείπω) **remaining over**, not in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, frequently from 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus and 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus downwards; λ. βίοτος 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 1.97; λ.εὐχαί [prev. work] 4.15; λ.γένος [prev. work] 2.15}}; also λοιποί **descendants**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “I.” 4(3).39: in Prose the _Article_ is commonly added, and ὁ λ.}} either agrees with the Noun or takes a dependent genitive, αἱ λ. τῶν νεῶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.72}}; τὴν λοιπὴν (i.e. ὁδὸν) πορευσόμεθα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.4.46; τὸ λ. τῆς ἡμέρας [prev. work] 16, etc.}}
**2** λοιπόν [ἐστι] with _infinitive_, **it remains** to show, etc., ἀποδεικνύναι, διελέσθαι, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Smp.” 4.1, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 466d, etc.: also with _Article_, τὸ λ. ἤδη ἡμῖν ἐστι σκέψασθαι, πότερον}} **what remains** for us is to.., {{Refs|[prev. work] 444e; διανομὴ τοίνυν τὸ λ. σοι [prev. work] 535a: without}}_infinitive_, ὃ δὲ λ. **quod superest**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1571 (Lyric poetry); ὅ τι λ. πόνων [prev. author] “Pr.” 684; τὸ εὐπρεπείας πέρι.. λοιπόν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 274b.}}
**3** frequently of Time, ὁ λ. χρόνος **the future**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 7.67; πρὸς τὸν λοιπὸν τοῦ χρόνου 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 15.16}}; τὸν λ. χρόνον **for the future**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 84; τοῦ λ. χρόνου [prev. author] “El.” 817; εἰς τὸν λ. χρόνον 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Epistulae” 358b; ἐκ τοῦ λ. χρόνου 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 59.46: so without}} _substantive_ in _neuter_, τὸ λ. **henceforward, hereafter**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 5.118, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 1031, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 795, etc.; τὸ λ. εἰς ἅπαντα.. χρόνον 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 763; τὰ λ. [prev. author] “Th.” 66, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1226, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.21; ἐς τὸ λ. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 526, “Eu.” 708, compare “Inscription Prien.” 64 (2nd c.BC)}}; also τοῦ λ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.189, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 1084; ἐκ τοῦ λ. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.4.9; ἐκ τῶν λ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 709e, “Ep.” 316d}}; καθεύδετε τὸ λ. sleep **now..,** {{NT|NT.Matt.26.45, NT.Mark.14.41}}; ἑσπέρα δὲ ἦν λ. καὶ.. it was **now** evening, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 1.24c.}}
**4** τὸ λ. and τὰ λ. **the rest**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 476, 697, 699. etc.}}; καὶ τὰ λ., = '**etc.**',{{Refs 1st c.AD+|Aristeas 190, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.1084c, etc.}}; also λοιπόν without the _Article_, as _adverb_, **for the rest, further**, and so frequently, ={ἤδη}, **already**, λ. δή {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 321c; αἰσχρὸν δὴ τὸ λ. γίγνεται [prev. author] “Grg.” 458d.}}
**5** λοιπόν, _adverb_ **then, well then**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.15.11, +others, 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 2.83, 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 1.24.1; οὐδεμία λ. ἀμφισβήτησίς ἐστιν “BGU” 969.19 (2nd c.AD).}}
**5.b** **finally**, λ., ἀδελφοί, χαίρετε {{NT|NT.2Cor.13.11.}}

