(λείπω) **remaining over**, not in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, frequently from 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus and 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus downwards; λ. βίοτος 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 1.97; λ.εὐχαί [prev. work] 4.15; λ.γένος [prev. work] 2.15}}; also λοιποί **descendants**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “I.” 4(3).39: in Prose the _Article_ is commonly added, and ὁ λ.}} either agrees with the Noun or takes a dependent genitive, αἱ λ. τῶν νεῶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.72}}; τὴν λοιπὴν (i.e. ὁδὸν) πορευσόμεθα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.4.46; τὸ λ. τῆς ἡμέρας [prev. work] 16, etc.}}
**2** λοιπόν [ἐστι] with _infinitive_, **it remains** to show, etc., ἀποδεικνύναι, διελέσθαι, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Smp.” 4.1, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 466d, etc.: also with _Article_, τὸ λ. ἤδη ἡμῖν ἐστι σκέψασθαι, πότερον}} **what remains** for us is to.., {{Refs|[prev. work] 444e; διανομὴ τοίνυν τὸ λ. σοι [prev. work] 535a: without}}_infinitive_, ὃ δὲ λ. **quod superest**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1571 (Lyric poetry); ὅ τι λ. πόνων [prev. author] “Pr.” 684; τὸ εὐπρεπείας πέρι.. λοιπόν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 274b.}}
**3** frequently of Time, ὁ λ. χρόνος **the future**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 7.67; πρὸς τὸν λοιπὸν τοῦ χρόνου 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 15.16}}; τὸν λ. χρόνον **for the future**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 84; τοῦ λ. χρόνου [prev. author] “El.” 817; εἰς τὸν λ. χρόνον 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Epistulae” 358b; ἐκ τοῦ λ. χρόνου 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 59.46: so without}} _substantive_ in _neuter_, τὸ λ. **henceforward, hereafter**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 5.118, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 1031, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 795, etc.; τὸ λ. εἰς ἅπαντα.. χρόνον 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 763; τὰ λ. [prev. author] “Th.” 66, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1226, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.21; ἐς τὸ λ. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 526, “Eu.” 708, compare “Inscription Prien.” 64 (2nd c.BC)}}; also τοῦ λ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.189, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 1084; ἐκ τοῦ λ. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.4.9; ἐκ τῶν λ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 709e, “Ep.” 316d}}; καθεύδετε τὸ λ. sleep **now..,** {{NT|NT.Matt.26.45, NT.Mark.14.41}}; ἑσπέρα δὲ ἦν λ. καὶ.. it was **now** evening, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 1.24c.}}
**4** τὸ λ. and τὰ λ. **the rest**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 476, 697, 699. etc.}}; καὶ τὰ λ., = '**etc.**',{{Refs 1st c.AD+|Aristeas 190, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.1084c, etc.}}; also λοιπόν without the _Article_, as _adverb_, **for the rest, further**, and so frequently, ={ἤδη}, **already**, λ. δή {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 321c; αἰσχρὸν δὴ τὸ λ. γίγνεται [prev. author] “Grg.” 458d.}}
**5** λοιπόν, _adverb_ **then, well then**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.15.11, +others, 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 2.83, 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 1.24.1; οὐδεμία λ. ἀμφισβήτησίς ἐστιν “BGU” 969.19 (2nd c.AD).}}
**5.b** **finally**, λ., ἀδελφοί, χαίρετε {{NT|NT.2Cor.13.11.}}

3067:
** λουτρόν**, τό,
in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} always **λοετρόν**, but _contraction_ form in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|“hymnus ad Cererem” 50, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 753}}; _Doric dialect_ **λωτρόν**, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}: (λούω):— **bath, bathing-place**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, always in _plural_, θερμὰ λοετρά}} hot **bath**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.444, +others; later θερμὰ λουτρά 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 670, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 634 (Lyric poetry), CratesCom. 15, etc.; θερμὰ Νυμφᾶν λουτρά 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 12.19; also called Ἡράκλεια λουτρά 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1051; λοετρὰ Ὠκεανοῖο 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.489, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.275}}; σίτοισι καὶ λουτροῖσι in matters of eating and **washing**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.52; λουτρῷ χρωμένους 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.1109b: _sing._ first in 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus [same place]; τραπέσθαι πρὸς τὸ λ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 115a, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 9.7}}: in _plural_, **bathing-establishment**, τὰ δημόσια λ. {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“POxy.” 1252 B 22 (3rd c.AD), etc.}}
**2** **water for bathing** or **washing**, ὑδάτων ἐνεγκεῖν λουτρά {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1599}}; ἐν λουτροῖς while **bathing**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.5.59}}; λοῦσαί τινα λουτρόν give one a **bath**, wash one with water, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1201, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 469; λουτρὸν παρέχειν [prev. work] 377}}; λοῦσθαι λουτρόν **bathe**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 366 (note); λουτρόν ἐστιν, οὐ πότος 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 9}}; νυμφικὰ λουτρά **the conveying of water to the bride** (compare λουτροφόρος), {{NT+2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 3.43; in “NT”, of baptism, NT.Eph.5.26; λ. παλιγγενεσίας NT.Tit.3.5.}}
**II** in Poets, ={σπονδαί}, **libations to the dead**, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 84, 434, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1667, compare 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus see at {χθόνια λ}.}}]

3068:
** λούω**,
_contraction_ from **λοέω**, from which come _imperfect_ λόεον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.252}}: _aorist_ _infinitive_ λοέσσαι {{Refs|19.320}}; _participle_ λοέσσας {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.282}}: _future_ _middle_ λοέσσομαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.221: _3rd.pers._ _sing._}}_aorist_ λοέσσατο{{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. work] 227; λοεσσάμενος 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.577, “Schwyzer” 633 (Eresus, 2nd-1st c.BC)}}: also _Epic dialect_ _imperfect_ ἐλούεον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“hymnus ad Cererem” 289.—Later forms, λούει 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.52}}; _infinitive_ λούειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ νούσων” 2.20, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 115a}}: _future_ λούσω {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Delum” 95}}; _Doric dialect_ λουσῶ {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 5.146}}: _aorist_ ἔλουσα {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Anacreon Lyricus 47, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 901, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 19}} (_Epic dialect_ λοῦσα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.679, etc.)}}:—_middle_ λούονται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.75}}; _infinitive_ λούεσθαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.508, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 5.70}}; _participle_ λουόμενοι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.23}}: _future_ λούσομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 837, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 116a:}}_aorist_ ἐλουσάμην{{Refs|[prev. passage]}}; _Epic dialect_ λούσαντο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.576}}; _Doric dialect_ _participle_ λωσάμενος {{Refs|“Berl.Sitzb.” 1927.157 (from Cyrene)}}: —_passive_, _aorist_ ἐλούθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “γυναικεῖα” 1.11, later ἐλούσθην 3rd c.BC: Lycophron Tragicus 446:}}_perfect_ λέλουμαι, _3rd.pers._ _singular_ λέλουται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 868}}; _participle_ λελουμένος {{LXX+8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.6, later λέλουσμαι LXX.Cant.5.12 (codex Vat.).}}—Another old form of the _present_ was **λόω**, whence _3rd.pers._ _singular_ λόει {{Refs|“Scol.” 25}}, _2nd pers._ _singular_ _middle_ κατα-λόει {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 838 (probably)}}: _3rd.pers._ _singular_ _imperfect_ λόε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.361}}, _3rd.pers._ _plural_ λόον {{Refs|“hymnus ad Apollinem” 120}}; _3rd.pers._ _singular_ _subjunctive_ _middle_ λόηται {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“IG” 12(5).569.5 (Ceos, 3rd c.BC)}}; _infinitive_ λόεσθαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 749}}:—to **λόω** also belong the following _contraction_ forms, _3rd.pers._ _singular_ _imperfect_ ἀπ-έλου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 118, ἐλοῦμεν [prev. author] “Pl.” 657}}; _present_ _passive_ λοῦται {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC(?): Semonides Iambographus 7.63, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.3.11, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 366 (note)}}; λοῦνται, ἐλοῦτο, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.198, 3.125, ἐλούμην 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 363}}; _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ἐλοῦντο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.5.4, etc. (_Doric dialect_ λῶντο, λώοντο, 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Lavacrum Palladis” 72, 73)}}; _infinitive_ λοῦσθαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.216, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.124, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1044, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 942b}}; _participle_ λούμενος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1623, “Pl.” 658, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.13.3: the forms ἐλούομεν, λούομαι, ἐλουόμην}}, etc., are rejected by {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Phrynichus Atticista 165, but are frequently found in codices, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 1.9, etc.: the _imperative_ form λοῦ (glossed by λοῦσαι, 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus), if correct, is _contraction_ for λόε}}: (Cf. Latin _l[acaron]vo_ [from ***lovo**]): — **wash**, properly **wash the body** (νίζω being used of the hands and feet, πλύνω of clothes), τὸν δ᾽ Ἥβη λοῦσεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.905; δμῳαὶ λοῦσαν καὶ χρῖσαν ἐλαίῳ 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.49, compare 6.210}}; λοῦσ᾽ ἐν ποταμῷ **bathed** me, i.e. **let** me **bathe**, {{Refs|7.296}}; τίς ἄν σφε λούσειε; {{NT+5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 739 (Lyric poetry); λούσαντες τὸν νεκρόν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.86, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 1152, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 901; λ. τινὰ ἀπὸ τῶν πληγῶν NT.Act.16.33}}; also λ ἐκ τρίποδος μεγάλοιο **washed** me [with water] from a great cauldron, [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.361; ὀϊστοὺς λοῦσεν φοινίσσᾳ.. Ἄρης ψακάδι 4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 106: with _accusative_ _cognate_, λουτρὸν λοῦσαί τινα, see at {λουτρόν} I.2.}}]
**b** rarely of things, λ. τὰ δύο μέρη τοῦ βαλανείου {{Refs 5th c.AD+|“PFlor.” 384.30 (5th c.AD).}}
**2** _metaphorically_, **purify**, τινὰ ἐκ τῶν ἁμαρτιῶν variant in {{NT|NT.Rev.1.5.}}
**II** _middle_ and _passive_, **bathe**, λοῦσθαι ποταμοῖο ῥοῇσι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.216: also with _genitive_, λελουμένος Ὠκεανοῖο (of a star just risen)}} **fresh from** Ocean's **bath**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.6}}; λούεσθαι ποταμοῖο **bathe** in the river,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|6.508; so ἀπὸ [κρήνης] λουόμενοι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.23: with _accusative_ _cognate_, τὸ λουτρὸν ἡ Ῥέα λοῦται 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Tactica” 33.4: _absolutely_, λούσαντο 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.48, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.20, etc.}}; λελουμένος **freshbathed, after bathing**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.126, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 1064 (Lyric poetry); ἐν βαλανείῳ λελουμένος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 495e; λούεσθαι ἐν πηλῷ 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 595a31; εἰς λουτρῶνας 2nd c.BC: Ptolemaeus Euergetes II Historicus 3 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus (uncertain): _metaphorically_, τόξα.. αἵματι λουσάμενα 4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 143, compare 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Delum” 95; λελουμένος τῷ φόνῳ 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DMeretr.” 13.3.}}
**II.2** in strict _passive_ sense, λοῦσθαι ὑπὸ τοῦ Διός, i.e. **to be washed** by the rain from heaven, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.124, 125.}}
**II.3** in strict _middle_ sense, with _accusative_, λοέσσασθαι χρόα **wash one's** body, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 522, “Th.” 5}}; λούονται (variant{λοῦνται}) ὕδατι τὸ σῶμα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.75.}}

3074:
** λύκος** [ῠ], ὁ,
**wolf**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.156, 352}}; πολιός **grisly**,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|10.334; κρατερώνυχες 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.218}}; ὀρέστεροι{{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. work] 212; ὠμοφάγοι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.156; κοιλογάστορες 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 1041}}; the small Egyptian wolves mentioned by {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.67}} were perhaps **jackals**: various kinds distinguished by {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Oppianus Apamensis Epicus “Cynegetica” 3.293 ff.: proverbial λύκον ἰδεῖν}} to see **a wolf**, i. e. **to be struck dumb**, as was vulgarly believed of any one at whom a wolf got the first look {{Refs 5th c.BC+|(5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 336d), 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 14.22}}; λύκου πτερά, of things that are not, 'pigeon's milk', {{Refs|10th c.AD: Suidas Legal icographus}}; ὡς λ. χανών, of vain expectation, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Eubulus Comicus 15.11, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 337, 3rd c.BC: Euphro Comicus 1.31; λ. κεχηνώς 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 629}}; πρίν κεν λ. οἶν ὑμεναιοῖ, of an impossibility, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Pax” 1076, 1112, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.263}}; ὡς λύκοι ἄρν᾽ ἀγαπῶσιν, of treacherous or unnatural love, poetical cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 241d}}; λύκου βίον ζῆν, i. e. live by rapine, Prov. cited in {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 16.24.4}}; ἐκ λύκου στόματος, of getting a thing **praeter spem**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Zenobius Paroemiographus 3.48}}; τῶν ὤτων ἔχειν τὸν λύκον 'catch a Tartar', {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Apollodorus Carystius Comicus 18, compare 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 30.20.8}}; λ. ἀετὸν φεύγει, of the inescapable, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th-5th c.AD: Diogenianus Paroemiographus 6.19; λ. περὶ φρέαρ χορεύει, of those engaged in vain pursuits, [prev. work] 21.}}
**II** a kind of **daw**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 617b17}}; compare λύκιος.
**III** **a fish**, ={καλλιώνυμος}, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Hicesius Medicus cited in 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 7.282d, “Geoponica” 18.14.1.}}
**IV** a kind of **spider**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 623a2, 2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “Theriaca” 734, 1st c.AD: Plinius Rerum Naturalium Scriptor “Historia Naturalis” 30.52.}}
**V** **anything shaped like a hook**:
**V.1** **a jagged bit** for hard-mouthed horses, Latin _lupus_, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.641f}}; compare λυκοσπάς.
**V.2** **hook** or **knocker** on a door, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}
**V.3** **flesh-hook**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 10.98.}}
**VI** nickname of παιδερασταί, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Anthologia Graeca” 12.250 (2nd c.AD: Strato Epigrammaticus), compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 241d.}}
**VII** **the flower of the iris**, Philin. cited in {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 15.682a.}}
**VIII** a kind of **noose**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus “de Usu Partium” 7.14, Heraclas cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 48.7, “Hippiatrica” 74.}}
**IX** **a pastille used in dysentery**, {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Aëtius Medicus 9.49 (Latin version).}}
**X** ={ὀροβάγχη}, variant in marginal of {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 2.142.}}
**XI** an engine of war for defending gates, {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Procopius Caesariensis Historicus “de Bello Gothico” 1.21}}. (Cf. Sanskrit _vŕ[null]kas_, Lithuanian _vi[ltilde]kas_, Slavonic _vl[ucaron]k[ucaron]_, Gothic _wulfs._)

3076:
** λῡπ-έω**,
(λύπη) **grieve, vex**, whether in body or mind, τινα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 401, 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in E. Diehl, 2.4, etc.}}; opposed to εὐφραίνειν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 239 (_anapaest meter_)}}; [ὁ θώραξ] λυπεῖ **distresses** by its weight, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.10.15}}: c._neuter_ _adjective_, λυπεῖν μηδὲν αὑτόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 338, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.144, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.3.50; ταὐτὰ ταῦτα λυποῦντες, ἅπερ ἐγὼ ὑμᾶς ἐλύπουν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 41e}}: with _participle_, ἐλύπει αὐτὸν ἡ χώρα πορθουμένη {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.7.12; οὐ σκοπεῖς ὅ τι μὴ λυπήσεις τοὺς ἄλλους ποιῶν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.135: followed by _interrogative_, καί μ᾽ ἦμαρ.. λυπεῖ, τί πράσσει 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 74, compare “El.” 59}}; οὐδὲν ἐλύπησεν [αὐτό], ὥστε μή.. **does** no **harm**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 393e}}; οὐδένα λυπήσας or -ασα, as formula in epitaphs, {{Refs|“IG” 14.1857, 2.1868.}}
**2** _absolutely_, **cause pain** or **grief**, ἄγαν γε λυπεῖς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 589, “Ant.” 573, compare “OT” 1231; τὸ λυποῦν 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 107, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 410.}}
**3** in historical writers, of cavalry and light troops, **harass, annoy** an army by constant attacks, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.40, compare 61, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.66, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.3.14, “An.” 2.3.23, etc.; λῃσταὶ.. τὴν Λακωνικὴν ἧσσον ἐλύπουν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.53, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1427.}}
**II** _passive_ with _future_ _middle_ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|(5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 474, etc.)}}, **to be grieved, distressed**, λυπεῖσθαι φρένα {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 593 codices; τῷ μήτε χαίρειν μήτε λυπεῖσθαι βροτούς 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 266, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 555, etc.}}; μήτε λυπέο μήτε.. **be** not **distressed**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.100; ὑπὸ θεραπαίνης ἐπίτηδες λ. 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 1.11: with _accusative_ _cognate_, τὰς ἐσχάτας λ. λύπας 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 494a, compare “Phd.” 85a; also διπλῇ τινι λύπῃ λ. [prev. author] “Phlb.” 36a}}: also with _accusative_ _of things_, **grieve about** a thing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1086; πρὸς τὰς ξυμφοράς 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.64; διά τι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 52b; ἐπί τινι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.9.8}}: with _participle_, λυπῇ.. ἐστερημένη {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 286; ταῦτ᾽ ἐλυπεῖθ᾽ ὁρῶν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.217}}: _absolutely_, **feel pain**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 632, etc.; τὸ -ούμενον τῆς ψυχῆς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 689b; τὸ -εῖσθαι [prev. author] “Prt.” 354d.}}

3077:
** λῡπ-η**, ἡ,
**pain of body**, opposed to ἡδονή, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Phlb.” 31c, etc.}}; also, **sad plight** or **condition**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.152.}}
**2** **pain of mind, grief**,{{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 16. ; δῆγμα δὲ λύπης οὐδὲν ἐφ᾽ ἧπαρ προσικνεῖται 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 791 (_anapaest meter_)}}; τί γὰρ καλὸν ζῆν βίοτον, ὃς λύπας φέρε; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “fragment” 177, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1217 (Lyric poetry), etc.; ἐρωτικὴ λ. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.59; λύπας προσβάλλειν 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2. 2.2; λ. φέρειν τινί 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 2.8}}; opposed to χαρά, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.1.32.}}

3080:
** λύσις** [ῠ], εως,
_Ionic dialect_ ιος, ἡ, (λύω) **loosing, releasing, ransoming**, νεκροῖο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.655; σώματος 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 4.13; ἡ λ. τῆς αἰσθήσεως ἐγρήγορσις 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Somno et Vigilia” 454b27: with _genitive_ objecti, θανάτου λ.}} **deliverance from** death, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.421, 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 1010; λ. ἔριδος 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 637; χρεέων [prev. author] “Op.” 404; πενίης 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 180; λύσιν αἰτησόμενοι τῶν παρεόντων κακῶν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.139; πενθέων 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 10.76; μόχθων τῶν ἐφεστώτων 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1171; τῶν δειμάτων 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.102; τῶν δεσμῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 515c}}; ἀπὸ τῶν δεσμῶν{{Refs 6th c.BC+|[prev. work] 532b; ἐκ χαλεπῶν 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 1385; βλασφημίας 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator “Epistulae” 3.39; μάχης “PLips.” 40 ii 16 (4th-5th c.AD).}}
**2** _absolutely_, οὐ γὰρ λ. ἄλλη στρατῷ πρὸς οἶκον no other **means of letting** the host **loose from port** for home, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 573.}}
**3** **deliverance from guilt** by expiatory rites, ὅπως λ. τιν᾽ ἡμὶν εὐαγῆ πόρῃς may'st grant us **a deliverance** such as may purify us, {{Refs|[prev. author] “OT” 921}}; οὐδ᾽ ἔχει λύσιν [τὰ πήματα] admit not of **atonement**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ant.” 598 (Lyric poetry); λύσεις τε καὶ καθαρμοὶ ἀδικημάτων 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 364e}}; τῇ [τῆς φιλοσοφίας] λύσει καὶ καθαρμῷ **by** her **offer to release them**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Phd.” 82d}}; αἱ νομιζόμεναι λ., in cases of homicide, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1262a32}}; λ. ἁμαρτημάτων **blotting out** of sins, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.151.}}
**4** **redemption** of mortgage or pledge, [χωρίον] πεπραμένον ἐπὶ λύσει {{Refs|“IG” 2.1103, +others, compare 12(7).55.14 (from Amorgos), 12(8).18 (from Lemnos).}}
**4.b** **release, discharge** from a financial obligation, λύσιν ποιήσασθαι τῆς συγχωρήσεως {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“BGU” 1115.46 (1st c.BC), etc.}}
**5** for ὠμὴ λύσις, see at {ὠμήλυσις}.
**II** **loosing, parting**, λ. καὶ χωρισμὸς ψυχῆς ἀπὸ σώματος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 67d; simply, ἡ τοῦ σώματος λ. [prev. author] “Ax.” 371a}}; **dissolution**, πολιτείας {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Lg.” 945c; νόμων ἢ πολιτείας 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1268b30; βίου λύσιν ἔσχε “IG” 14.140 (from Syracuse)}}; λ. κομήτου {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Philoponus, Joannes Philosophus “in Aristotelis Meteorologicorum librum primum commentarium - Comm. in Arist. Graeca” 86.25}}; τῶν σφραγίδων αἱ λ. **breaking** them, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Alex.” 20.}}
**II.2** **emptying, evacuation**, πείνη μέν που λ. καὶ λύπ; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 31e}}; ἡ λ. τῶν κοιλιῶν, κοιλίας, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 947b29, 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 1.64 (variant)}}; **emission of semen**, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Alexander Aphrodisiensis Philosophus “Problemata - Physici et Medici Graeci Minores” 1.125 (_plural_).}}
**II.3** λ. πυρετοῦ **remission** of fever, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 11.28}}; λ., opposed to κρίσις, {{Refs|[prev. author] 9.732}}; **cure**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Anonymus Londinensis 3.20}}; τὰ πάθη defined as συστολαὶ καὶ λύσεις (variant{χύσεις}) τῆς ψυχῆς, opposed to κρίσεις, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Zeno Citieus Stoicus 3.113 = 1.51.}}
**II.4** as a technical term,
**II.4.a** **solution** of a difficulty, ἡ λ. τῆς ἀπορίας its **solution**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1146b7, +others}}; ἔχει τινὰ λ. πρὸς ταύτην τὴν ἀπορίαν, ὅτι.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “de An.” 422b28; οὐ συμβαίνει ἡ λ. [prev. author] “EN” 1153b5; ὅταν τὸ θάμβος.. μὴ δύνηται τὴν λ. λαμβάνειν 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 1p.29U.; εὑρεῖν λ. τοῦ προβλήματος 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 30.19.5; λ. εὑρέσθαι 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.267 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus}}; also, **interpretation**, σημείων τεράτων τε λύσεις {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Orphica “Argonautica” 37.}}
**II.4.b** **refutation** of an argument, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Sophistici Elenchi” 179a27, “Rh.” 1402b23, +others}}
**II.4.c** **unravelling** of the plot of a tragedy, opposed to δέσις, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Po.” 1454a37 (_plural_), 1455b24.}}
**II.4.d** **softening** of a strong expression, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Longinus Rhetor 38.5.}}
**II.4.e** **resolution of one vowel into two**, as in ἥλιος, ἠέλιος, like{διαίρεσις}, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demetrius Phalereus Rhetor “Demetrius on Style” 70.}}
**II.4.f** **looseness** of structure in writing, especially asyndeton, {{Refs|[prev. work] 192, +others}}
**II.4.g** in metric, **resolution** of [musical notation] into [musical notation], {{Refs 7th c.AD+|7th c.AD: Hephaestio Grammaticus 6.}}
**II.5** **divorce**, {{NT+6th c.AD+|NT.1Cor.7.27; λ. γάμου 6th c.AD: Justinianus Imperator “Novellae” 140 “Prooem.”, compare at Title Page}}
**III** ={δόρπου λ}., **place** for banqueting {{Refs 5th c.BC+|compare (κατάλυσις 11), 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 10(11).47.}}

3081:
** λῡσῐτελ-έω**,
properly **indemnify for expenses incurred**, or **pay what is due**, and then '**pay**', i.e. **profit, avail** (compare λύω see 2), with _dative_,
**I** with subject expressed, οὔ φημ᾽ ἂν λυσιτελεῖν σφῷν [τοῦτο] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 509; λυσιτελεῖ ἡμῖν ἡ δικαιοσύνη 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 327b; δοῦλος τοιοῦτος οἷος μηδενὶ δεσπότῃ λυσιτελεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.1.15.}}
**I.2** mostly _impersonal_, λυσιτελεῖ μοι **it profits** me, **is better** for me, with _participle_, οἷς οὐδ᾽ ἅπαξ ἐλυσιτέλησε πειθομένοις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 25.27; πολλοῖς δὴ ἐλυσιτέλησεν ἀδικήσασι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus Alcaeus1.113d}}: with _infinitive_, λ. προϊέναι {{Refs|[prev. author] “Tht.” 181b}}; τεθνάναι νομίσασα λυσιτελεῖν ἢ ζῆν thinking it **better** to be dead than alive, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.125, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 407a, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.4.12 (variant), “PHamb.” 27.17 (3rd c.BC)}}, etc.: with _dative_ _person_, **it profits** one to do so and so, οὐ γάρ οἱ λυσιτελέειν.. δικάζειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.97; ὅτι μοι λυσιτελοῖ ὥσπερ ἔχω ἔχειν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 22e, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Hiero” 7.13}}: sometimes with _accusative_ _person_, **it is good** that.., λυσιτελεῖ τὸν μέλλοντα κακῶς ἰητρεύεσθαι ἀμφότερα καταγῆναι τὰ σκέλεα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀγμῶν” 19, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 406d: _absolutely_, ἐλυσιτέλει γάρ 4th c.BC: Axionicus Comicus 6.6.}}
**I.3** in bad sense, **conspire**, as gloss on{ἐς τὸ κακὸν ἀλλήλοισι συντιμωρεῖ} [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|(5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ διαίτης ὀξέων” 17), 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 15.494 (variant{συντελεῖ}).}}]
**II** _neuter_ _participle_ as _substantive_, τὸ λυσιτελοῦν **profit, gain, advantage**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.85, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 336d, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 2.28}}; a wrong _etymology_ is given in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 417c.}}

3083:
** λύτρ-ον**, τό,
(λύω) **price of release**:
**1** **ransom**, mostly in _plural_ (later _singular_, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 20.84, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.295c, etc.), τῶν λ. τὴν δεκάτην}} the tithe **of the ransom-money**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.77}}; Ἕκτορος λύτρα, title of {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24}} and of play by Aeschylus; λύτρα λαβεῖν τινος receive **as ransom** for.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.5; τῆς θυγατρὸς λύτρα φέρων 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 393d}}; λύτρα ἀποδιδόναι, καταθεῖναι, pay **ransom**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 53.11, 13}}; εἰσενεγκεῖν εἰς λύτρα contribute towards **it**, {{Refs|[prev. work]7}}; ἀφιέναι ἄνευ λύτρων release without **ransom**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.2.16, compare 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.100, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.169, etc.}}; δώσουσιν ἕκαστος λύτρα τῆς ψυχῆς αὐτοῦ Κυρίῳ a **ransom** of his soul, {{LXX+NT|LXX.Exo.30.12; _sing._ in “NT”, λ. ἀντὶ πολλῶν NT.Matt.20.28, NT.Mark.10.45; λ. ὑπὲρ γαμέτου “IG” 14.607f (from Carales)}}; _plural_, **sum paid for manumission** of a slave, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“POxy.” 48.6 (1st c.AD), etc.}}
**1.b** **sum paid for redemption of a pledge**, in _plural_, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PBad.” 3.4 (2nd c.BC), etc.}}
**2** **atonement**, τί γὰρ λ. πεσόντος αἵματο; (so Canter for λυγρόν) {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 48}}; of blood-money, {{LXX|LXX.Exo.21.30, +others}}
**3** generally, **recompense**, λύτρον καμάτων for toil, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 8(7).1; συμφορᾶς [prev. author] “O.” 7.77.}}
**II** a plant, ={λυσιμάχειος}, Ps.-{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 4.3.}}

3084:
** λυτρ-όω**,
**release on receipt of a ransom, hold to ransom**, τινὰ χρημάτων ὅσων δοκεῖ **for** such a sum as is agreed on, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 165e}}; **redeem** a pledge, λ. τὰ ἱμάτια {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“POxy.” 530.14 (2nd c.AD)}}, etc.:—_middle_, **release by payment of ransom, redeem**, παρὰ τῶν Αἰτωλῶν {{NT+3rd c.BC+|“IG” 12(5).36 (Naxos, 3rd c.BC),cf. LXX.Exo.13.15, +others; τὴν χώραν χρημάτων οὐκ ὀλίγων 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 18.16.1; ἡμᾶς ἀπὸ πάσης ἀνομίας NT.Tit.2.14}}:—_passive_, **to be ransomed**, ἐκ τῶν ἰδίων λελυτρῶσθαι {{NT+4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.170, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1164b34; αἵματι NT.1Pet.1.18.}}
**II** _passive_, **to be released** from an obligation, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PEleph.” 19.8 (3rd c.BC).}}
**III** mistranslation of Hebrew '**rp** 'break the neck', {{LXX|LXX.Exo.13.13}}. -ών, ῶνος, ὁ, ={ἀπόπατος}{{LXX|2, LXX.2Ki.10.27, “Anecdota Graeca - Bekker” 433.}}

3085:
** λύτρ-ωσις**, εως, ἡ,
**ransoming**, αἰχμαλώτων {{NT+1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Aratus” 11, cf. LXX.Lev.25.29, NT.Luke.1.68, NT.Heb.9.12}}; **redemption** of a pledge, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“PTeb.” 120.41 (1st c.AD).}}
**2** **release, discharge** from an obligation, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|“POxy.” 1130.20 (5th c.AD).}}
**II** λ. ὕδατος, = **spring** of water, {{LXX|LXX.Josh.1.15.}}

3088:
** λύχνος**, ὁ,
_plural_ λύχνοι {{Refs 7th c.BC+|Batrachomyomachia 180, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1315, 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 70, 152, “PPetr.” 2p.72 (3rd c.BC): frequently also λύχνα, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.62, 133, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 514 (Lyric poetry), 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hecale” 1.4.11, etc., probably in 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 41.1}}. (fragment λυκ-**sno**-, compare Λύκη):—**portable light, lamp**, χρύσεος λ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.34}}; λύχνα καίειν, ἀνάπτειν, light **lamps**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus [same places]; ἅπτε, παῖ, λ. 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 18}}; λύχνους ἅπτειν **to have an illumination**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 2.17.37; λύχνους ἀποσβέσαι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 668}}; λ. ἀπεσβήκει had been put out, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 218b}}; περὶ λύχνων ἁφάς about **lamp-lighting time**, i. e. towards nightfall, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.215, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 19.31}}; πάννυχος λ. παρακαίεται **lamps** are kept burning all night, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.130; καύσεις λύχνων “Sammelb.” 1161.14 (1st c.BC)}}; ἔλαιον ἡμῖν οὐκ ἔνεστ᾽ ἐν τῷ λύχνῳ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 56}}; compare κεράτινος.
**2** in _plural_, οἱ λύχνοι or τὰ λύχνα **the lamp-market**, οὑκ τῶν λ. {{Refs|[prev. work] 1065.}}
**II** a fish, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 17.2.4, 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}; cf. Latin _lucerna_, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Plinius Rerum Naturalium Scriptor “Historia Naturalis” 9.82.}}

3089:
** λύω**,
_poetry_ _imperative_ λῦθι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 85:}}_future_ λύσω [ῡ] {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.29, etc.}}: _aorist_ ἔλῡσα {{Refs|18.244, etc.}}: _perfect_ λέλῠκα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.18, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 992 (ἀπο-)}}, etc.:— _passive_, _perfect_ λέλῠμαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.103}}, etc.: _pluperfect_ ἐλελύμην [ῠ] {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.186}}, etc.: _aorist_ ἐλύθην, _Epic dialect_ λύθην [ῠ] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|8.360, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 860, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.103, etc.}}: _future_ λῠθήσομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 41b, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.116, etc., also λελύσομαι [ῡ] 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 14.2, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.2.37 (ἀπο-): _Epic dialect_}}_aorist_ _passive_ λύμην [ῠ] {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.80}}; λύτο [ῠ[{{Refs|] [prev. work] 114, but λῦτο 24.1 (at beginning of line, variant{λύτο})}}]; λύντο {{Refs|7.16}}: also _3rd.pers._ _singular_ _optative_ _perfect_ λελῦτο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.238}}:—_middle_, _future_ λύσομαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.13, etc.}}: _aorist_ ἐλυσάμην {{Refs|14.214:}}_perfect_ _passive_ λέλῦμαι in _middle_ sense, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 36.45, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1400a22 (compare δια-, κατα-λύω):}}_future_ λύσομαι in _passive_ sense, (δια-) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.12, (ἐπι-) 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 25.33 codices (καταλύσεσθαι editions), (κατα-) 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.9.}}—Homer uses all tenses except _perfect_ _active_, _present_ and _future_ _passive_ [In _present_ and _imperfect_ ῡ always in _Attic dialect_, ῠ mostly in _Epic dialect_, though {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} has ῡ twice, ἔλῡεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.513, λῡει 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.74}}; also in compounds, ἀλλῡεσκεν {{Refs|2.105, ἀλλῡουσαν [prev. work] 109: in}}_future_ and _aorist 1_ ῡ always: in other tenses ῠ always, except in the forms λελῦτο, λῦτο (see. above).] (Cf. Latin _luo_ (pay), **re-luo, solvo** (for **se-luo), solūtus**, etc.):—**loosen**:
**I** of things, **unbind, unfasten**, especially clothes and armour, λῦσε δέ οἱ ζωστῇρα, θώρηκα, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.215, 16.804}}; λ. παρθενίην ζώνην **loose** the maiden-girdle, of the husband after marriage, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.245}}; of the wife, λύοι χαλινὸν ὑφ᾽ ἥρωϊ παρθενίας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 8(7).48; ἔνθα παρθένει᾽ ἔλυσ᾽ ἐγὼ κορεύματα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 177; so ἔλυσας.. ἅγνευμα σόν [prev. author] “Tr.” 501}}; frequently of the tackling of ships, λ. πρυμνήσια, ἱστία, λαῖφος, etc., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.418, 15.496, 552, “hymnus ad Apollinem” 406, etc. (never in 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad”)}}; λ. πρύμνας, νεῶν πόδα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 539, 1020, etc.: _absolutely_, λύειν}}, of ships, **set sail**, λῦε, κυβερνήτα {{Refs|“APl.” 1.6 *.9 (from Panteleus)}}; ἀσκὸν λ. **untie** a skin (used as a bag), {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.47: frequently in Trag., λ. στολάς, πέπλον, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1597, “Tr.” 924}}; λ. ἡνίαν **slacken** the rein, {{Refs|[prev. author] “El.” 743}}; κλῄθρων λυθέντων when the gates have been **opened**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 396}}; λ. γράμματα, δέλτον, **open** a letter, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 38 (_anapaest meter_), 307}}; λ. πέδας, δεσμά, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 645 (_passive_), 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 1123; ἀρβύλας 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 945}}; ἀρτάνας.. δέρης ἔλυσαν **loosed** it from my neck,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 876, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 781:}} —_middle_, ἀπὸ στήθεσφιν ἐλύσατο κεστὸν ἱμάντα **undid her** belt, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.214}}; but λύοντο τεύχεα they **undid** the armour **for themselves**, i.e. stripped it off (others), {{Refs|17.318}}; later λυσαμένα πλοκαμῖδας **unbinding her** hair, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Bion Bucolicus 1.20, etc.}}
**I.b** in various phrases, στόμα λ. **open** the mouth, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 1060, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.96; γλώσσας λ. εἰς αἰσχροὺς μύθους 5th c.BC: Critias Philosophus, Tragicus, Elegiacus 6.9 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator}}; λ. βλεφάρων ἕδραν wake up, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Rhesus” 8 (_anapaest meter_)}}; λ. ὀφρύν **unfold** the brow, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Hipp.” 290; λ. ἄχος ἀπ᾽ ὀμμάτων 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 706 (Lyric poetry), etc.}}
**I.2** of living beings,
**I.2.a** of horses, etc., **unyoke, unharness**, opposed to ζεύγνυμι, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.35}}; ἐξ ὀχέων, ὑπὲξ ὀχέων, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.369, 8.504; ὑφ᾽ ἅρμασιν 18.244; ὑπὸ ζυγοῦ 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.39: ὑπὸ ζυγόφιν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.576; ὑπ᾽ ἀπήνης 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.6 (also in _middle_, μὴ.. ὑπ᾽ ὄχεσφι λυώμεθα μώνυχας ἵππους}} **unyoke** our horses, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.7; βόε λῦσαι 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 608)}}; λύε μώνυχας ἵππους **loosed** them, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.498}}; λ. κύνα **let** him **loose**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 6.13, etc.}}
**I.2.b** of men, **release, deliver**, especially from bonds or prison, and so, generally, from difficulty or danger, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.22, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.345, 12.53, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 24.206, etc.}}; ὁ λύσων **he that shall deliver**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 771, 785: with _genitive_ _of things_, τὸν.. θεοὶ κακότητος ἔλυσαν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.397, compare 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 3.50, etc.; λ. τινὰ δεσμῶν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 1006; ὄκνου 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 181; τὼ.. ἐκ δεσμοῖο λύθεν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.360, compare 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 4.23, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 873, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 1244, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 360c}}; also λ. δόμους ἁβρότατος **rob** the house of.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 11.34}}; λ. τινὰ τῆς ἀρχῆς **depose** him from.., {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 13.92}}:—_middle_, properly **get** one **loosed** or **set free**, λύσασθαί τινα δυσφροσυνάων {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 528; ὅσπερ Ἰὼ πημονᾶς ἐλύσατο 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 1065 (Lyric poetry)}}:—_passive_, λυθῆναι τὰς πέδας {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 17.116}}; λέλυται γὰρ λαὸς ἐλεύθερα βάζειν, ὡς ἐλύθη ζυγὸν ἀλκᾶς **has been let loose** to speak, since the yoke **was loosed**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 592 (Lyric poetry).}}
**I.2.c** of prisoners, **release on receipt of ransom, admit to ransom, release**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.29, 24.137, 555, etc.; λ. τινά τινι 1.20, 24.561, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.298}}; Σαρπηδόνος ἔντεα καλὰ λύσειαν **would give** them **up**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.163; in full, λ. τινὰ ἀποίνων 11.106; χρημάτων μεγάλων 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.135 (_passive_)}}; ἀνὴρ ἀντ᾽ ἀνδρὸς λυθείς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.3}}:—_middle_, **release by payment of ransom, get** a person **released, redeem**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.13, 24.118, +others, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.284, 385, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Menexenus” 243c, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.229; λύσασθαί τινας ἐκ πολεμίων 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 12.20; ἵππον 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.8.6; ὅσους αὐτὸς ἐλυσάμην τῶν αἰχμαλώτων 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.169; λ. τινὶ τὸ χωρίον [prev. author] 50.28}}; ἑαυτοὺς λ. **pay their** own **ransom**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 19.169}}; **buy** from a pimp, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1353.}}
**I.2.d** λελύσθαι τῶν νόμων, = Latin _legibus solvi_, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 53.18.}}
**I.3** **give up**, [θρόνον] λῦσον ἄμμιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.155.}}
**II** **resolve a whole into its parts, dissolve, break up**, λ. ἀγορήν **dissolve** the assembly, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.305; ἀγορὰς ἠμὲν λύει ἠδὲ καθίζει 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.69, etc.}}:—_passive_, λῦτο δ᾽ ἀγών {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.1; μὴ λυθείη ἡ στρατιά 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.1.2}}; πρὶν [ἂν].. ἡ ἀγορὰ (**market**) λυθῇ {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|[prev. author] “Oec.” 12.1; λυθείσης τῆς συνουσίας 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.15.3.}}
**II.2** of concrete objects, σπάρτα λέλυνται, i. e. **have rotted**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.135; ῥαφαὶ δ᾽ ἐλέλυντο ἱμάντων 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.186}}; λ. τὴν σχεδίην **break** it **up**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.97}}; [τὴν γέφυραν] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.4.17; τὴν ἀπόφραξιν [prev. work] 4.2.25.}}
**II.3** especially of physical strength, **loosen**, i. e. **weaken, relax**, λῦσε δὲ γυῖα **made** his limbs **slack** or **loose**, i. e. killed him, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.469, +others; ὅς τοι γούνατ᾽ ἔλυσα 22.335; πολλῶν τε καὶ ἐσθλῶν γούνατ᾽ ἔλυσεν 5.176, etc.; ἀλλά οἱ αὖθι λῦσε μένος 16.332; πέλεκυς λῦσεν.. βοὸς μένος 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.450, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.29}}; but οἵ μοι καμάτῳ.. γούνατ᾽ ἔλυσαν **made** my knees **weak** with toil, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.118:}} —_passive_, λύντο δὲ γυῖα, etc., as the effect of death, sleep, weariness, fear, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.16, etc.; καμάτῳ φίλα γυῖα λέλυντο 13.85, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.233; αὐτοῦ λύτο γούνατα καὶ φίλον ἦτορ 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.114, 425; λύθη ψυχή τε μένος τε 5.296, etc.; λύθεν δέ οἱ ἅψεα πάντα 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.794, 18.189; λέλυται γυίων ῥώμη 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 913 (_anapaest meter_); λύεται δέ μου μέλη 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 438; λέλυμαι μελέων σύνδεσμα [prev. author] “Hipp.” 199 (_anapaest meter_).}}
**II.3.b** λύει βλέφαρα **closes** her eyes **in sleep**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1302.}}
**II.3.c** _metaphorically_, λ. τὴν ἐν ταῖς ψυχαῖς πρὸς μάχην παρασκευήν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.5.22.}}
**II.4** **undo, bring to naught, destroy**, πολίων κάρηνα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.25; Τροίης κρήδεμνα 16.100, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.388, compare 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus “fragment” 16.7}}: generally, **put an end to**, νείκεα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.205; μελεδήματα 23.62; ἔριν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 81, “Anthologia Graeca” 9.316.12 (1st c.AD: Leonidas Alexandrinus Epigrammaticus); πόλεμον 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.31; ἐπιμομφάν 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 10(11).9; μέμψιν 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 271; φόβον 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 270; φόβον καὶ τὴν ὑποψίαν Polystr. p.7 W.,compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Sententiae” 12; μοχθήματα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1616; ἀνάγκας 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 39}}; βίον, i.e. die, {{Refs|[prev. author] “IT” 692}}; αἰῶν᾽ ἔλυσε, i.e. died, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 1.43; λ. τὸ τέλος βίον 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1720 (Lyric poetry)}}; μαχας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 991 (_anapaest meter_); νοσήματα Diocl.fragment 35 (_passive_),compare 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 6.476; κόπους 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus “Eup.” 1.220}}; **forgive**, ἁμαρτήματα {{LXX|LXX.Job.42.9.}}
**II.4.b** in Prose, λ. νόμους **repeal** or **annul** laws, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.82, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 3.10, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1269a15}}; οὐθὲν τῶν περὶ τὴν πολιτείαν{{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 1298b31; λ. ψήφῳ τὸ παράνομον 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.197 (_passive_), etc.}}; ἐπεὶ ἐκεῖνοι ἔλυσαν τὰς σπονδὰς λελύσθαι μοι δοκεῖ ἡ ἐκείνων ὕβρις καὶ ἡ ἡμετέρα ὑποψία {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.1.21}}; **rescind** a vote, ψῆφον λύει ὁ νόμος {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 24.2}}; **revoke** a will, διαθήκην {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 6.33, etc.}} (but in _passive_, **to be opened**, of a will, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“POxy.” 715.19 (2nd c.AD), etc.)}}; **unbind** a spell, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “de Mysteriis” 3.27:}} —_passive_, λέλυται πάντα all **ties are broken**, all **is in confusion**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 25.25.}}
**II.4.c** as a technical term, **solve** a difficulty, a problem, a question, λύεται ἡ ἀπορία {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 324e, +others; λ. ζήτημα 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 6.436.}}
**II.4.d** **refute** an argument, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 509a, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1402b24, +others; compare λύσις 11.4b, λυτικός 11.}}
**II.4.e** **unravel** the plot of a tragedy, opposed to πλέκειν, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Po.” 1456a10.}}
**II.4.f** λ. τὴν φάσιν, of the Moon, **pass out of**, {{Refs|Vett. Val. 134.1, compare 2.}}
**II.5** **break** a legal agreement or obligation, τὸν νόμον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.106; τὰς σπονδάς 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.23, 78, compare 4.23, +others; τὰ συγκείμενα 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 6.41}}; σίς κε τὰς ϝρήτας τάσδε λύση whoso **breaks** this agreement, {{Refs|“Inscription Cyprian dialect” 135.29 H.}}
**II.6** in physical sense, **dissolve**, λύθεν, opposed to πάγεν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 15.4}}; τὸ θερμὸν λύει, opposed to πήγνυσι, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 384b11, compare 382b33 (_passive_)}}; ἀμμωνιακὸν ὄξει λύσας {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 11.106}}; **melt**, παγείσας χιόνας {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 8.4.2; τι πυρὶ λ. “Hippiatrica” 52.}}
**II.7** of medicines, λ. τὴν κοιλίαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 863b29, compare 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Acut.(Sp.)” 38, Diocl.fragment 140}}; so of the effects of terror, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 877a32 (_passive_).}}
**II.8** **resolve** [musical notation] into [musical notation], in _passive_, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|7th c.AD: Hephaestio Grammaticus 8, 10, 2nd c.AD: Aristides Rhetor 1st c.AD: Quintilianus Rhetor Latinus 1.28.}}
**III** **solve, fulfil, accomplish**, τὰ τοῦ θεοῦ μαντεῖα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 407; ὅρκον 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 6.58.4.}}
**IV** **atone for, make up for**, τὰς πρότερον ἁμαρτίας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ra.” 691; λύσων ὅσ᾽ ἐξήμαρτον 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1224; λ. φόνον φόνῳ [prev. author] “OT” 101, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 511; αἱ πρόσοδοι λύουσι τἀναλώματα 4th-3rd c.BC: Diphilus Comicus 32.5}}:—_middle_, τῶν πάλαι πεπραγμένων λύσασθ᾽ αἷμα.. δίκαις {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 804 (Lyric poetry).}}
**V** μισθὸν λύειν **pay** wages **in full, quit oneself of** them, used only in cases of obligation, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Agesilaus” 2.31.}}
**V.2** τέλη λύειν, ={λυσιτελεῖν}, **pay, profit. avail**, ἔνθα μὴ τέλη λύει φρονοῦντι where **it boots** not to be wise, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 316: but more frequently λύει without τέλη}}, construed like{λυσιτελεῖ}, _absolutely_, λύει δ᾽ ἄλγος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 1362, compare “PSI” 4.400.16: with _dative_ _person_, φημὶ τοιούτους γάμους λύειν βροτοῖσιν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 628, compare “Hipp.” 441: with}}_infinitive_, πῶς οὖν λύει.. ἐπιβάλλει; {{Refs|[prev. author] “Med.” 1112 (_anapaest meter_)}}; ἐμοί τελύειτοῖσιμέλλουσιν τέκνοις τὰ ζῶντ᾽ ὀνῆσαι **it is good for** me to benefit my living children by means of those to come,{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|[prev. work] 566; λύει ἀπελθεῖν “UPZ” 77i12 (2nd c.BC): with _accusative_ et}}_infinitive_, λύει γὰρ ἡμᾶς οὐδέν, οὐδ᾽ ἐπωφελεῖ,.. θανεῖν **it is** not **expedient that** we should die (οὐδ᾽ ἐπωφελεῖ being parenthetic), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1005; οὐ γάρ με λύει.. κακορροθεῖσθαι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Sthen.Prol.” 35}}; compare λυσιτελέω.

3095:
** μᾰγεία**, ἡ,
**theology of the Magians**, μ. ἡ Ζωροάστρου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus Alcaeus1.122a.}}
**II** **magic**, {{NT+4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 9.15.7 (_plural_), NT.Act.8.11 (_plural_), “PMag.Berol.” 1.127, etc.}}; τὴν γοητικὴν μ. οὐδ᾽ ἔγνωσαν [οἱ μάγοι] {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Fragmenta” 36.}}

3096:
** μᾰγ-εύω**,
**to be a Magus** or **skilled in Magian lore**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Artoxerxes” 3, 6, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 1.2.}}
**II** **use magic arts**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 1338; καταγαγεῖν τὸν Δία μαγεύσαντας 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Numa” 15.}}
**III** _transitive_, **bewitch**, e.g. by philtres, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD(?): Achilles Tatius Astronomus 5.22}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Clearchus Historicus 25, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Asin.” 54; πέπλον μεμαγευμένον φαρμάκοις 2nd c.AD: Apollodorus Damascenus Mechanicus 1.9.28.}}
**III.2** **call forth by magic arts**, ἔμψυχα {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“Anthologia Graeca” 12.57 (1st c.BC: Meleager Epigrammaticus), compare 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Asin.” 11.}}

3097:
** Μάγος** [ᾰ], ου, ὁ,
“Magian”, one of a Median tribe, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.101, 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 15.3.1}}: hence, as belonging to this tribe,
**2** **one of the priests and wise men in Persia** who interpreted dreams, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.37, +others, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Fragmenta” 36, 3rd c.BC: Phoenix Iambographus 1.5, NT.Matt.2.1.}}
**3** **enchanter, wizard**, especially in bad sense, **impostor, charlatan**, {{NT+5th c.BC+|1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 14, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 387, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1498 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 572e, NT.Act.13.6, Vett. Val. 74.17: also _feminine_, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Asin.” 4, “Anthologia Graeca” 5.15 (Marc. argument).}}
**II** μάγος, ον, as _adjective_, **magical**, μάγῳ τέχνῃ πράττειν τι {{Refs 1st c.BC+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 1.2; κεστοῦ φωνεῦσα μαγώτερα “Anthologia Graeca” 5.120 (1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus)}}. (Opers. **maguš** 'Magian'.)
